self.CONTENT = {"meta": {"built": "2026-10-18T20:18:38", "sources": {"/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md": "1ee605f0d8fa", "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md": "9bc8f6b84cbc", "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md": "f534f13bb734", "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/drafts/ANT-TRAUMA_trauma_in_pregnancy_DRAFT_2026-11-13.md": "draft-2026-11-13", "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/drafts/ANT-STI_STIs_on_labor_and_delivery_DRAFT_2026-11-13.md": "draft-2026-11-13"}}, "items": [{"id": "FORMULARY", "kind": "card", "title": "Quick reference formulary (OB)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Confirm vs house: doses are ACOG-card extracts. Cross-check the CSM pharmacy build and antibiogram before you hang the bag. <strong>If the house cart later disagrees, the cart wins.</strong></p>\n<h5>Postpartum hemorrhage</h5>\n<ul><li>Oxytocin treatment: 10–40 U in 500–1000 mL infusion or 10 U IM. Do not IV-push.</li><li>Methylergonovine: 0.2 mg IM q2–4h. No IV. Stop if HTN / PEC / CVD.</li><li>Carboprost: 0.25 mg IM or intramyometrial q15–90 min, max 8. Stop if asthma.</li><li>Misoprostol: 600–1000 mcg PO / SL / PR ×1.</li><li>TXA: 1 g IV when first-line meds fail. Best &lt;3h from birth. Not routine prophylaxis.</li><li>Device (house): <strong>JADA first.</strong> Bakri if JADA is not available or not appropriate (see PP-01).</li><li>MTP: ~1:1:1 RBC:FFP:plt. Cryo early if fibrinogen is low. Do not drown in crystalloid.</li></ul>\n<h5>Severe-range BP and eclampsia</h5>\n<ul><li>Treat SBP ≥160 or DBP ≥110 confirmed on a recheck within 15 min (persistent 15 min or more = hypertensive emergency). Give the first-line agent within 30 to 60 min <strong>of confirmation</strong>, as soon as possible. Severe-range gHTN is treated as severe PEC.</li><li><strong>First-line: any of three, equally appropriate (CO 767).</strong> Climb one ladder; stop as soon as BP is below threshold.</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Labetalol IV</strong> (Box 3): 20 mg over 2 min → recheck 10 min → 40 mg → 10 min → 80 mg → 10 min → switch to hydralazine 10 mg IV → 20 min → still severe: emergency consult. Max cumulative 300 mg (PB 222); CMQCC 220 mg; use pharmacy cap. Avoid asthma, heart disease or CHF, heart block, bradycardia.</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Hydralazine IV</strong> (Box 2): 5 or 10 mg over 2 min → recheck 20 min → 10 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV → 10 min → labetalol 40 mg IV and emergency consult. Max 20 mg (PB 222).</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Nifedipine IR PO</strong> (Box 1): 10 mg → recheck 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV and emergency consult. Swallow whole; never SL or punctured. Max 180 mg/day (PB 222).</li><li><strong>Local default (not a preference ranking):</strong> start IV labetalol when IV access is present and there is no asthma, heart block, or bradycardia; oral IR nifedipine when there is no IV access; IV hydralazine when labetalol is contraindicated. If IR nifedipine is unavailable and there is no IV: labetalol 200 mg PO, may repeat in 30 min (CO 767).</li><li>Primary management with oral medications other than Nifedipine may be a reasonable adjunctive approach with concomitant IV therapy, but use alone is not supported by ACOG or local policy.</li><li>Magnesium sulfate seizure: 4–6 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1–2 g/h. 5 g IM each buttock if no IV. Continue 24h after birth / last seizure.</li><li>Mg toxicity: loss of reflexes → stop. Calcium gluconate 1 g IV over 3 min. Check creatinine; lower infusion if oliguric.</li></ul>\n<h5>GBS IAP · IAI · ACS · latency · EPL · NVP/HG · VTE · iron</h5>\n<ul><li>GBS, no allergy: PCN G 5 million U IV then <strong>2.5–3 million U q4h</strong> — or — amp 2 g then 1 g q4h.</li><li>GBS, low-risk PCN allergy: cefazolin 2 g IV then 1 g q8h.</li><li>GBS, high-risk + clinda-S: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h.</li><li>GBS, high-risk + clinda-R / unknown: vancomycin 20 mg/kg IV q8h, max 2 g/dose.</li><li>IAI: ampicillin 2 g IV q6h + gentamicin 5 mg/kg q24h (or 2 mg/kg then 1.5 mg/kg q8h). Cesarean: add clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV; after cesarean, at least one more dose plus anaerobic coverage. After vaginal birth: no additional dose. Antipyretic per CSM order set.</li><li>ACS: betamethasone 12 mg IM ×2, 24h apart — or — dexamethasone 6 mg IM q12h ×4.</li><li>Latency &lt;34w PPROM: amp 2 g IV q6h ×48h then amox 250 q8h to day 7 <strong>PLUS azithromycin 1 g PO ×1</strong>. Azithromycin, not erythromycin. No amoxicillin–clavulanate. See ANT-01.</li><li>EPL medical: mifepristone 200 mg PO, then misoprostol 800 mcg vaginally 24h later. Miso alone 800 mcg vaginally, repeat ≥3h / within 7 days if needed.</li><li>NVP/HG first-line (PB 189): pyridoxine 10–25 mg PO TID–QID alone or with doxylamine 12.5 mg PO TID–QID; or pyridoxine 10 mg / doxylamine 10 mg combo (up to 4 tabs/day); or delayed-release 20/20 (up to 2 tabs/day). See ANT-08.</li><li>NVP/HG dehydrated: IV fluids; thiamine 100 mg IV with first bag then 100 mg IV daily ×2–3 days if vomiting &gt;3 weeks (before/with dextrose). Ondansetron IV max 16 mg single dose. See ANT-08 ladder.</li><li>Therapeutic LMWH: enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Hold 24h before scheduled birth. Restart 4–6h after SVD / 6–12h after cesarean.</li><li>Prophylactic LMWH: enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily. Hold 12h before scheduled birth.</li><li>Oral iron: ferrous sulfate once daily or every other day — as effective as BID and better tolerated.</li><li>perimortem cesarean at bedside, still in arrest, fundus≥20 wks—scalpel is the instrument.</li></ul>", "flags": [], "text": " Confirm vs house: doses are ACOG-card extracts. Cross-check the CSM pharmacy build and antibiogram before you hang the bag.  If the house cart later disagrees, the cart wins.  \n Postpartum hemorrhage \n  Oxytocin treatment: 10–40 U in 500–1000 mL infusion or 10 U IM. Do not IV-push.  Methylergonovine: 0.2 mg IM q2–4h. No IV. Stop if HTN / PEC / CVD.  Carboprost: 0.25 mg IM or intramyometrial q15–90 min, max 8. Stop if asthma.  Misoprostol: 600–1000 mcg PO / SL / PR ×1.  TXA: 1 g IV when first-line meds fail. Best &lt;3h from birth. Not routine prophylaxis.  Device (house):  JADA first.  Bakri if JADA is not available or not appropriate (see PP-01).  MTP: ~1:1:1 RBC:FFP:plt. Cryo early if fibrinogen is low. Do not drown in crystalloid.  \n Severe-range BP and eclampsia \n  Treat SBP ≥160 or DBP ≥110 confirmed on a recheck within 15 min (persistent 15 min or more = hypertensive emergency). Give the first-line agent within 30 to 60 min  of confirmation , as soon as possible. Severe-range gHTN is treated as severe PEC.   First-line: any of three, equally appropriate (CO 767).  Climb one ladder; stop as soon as BP is below threshold.   Labetalol IV  (Box 3): 20 mg over 2 min → recheck 10 min → 40 mg → 10 min → 80 mg → 10 min → switch to hydralazine 10 mg IV → 20 min → still severe: emergency consult. Max cumulative 300 mg (PB 222); CMQCC 220 mg; use pharmacy cap. Avoid asthma, heart disease or CHF, heart block, bradycardia.   Hydralazine IV  (Box 2): 5 or 10 mg over 2 min → recheck 20 min → 10 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV → 10 min → labetalol 40 mg IV and emergency consult. Max 20 mg (PB 222).   Nifedipine IR PO  (Box 1): 10 mg → recheck 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV and emergency consult. Swallow whole; never SL or punctured. Max 180 mg/day (PB 222).   Local default (not a preference ranking):  start IV labetalol when IV access is present and there is no asthma, heart block, or bradycardia; oral IR nifedipine when there is no IV access; IV hydralazine when labetalol is contraindicated. If IR nifedipine is unavailable and there is no IV: labetalol 200 mg PO, may repeat in 30 min (CO 767).  Primary management with oral medications other than Nifedipine may be a reasonable adjunctive approach with concomitant IV therapy, but use alone is not supported by ACOG or local policy.  Magnesium sulfate seizure: 4–6 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1–2 g/h. 5 g IM each buttock if no IV. Continue 24h after birth / last seizure.  Mg toxicity: loss of reflexes → stop. Calcium gluconate 1 g IV over 3 min. Check creatinine; lower infusion if oliguric.  \n GBS IAP · IAI · ACS · latency · EPL · NVP/HG · VTE · iron \n  GBS, no allergy: PCN G 5 million U IV then  2.5–3 million U q4h  — or — amp 2 g then 1 g q4h.  GBS, low-risk PCN allergy: cefazolin 2 g IV then 1 g q8h.  GBS, high-risk + clinda-S: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h.  GBS, high-risk + clinda-R / unknown: vancomycin 20 mg/kg IV q8h, max 2 g/dose.  IAI: ampicillin 2 g IV q6h + gentamicin 5 mg/kg q24h (or 2 mg/kg then 1.5 mg/kg q8h). Cesarean: add clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV; after cesarean, at least one more dose plus anaerobic coverage. After vaginal birth: no additional dose. Antipyretic per CSM order set.  ACS: betamethasone 12 mg IM ×2, 24h apart — or — dexamethasone 6 mg IM q12h ×4.  Latency &lt;34w PPROM: amp 2 g IV q6h ×48h then amox 250 q8h to day 7  PLUS azithromycin 1 g PO ×1 . Azithromycin, not erythromycin. No amoxicillin–clavulanate. See ANT-01.  EPL medical: mifepristone 200 mg PO, then misoprostol 800 mcg vaginally 24h later. Miso alone 800 mcg vaginally, repeat ≥3h / within 7 days if needed.  NVP/HG first-line (PB 189): pyridoxine 10–25 mg PO TID–QID alone or with doxylamine 12.5 mg PO TID–QID; or pyridoxine 10 mg / doxylamine 10 mg combo (up to 4 tabs/day); or delayed-release 20/20 (up to 2 tabs/day). See ANT-08.  NVP/HG dehydrated: IV fluids; thiamine 100 mg IV with first bag then 100 mg IV daily ×2–3 days if vomiting &gt;3 weeks (before/with dextrose). Ondansetron IV max 16 mg single dose. See ANT-08 ladder.  Therapeutic LMWH: enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Hold 24h before scheduled birth. Restart 4–6h after SVD / 6–12h after cesarean.  Prophylactic LMWH: enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily. Hold 12h before scheduled birth.  Oral iron: ferrous sulfate once daily or every other day — as effective as BID and better tolerated.  perimortem cesarean at bedside, still in arrest, fundus≥20 wks—scalpel is the instrument.  "}, {"id": "ANT-10", "kind": "card", "title": "Indicated late-preterm / early-term delivery (ACOG CO 831 + CO 751)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Rules that sit above the table</p>\n<ul><li>Do not defer to 39w when there is a medical or obstetric indication to deliver earlier.</li><li>If the indication is real, deliver regardless of fetal lung-maturity testing. If you would wait on an immature result, there was no indication.</li><li>Anticipated late-preterm birth: one betamethasone course within 7 days if she has never had ACS. Do not delay an indicated delivery for the shot.</li><li>The lower end of a range is not automatically preferable. When two indications collide, the more severe clock wins. Uterine dehiscence and chronic abruption have no table row — individualize.</li></ul>\n<table><tr><th>Condition</th><th>Delivery timing</th></tr><tr><td><strong>Placenta / uterus</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Previa, uncomplicated</td><td>36+0–37+6</td></tr><tr><td>Suspected PAS</td><td>34+0–35+6 (planned/stable at a PAS-experienced level III/IV center — ANT-07)</td></tr><tr><td>Vasa previa</td><td>34+0–37+0</td></tr><tr><td>Prior classical cesarean or prior rupture</td><td>36+0–37+0</td></tr><tr><td>Prior myomectomy requiring cesarean</td><td>37+0–38+6 (extensive scar may behave like classical)</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Fetus, fluid, multiples</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Isolated oligohydramnios (DVP &lt;2 cm)</td><td>36+0–37+6, or at diagnosis if later</td></tr><tr><td>Mild idiopathic polyhydramnios</td><td>39+0–40+6 (early-term not routine)</td></tr><tr><td>FGR EFW 3rd–10th, otherwise well</td><td>38+0–39+0</td></tr><tr><td>FGR EFW &lt;3rd, or elevated UA Doppler with EDF</td><td>37+0, or at diagnosis if later</td></tr><tr><td>UA Doppler AEDF</td><td>33+0–34+0 + MFM (or at diagnosis if later)</td></tr><tr><td>UA Doppler REDF</td><td>30+0–32+0 + MFM (or at diagnosis if later)</td></tr><tr><td>di/di twins</td><td>38+0–38+6</td></tr><tr><td>mo/di twins</td><td>34+0–37+6</td></tr><tr><td>mo/mo twins</td><td>32+0–34+0</td></tr><tr><td>di/di + isolated FGR</td><td>36+0–37+6</td></tr><tr><td>mo/di + isolated FGR</td><td>32+0–34+6</td></tr><tr><td><strong>Maternal / obstetric</strong></td><td></td></tr><tr><td>cHTN controlled, no meds</td><td>38+0–39+6</td></tr><tr><td>cHTN on meds</td><td>37+0–39+6</td></tr><tr><td>cHTN difficult</td><td>36+0–37+6</td></tr><tr><td>gHTN without severe-range BP; PEC without SF</td><td>37+0</td></tr><tr><td>Severe-range gHTN; stable PEC with SF</td><td>34+0, or at diagnosis if later</td></tr><tr><td>Unstable PEC / HELLP / previable severe PEC</td><td>soon after maternal stabilization</td></tr><tr><td>Pregestational DM well controlled</td><td>39+0–39+6</td></tr><tr><td>Pregestational DM vascular / poor control / prior SB</td><td>36+0–38+6</td></tr><tr><td>GDM A1</td><td>39+0–40+6</td></tr><tr><td>GDM A2 well controlled</td><td>39+0–39+6</td></tr><tr><td>GDM poorly controlled</td><td>individualize</td></tr><tr><td>HIV on ART, VL ≤1,000 at or near delivery</td><td>Vaginal delivery is appropriate. Cesarean is not required for HIV prevention. Deliver ≥39+0 unless another indication exists.</td></tr><tr><td>HIV VL &gt;1,000 or unknown</td><td>Offer scheduled prelabor, prerupture cesarean at 38+0. Informed choice of route still stands.</td></tr><tr><td>ICP bile acids &lt;100</td><td>36+0–39+0</td></tr><tr><td>ICP bile acids ≥100</td><td>36+0</td></tr><tr><td>PPROM</td><td>34+0–36+6, not past 37+0; no latency Abx</td></tr><tr><td>Term PROM</td><td>at diagnosis</td></tr><tr><td>Prior stillbirth</td><td>full term preferred; 37+0–38+6 acceptable after counseling</td></tr></table>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — infection delivery / newborn</strong></p>\n<ul><li>HSV: start suppression at 36w; cesarean if genital lesions or prodrome in labor.</li><li>HBV+: cesarean not for HBV alone — confirm newborn HBIG + vaccine within 12 hours.</li><li>Chlamydia: pregnancy test-of-cure NAAT ~4 weeks after treatment (not optional).</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Indicated beats 39 weeks. Do not wait on lung-maturity testing. Do not hold an indicated late-preterm birth for steroids. HIV with VL ≤1,000 on ART: any route; cesarean is not required for prevention.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Rules that sit above the table \n  Do not defer to 39w when there is a medical or obstetric indication to deliver earlier.  If the indication is real, deliver regardless of fetal lung-maturity testing. If you would wait on an immature result, there was no indication.  Anticipated late-preterm birth: one betamethasone course within 7 days if she has never had ACS. Do not delay an indicated delivery for the shot.  The lower end of a range is not automatically preferable. When two indications collide, the more severe clock wins. Uterine dehiscence and chronic abruption have no table row — individualize.  \n   Condition  Delivery timing     Placenta / uterus       Previa, uncomplicated  36+0–37+6    Suspected PAS  34+0–35+6 (planned/stable at a PAS-experienced level III/IV center — ANT-07)    Vasa previa  34+0–37+0    Prior classical cesarean or prior rupture  36+0–37+0    Prior myomectomy requiring cesarean  37+0–38+6 (extensive scar may behave like classical)     Fetus, fluid, multiples       Isolated oligohydramnios (DVP &lt;2 cm)  36+0–37+6, or at diagnosis if later    Mild idiopathic polyhydramnios  39+0–40+6 (early-term not routine)    FGR EFW 3rd–10th, otherwise well  38+0–39+0    FGR EFW &lt;3rd, or elevated UA Doppler with EDF  37+0, or at diagnosis if later    UA Doppler AEDF  33+0–34+0 + MFM (or at diagnosis if later)    UA Doppler REDF  30+0–32+0 + MFM (or at diagnosis if later)    di/di twins  38+0–38+6    mo/di twins  34+0–37+6    mo/mo twins  32+0–34+0    di/di + isolated FGR  36+0–37+6    mo/di + isolated FGR  32+0–34+6     Maternal / obstetric       cHTN controlled, no meds  38+0–39+6    cHTN on meds  37+0–39+6    cHTN difficult  36+0–37+6    gHTN without severe-range BP; PEC without SF  37+0    Severe-range gHTN; stable PEC with SF  34+0, or at diagnosis if later    Unstable PEC / HELLP / previable severe PEC  soon after maternal stabilization    Pregestational DM well controlled  39+0–39+6    Pregestational DM vascular / poor control / prior SB  36+0–38+6    GDM A1  39+0–40+6    GDM A2 well controlled  39+0–39+6    GDM poorly controlled  individualize    HIV on ART, VL ≤1,000 at or near delivery  Vaginal delivery is appropriate. Cesarean is not required for HIV prevention. Deliver ≥39+0 unless another indication exists.    HIV VL &gt;1,000 or unknown  Offer scheduled prelabor, prerupture cesarean at 38+0. Informed choice of route still stands.    ICP bile acids &lt;100  36+0–39+0    ICP bile acids ≥100  36+0    PPROM  34+0–36+6, not past 37+0; no latency Abx    Term PROM  at diagnosis    Prior stillbirth  full term preferred; 37+0–38+6 acceptable after counseling   \n   OBHG pearl — infection delivery / newborn  \n  HSV: start suppression at 36w; cesarean if genital lesions or prodrome in labor.  HBV+: cesarean not for HBV alone — confirm newborn HBIG + vaccine within 12 hours.  Chlamydia: pregnancy test-of-cure NAAT ~4 weeks after treatment (not optional).   \n BOTTOM LINE. Indicated beats 39 weeks. Do not wait on lung-maturity testing. Do not hold an indicated late-preterm birth for steroids. HIV with VL ≤1,000 on ART: any route; cesarean is not required for prevention. "}, {"id": "LND-01", "kind": "card", "title": "Intrapartum FHR (ACOG CPG 10)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Categories</p>\n<ul><li>I — baseline 110–160, moderate variability, no late or variable decels. Optional early decels / accelerations. Continue current care.</li><li>III — sinusoidal, or absent variability plus recurrent lates, recurrent variables, or bradycardia. Resuscitate while you move toward delivery.</li><li>II — everything else. Interpret in context (stage, infection, bleeding, scar).</li></ul>\n<p>Intrauterine resuscitation — do these before you declare failure</p>\n<ul><li>Reposition. Stop or halve oxytocin. IV fluid bolus if appropriate. Maternal oxygen only if she is hypoxic — not as a reflex.</li><li>Tachysystole: terbutaline 0.25 mg SQ. Scalp stimulation: an acceleration makes ongoing acidemia unlikely in that moment; no acceleration does not prove it.</li><li>Amnioinfusion is reasonable for recurrent variables with ruptured membranes.</li><li>No fetal scalp electrode if HIV VL &gt;1,000, active genital HSV, previa / vasa previa, unknown presenting part, or a suspected fetal bleeding diathesis.</li></ul>\n<p>When to proceed to cesarean</p>\n<ul><li>Category III that does not correct. Do not wait for a pH.</li><li>Category II with absent or minimal variability and recurrent decels that do not respond — especially with a scarred uterus or bleeding.</li><li>Document the category, the maneuvers, and the decision time. Pair with LND-02 if the question is arrest rather than the tracing.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — Cat II triage &amp; adjuncts</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Weigh higher-risk Cat II features (absent variability; prolonged unexplained minimal variability; unexplained baseline shift to tachycardia; recurrent late/variable or &gt;1 prolonged deceleration) against protective features (moderate variability; accelerations).</li><li>Do not use ST analysis, intrapartum fetal pulse oximetry, or computerized FHR interpretation as primary management.</li><li>Same FHR framework is reasonable &lt;32w, interpreted in clinical context.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Category I observe. Category III move. Category II resuscitate with a clock — do not admire a deteriorating tracing.</p>\n<p>Category II re-look: no later than 60 min after resuscitation starts, and again every 60 min while resuscitation continues (local CSM standard; CPG 10 sets no interval).</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Categories \n  I — baseline 110–160, moderate variability, no late or variable decels. Optional early decels / accelerations. Continue current care.  III — sinusoidal, or absent variability plus recurrent lates, recurrent variables, or bradycardia. Resuscitate while you move toward delivery.  II — everything else. Interpret in context (stage, infection, bleeding, scar).  \n Intrauterine resuscitation — do these before you declare failure \n  Reposition. Stop or halve oxytocin. IV fluid bolus if appropriate. Maternal oxygen only if she is hypoxic — not as a reflex.  Tachysystole: terbutaline 0.25 mg SQ. Scalp stimulation: an acceleration makes ongoing acidemia unlikely in that moment; no acceleration does not prove it.  Amnioinfusion is reasonable for recurrent variables with ruptured membranes.  No fetal scalp electrode if HIV VL &gt;1,000, active genital HSV, previa / vasa previa, unknown presenting part, or a suspected fetal bleeding diathesis.  \n When to proceed to cesarean \n  Category III that does not correct. Do not wait for a pH.  Category II with absent or minimal variability and recurrent decels that do not respond — especially with a scarred uterus or bleeding.  Document the category, the maneuvers, and the decision time. Pair with LND-02 if the question is arrest rather than the tracing.  \n   OBHG pearl — Cat II triage &amp; adjuncts  \n  Weigh higher-risk Cat II features (absent variability; prolonged unexplained minimal variability; unexplained baseline shift to tachycardia; recurrent late/variable or &gt;1 prolonged deceleration) against protective features (moderate variability; accelerations).  Do not use ST analysis, intrapartum fetal pulse oximetry, or computerized FHR interpretation as primary management.  Same FHR framework is reasonable &lt;32w, interpreted in clinical context.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Category I observe. Category III move. Category II resuscitate with a clock — do not admire a deteriorating tracing.  Category II re-look: no later than 60 min after resuscitation starts, and again every 60 min while resuscitation continues (local CSM standard; CPG 10 sets no interval). "}, {"id": "LND-02", "kind": "card", "title": "First and second stage labor (ACOG CPG 8)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Latent labor can be long. Active labor starts at 6 cm, not 4 cm.</li><li>Do not diagnose arrest in the latent phase. Failed induction is a different diagnosis (LND-03).</li><li>Arrest of dilation (active phase): ≥6 cm, membranes ruptured, and no change for ≥4 hours with adequate MVUs or ≥6 hours with inadequate MVUs despite oxytocin.</li><li>Adequate contractions are generally ≥200 MVU for ≥4 hours. If there is no IUPC, use clinical adequacy and time.</li><li>Second stage / arrest of descent: nullipara &gt;3 hours of pushing, multipara &gt;2 hours. Beyond this, individualize (descent/rotation, tracing, patient preference); a longer time with neuraxial may be reasonable. Arrest may be called earlier if there is no descent or rotation despite adequate effort (CPG 8).</li><li>Longer may be appropriate if descent is occurring and the tracing is acceptable. Operative vaginal birth is an option when prerequisites are met (LND-07).</li><li>Do not diagnose failed labor because she is 5 cm at hour 18. That is still latent. Treat the tracing and the pelvis, not the clock alone.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — active labor tactics</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Offer early amniotomy when appropriate to shorten labor without raising cesarean.</li><li>Begin pushing at complete dilation — do not routinely labor down.</li><li>For persistent OP/OT, consider manual rotation before second-stage cesarean if prerequisites for OVB/rotation are met.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. 6 cm opens the arrest clock. 4 hours adequate / 6 hours inadequate. Second stage 3/2 hours, then individualize. Ongoing descent plus a safe tracing can support more time.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Latent labor can be long. Active labor starts at 6 cm, not 4 cm.  Do not diagnose arrest in the latent phase. Failed induction is a different diagnosis (LND-03).  Arrest of dilation (active phase): ≥6 cm, membranes ruptured, and no change for ≥4 hours with adequate MVUs or ≥6 hours with inadequate MVUs despite oxytocin.  Adequate contractions are generally ≥200 MVU for ≥4 hours. If there is no IUPC, use clinical adequacy and time.  Second stage / arrest of descent: nullipara >3 hours of pushing, multipara >2 hours. Beyond this, individualize (descent/rotation, tracing, patient preference); a longer time with neuraxial may be reasonable. Arrest may be called earlier if there is no descent or rotation despite adequate effort (CPG 8).  Longer may be appropriate if descent is occurring and the tracing is acceptable. Operative vaginal birth is an option when prerequisites are met (LND-07).  Do not diagnose failed labor because she is 5 cm at hour 18. That is still latent. Treat the tracing and the pelvis, not the clock alone.  \n   OBHG pearl — active labor tactics  \n  Offer early amniotomy when appropriate to shorten labor without raising cesarean.  Begin pushing at complete dilation — do not routinely labor down.  For persistent OP/OT, consider manual rotation before second-stage cesarean if prerequisites for OVB/rotation are met.   \n BOTTOM LINE. 6 cm opens the arrest clock. 4 hours adequate / 6 hours inadequate. Second stage 3/2 hours, then individualize. Ongoing descent plus a safe tracing can support more time. "}, {"id": "LND-03", "kind": "card", "title": "Cervical ripening (ACOG CPG 9)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Mechanical (Foley / double-balloon) and prostaglandins are both acceptable. Combination Foley + misoprostol or Foley + oxytocin is reasonable.</li><li>Misoprostol typical vaginal dose 25 mcg every 3–6 hours. Do not start with 50 mcg on a scarred or grand-multiparous uterus.</li><li>Dinoprostone insert / gel per label. Remove the insert for tachysystole or a deteriorating tracing.</li><li>Do not use PGE for ripening or induction at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery (LND-08).</li><li>Do not call failed induction until ripening plus approximately 24 hours of oxytocin after rupture of membranes (or the equivalent house definition), unless maternal or fetal status forces the issue.</li><li>Outpatient ripening only in a selected low-risk patient with a clear return plan — not for a scar, twins, FGR with abnormal Dopplers, or previa.</li><li>2024 CPU — full-term nullipara without a medical indication: elective induction at 39 weeks is acceptable if the unit can staff it. Not a mandate. Bishop score still matters for counseling.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Ripen the closed cervix you intend to deliver. Foley or 25-mcg misoprostol. No misoprostol on a scar. Failed induction is a long clock, not a 12-hour Bishop score of 3.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Mechanical (Foley / double-balloon) and prostaglandins are both acceptable. Combination Foley + misoprostol or Foley + oxytocin is reasonable.  Misoprostol typical vaginal dose 25 mcg every 3–6 hours. Do not start with 50 mcg on a scarred or grand-multiparous uterus.  Dinoprostone insert / gel per label. Remove the insert for tachysystole or a deteriorating tracing.  Do not use PGE for ripening or induction at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery (LND-08).  Do not call failed induction until ripening plus approximately 24 hours of oxytocin after rupture of membranes (or the equivalent house definition), unless maternal or fetal status forces the issue.  Outpatient ripening only in a selected low-risk patient with a clear return plan — not for a scar, twins, FGR with abnormal Dopplers, or previa.  2024 CPU — full-term nullipara without a medical indication: elective induction at 39 weeks is acceptable if the unit can staff it. Not a mandate. Bishop score still matters for counseling.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Ripen the closed cervix you intend to deliver. Foley or 25-mcg misoprostol. No misoprostol on a scar. Failed induction is a long clock, not a 12-hour Bishop score of 3. "}, {"id": "LND-04", "kind": "card", "title": "Intraamniotic infection (CO 712 + 2024 CPU)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Isolated maternal fever (38.0 to 38.9 °C, no other risk factor): consider antibiotics unless a source other than IAI is identified and documented (CO 712). The 2024 CPU keeps 38.0 to 38.9 °C plus one risk factor as suspected IAI and adds diagnosis without fever when other signs are present; it does not make isolated fever alone suspected IAI.</li><li>Suspected IAI: temperature ≥39.0 °C, or 38.0 to 38.9 °C plus maternal leukocytosis, purulent cervical drainage, or fetal tachycardia; or no fever with other signs such as fundal tenderness or foul discharge (2024 CPU). Confirmed IAI: positive amniotic fluid Gram stain, glucose, or culture, or placental histology (CO 712).</li><li>Do not wait for the next hour's temperature if she looks infected.</li><li>Antibiotics as soon as suspected. Ampicillin 2 g IV q6h + gentamicin 5 mg/kg q24h (or 2 mg/kg then 1.5 mg/kg q8h).</li><li>Give an antipyretic with the antibiotics (CO 712); agent and dose per CSM order set.</li><li>If she goes to cesarean, add anaerobic coverage: clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV (CO 712).</li><li>Delivery is indicated. IAI is not by itself an indication for cesarean. Labor may continue if the tracing and progress allow.</li><li>After vaginal birth: no additional postpartum dose (CO 712). After cesarean: at least one additional dose of the regimen plus clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV for at least one dose (CO 712). Extend for bacteremia or persistent fever.</li><li>GBS-only prophylaxis is the wrong regimen once IAI is called. Switch to the IAI pair and keep GBS covered.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Suspected IAI: ampicillin plus gentamicin now. Isolated fever: consider antibiotics. Add anaerobic coverage for cesarean. Deliver; usually still vaginal if she is laboring well.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Isolated maternal fever (38.0 to 38.9 °C, no other risk factor): consider antibiotics unless a source other than IAI is identified and documented (CO 712). The 2024 CPU keeps 38.0 to 38.9 °C plus one risk factor as suspected IAI and adds diagnosis without fever when other signs are present; it does not make isolated fever alone suspected IAI.  Suspected IAI: temperature ≥39.0 °C, or 38.0 to 38.9 °C plus maternal leukocytosis, purulent cervical drainage, or fetal tachycardia; or no fever with other signs such as fundal tenderness or foul discharge (2024 CPU). Confirmed IAI: positive amniotic fluid Gram stain, glucose, or culture, or placental histology (CO 712).  Do not wait for the next hour's temperature if she looks infected.  Antibiotics as soon as suspected. Ampicillin 2 g IV q6h + gentamicin 5 mg/kg q24h (or 2 mg/kg then 1.5 mg/kg q8h).  Give an antipyretic with the antibiotics (CO 712); agent and dose per CSM order set.  If she goes to cesarean, add anaerobic coverage: clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV (CO 712).  Delivery is indicated. IAI is not by itself an indication for cesarean. Labor may continue if the tracing and progress allow.  After vaginal birth: no additional postpartum dose (CO 712). After cesarean: at least one additional dose of the regimen plus clindamycin 900 mg IV or metronidazole 500 mg IV for at least one dose (CO 712). Extend for bacteremia or persistent fever.  GBS-only prophylaxis is the wrong regimen once IAI is called. Switch to the IAI pair and keep GBS covered.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Suspected IAI: ampicillin plus gentamicin now. Isolated fever: consider antibiotics. Add anaerobic coverage for cesarean. Deliver; usually still vaginal if she is laboring well. "}, {"id": "LND-05", "kind": "card", "title": "GBS prophylaxis (ACOG CO 797)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Who receives IAP</p>\n<ul><li>Positive 36+0–37+6 vaginal–rectal culture. GBS bacteriuria at any colony count this pregnancy. Prior GBS-infected newborn.</li><li>Unknown culture: preterm labor or PPROM, ROM ≥18 hours at term, temperature ≥38.0 °C.</li><li>Do not give GBS-specific IAP for planned prelabor cesarean with intact membranes, even if culture-positive. Still screen at 36–37+6.</li></ul>\n<p>Allergy</p>\n<ul><li>None: PCN G 5 million U then <strong>2.5–3 million U q4h</strong>, or amp 2 g then 1 g q4h</li><li>Low-risk (GI, morbilliform, family history, cannot recall): cefazolin 2 g then 1 g q8h</li><li>High-risk + clindamycin-susceptible isolate: clindamycin 900 mg q8h</li><li>High-risk + clindamycin-resistant or unknown: vancomycin 20 mg/kg q8h, max 2 g/dose, 60–90 min infusion</li><li>High-risk = hives, anaphylaxis, SJS/TEN, DRESS, recurrent beta-lactam reactions. Mark penicillin allergy on the culture order so the laboratory runs clindamycin susceptibility.</li><li>Do not delay oxytocin, amniotomy, or cesarean solely to finish 4 hours. Two hours still reduces colony counts.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Screen 36+0–37+6. Penicillin first. Low-risk allergy receives cefazolin, not vancomycin. Do not hold delivery for hour 4.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Who receives IAP \n  Positive 36+0–37+6 vaginal–rectal culture. GBS bacteriuria at any colony count this pregnancy. Prior GBS-infected newborn.  Unknown culture: preterm labor or PPROM, ROM ≥18 hours at term, temperature ≥38.0 °C.  Do not give GBS-specific IAP for planned prelabor cesarean with intact membranes, even if culture-positive. Still screen at 36–37+6.  \n Allergy \n  None: PCN G 5 million U then  2.5–3 million U q4h , or amp 2 g then 1 g q4h  Low-risk (GI, morbilliform, family history, cannot recall): cefazolin 2 g then 1 g q8h  High-risk + clindamycin-susceptible isolate: clindamycin 900 mg q8h  High-risk + clindamycin-resistant or unknown: vancomycin 20 mg/kg q8h, max 2 g/dose, 60–90 min infusion  High-risk = hives, anaphylaxis, SJS/TEN, DRESS, recurrent beta-lactam reactions. Mark penicillin allergy on the culture order so the laboratory runs clindamycin susceptibility.  Do not delay oxytocin, amniotomy, or cesarean solely to finish 4 hours. Two hours still reduces colony counts.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Screen 36+0–37+6. Penicillin first. Low-risk allergy receives cefazolin, not vancomycin. Do not hold delivery for hour 4. "}, {"id": "LND-06", "kind": "card", "title": "Shoulder dystocia (ACOG PB 178)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Call it</p>\n<ul><li>The shoulder will not deliver with gentle axial traction and additional maneuvers are required. Turtle sign is a hint, not the definition.</li><li>Cannot be reliably predicted or prevented. Recurrence 1–17%. Brachial plexus injury can occur without a documented dystocia.</li></ul>\n<p>Maneuver order</p>\n<ul><li>Mark time in and time out. Call extra hands, anesthesia, and the neonatal team (CSM). Stop pushing until a maneuver is set. No fundal pressure. No lateral-only traction.</li><li>First: McRoberts + suprapubic pressure (directed down and toward the fetal face or sternum). Avoid aggressive or prolonged leg flexion: PB 178 links it to symphyseal separation and lateral femoral cutaneous neuropathy.</li><li>Next: deliver the posterior arm, then Rubin / Woods, then Gaskin if she can be rolled.</li><li>Last resort, only with help already in the room: Zavanelli (cephalic replacement, then cesarean), abdominal rescue, intentional clavicle fracture, symphysiotomy (PB 178). A posterior axilla sling (12 or 14 French soft catheter) may help deliver the posterior shoulder.</li></ul>\n<p>Planned cesarean counseling</p>\n<ul><li>No diabetes, EFW ≥5000 g — consider scheduled cesarean.</li><li>Diabetes, EFW ≥4500 g — consider scheduled cesarean.</li><li>Do not schedule cesarean for every 4000 g fetus.</li><li>Document: diagnosis time, delivery time (head-to-body interval), anterior shoulder, maneuver sequence with timing and who performed each, traction, episiotomy, gases, neonatal examination, who was present, counseling given.</li><li>After delivery: PPH (11%) and fourth-degree laceration (3.8%) are more common (PB 178). Active third stage; inspect the anal sphincter. Any PPH: PP-01.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. McRoberts and suprapubic pressure first. Posterior arm next. No fundal pressure. Write the times.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Call it \n  The shoulder will not deliver with gentle axial traction and additional maneuvers are required. Turtle sign is a hint, not the definition.  Cannot be reliably predicted or prevented. Recurrence 1–17%. Brachial plexus injury can occur without a documented dystocia.  \n Maneuver order \n  Mark time in and time out. Call extra hands, anesthesia, and the neonatal team (CSM). Stop pushing until a maneuver is set. No fundal pressure. No lateral-only traction.  First: McRoberts + suprapubic pressure (directed down and toward the fetal face or sternum). Avoid aggressive or prolonged leg flexion: PB 178 links it to symphyseal separation and lateral femoral cutaneous neuropathy.  Next: deliver the posterior arm, then Rubin / Woods, then Gaskin if she can be rolled.  Last resort, only with help already in the room: Zavanelli (cephalic replacement, then cesarean), abdominal rescue, intentional clavicle fracture, symphysiotomy (PB 178). A posterior axilla sling (12 or 14 French soft catheter) may help deliver the posterior shoulder.  \n Planned cesarean counseling \n  No diabetes, EFW ≥5000 g — consider scheduled cesarean.  Diabetes, EFW ≥4500 g — consider scheduled cesarean.  Do not schedule cesarean for every 4000 g fetus.  Document: diagnosis time, delivery time (head-to-body interval), anterior shoulder, maneuver sequence with timing and who performed each, traction, episiotomy, gases, neonatal examination, who was present, counseling given.  After delivery: PPH (11%) and fourth-degree laceration (3.8%) are more common (PB 178). Active third stage; inspect the anal sphincter. Any PPH: PP-01.  \n BOTTOM LINE. McRoberts and suprapubic pressure first. Posterior arm next. No fundal pressure. Write the times. "}, {"id": "LND-07", "kind": "card", "title": "Operative vaginal birth (ACOG PB 219)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Do not apply</p>\n<ul><li>Head unengaged. Position unknown. Live fetus with a bone-demineralization disorder or a bleeding diathesis.</li><li>Vacuum is discouraged before 34 weeks. Sequential vacuum-then-forceps is not routine.</li></ul>\n<p>Doing the procedure</p>\n<ul><li>Forceps more often complete the birth and are associated with more OASIS. Vacuum detaches more often and bruises scalp. Both are acceptable.</li><li>No routine episiotomy. Midline episiotomy plus an instrument is a poor perineal combination.</li><li>Expect descent with the first pulls. If nothing moves, stop and proceed to cesarean. Vacuum application &gt;5 minutes raises cephalohematoma risk.</li><li>No rocking. No torque-to-rotate. Notify pediatrics that an instrument was used.</li></ul>\n<p>Antibiotics</p>\n<ul><li>ANODE reduced perineal infection with a single IV dose in a trial population that mostly had episiotomy. ACOG does not make pre-instrument antibiotics routine for a U.S. population.</li><li>Antibiotics are reasonable after OVB plus episiotomy, and after a 3rd- or 4th-degree laceration.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Know the position. One instrument. Descend or abandon. Notify the neonatal team. Give antibiotics if a 3rd- or 4th-degree laceration is observed or incised.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Do not apply \n  Head unengaged. Position unknown. Live fetus with a bone-demineralization disorder or a bleeding diathesis.  Vacuum is discouraged before 34 weeks. Sequential vacuum-then-forceps is not routine.  \n Doing the procedure \n  Forceps more often complete the birth and are associated with more OASIS. Vacuum detaches more often and bruises scalp. Both are acceptable.  No routine episiotomy. Midline episiotomy plus an instrument is a poor perineal combination.  Expect descent with the first pulls. If nothing moves, stop and proceed to cesarean. Vacuum application &gt;5 minutes raises cephalohematoma risk.  No rocking. No torque-to-rotate. Notify pediatrics that an instrument was used.  \n Antibiotics \n  ANODE reduced perineal infection with a single IV dose in a trial population that mostly had episiotomy. ACOG does not make pre-instrument antibiotics routine for a U.S. population.  Antibiotics are reasonable after OVB plus episiotomy, and after a 3rd- or 4th-degree laceration.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Know the position. One instrument. Descend or abandon. Notify the neonatal team. Give antibiotics if a 3rd- or 4th-degree laceration is observed or incised. "}, {"id": "LND-08", "kind": "card", "title": "TOLAC / VBAC (ACOG PB 205)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Who is a candidate</p>\n<ul><li>Offer: one prior low-transverse cesarean. Two prior LTCS is reasonable after counseling. Unknown scar is acceptable unless the prior birth was extremely preterm (consider classical). Twins are acceptable if otherwise a twin-vaginal candidate.</li><li>Do not plan TOLAC: classical or T incision, prior rupture, extensive transfundal surgery, previa.</li><li>Induction remains an option. Foley is acceptable. No misoprostol at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery.</li></ul>\n<p>In labor</p>\n<ul><li>Continuous electronic fetal monitoring. Epidural is allowed and does not mask rupture.</li><li>Most rupture signs are FHR abnormalities. Do not wait for loss of station or a nonfunctioning IUPC.</li><li>The facility must be able to perform emergency cesarean for an immediate threat. Discuss real operating-room and anesthesia availability. Planned home birth is contraindicated.</li><li>Counsel both paths. Document the plan.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. One prior low-transverse cesarean: offer TOLAC. No misoprostol. Continuous tracing. Be prepared for emergent cesarean.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Who is a candidate \n  Offer: one prior low-transverse cesarean. Two prior LTCS is reasonable after counseling. Unknown scar is acceptable unless the prior birth was extremely preterm (consider classical). Twins are acceptable if otherwise a twin-vaginal candidate.  Do not plan TOLAC: classical or T incision, prior rupture, extensive transfundal surgery, previa.  Induction remains an option. Foley is acceptable. No misoprostol at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery.  \n In labor \n  Continuous electronic fetal monitoring. Epidural is allowed and does not mask rupture.  Most rupture signs are FHR abnormalities. Do not wait for loss of station or a nonfunctioning IUPC.  The facility must be able to perform emergency cesarean for an immediate threat. Discuss real operating-room and anesthesia availability. Planned home birth is contraindicated.  Counsel both paths. Document the plan.  \n BOTTOM LINE. One prior low-transverse cesarean: offer TOLAC. No misoprostol. Continuous tracing. Be prepared for emergent cesarean. "}, {"id": "LND-09", "kind": "card", "title": "Umbilical cord prolapse (RCOG GTG 50, international)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md", "html": "<p>Call it</p>\n<ul><li>Cord felt or seen at or past the presenting part after membranes rupture, or sudden bradycardia or variables after ROM: examine now (speculum or digital) (GTG 50).</li><li>Call for help: OBHG hospitalist, anesthesia, charge RN, neonatal team, OR. Prepare for immediate birth (GTG 50). Note the time of diagnosis.</li><li><strong>Stop oxytocin</strong> (standard practice).</li></ul>\n<p>Take the pressure off the cord</p>\n<ul><li><strong>Elevate the presenting part manually</strong>: two gloved fingers push the head up and keep it there until the baby is delivered (GTG 50). Do not push so far that more cord comes down.</li><li>Do not try to replace the cord above the presenting part to continue labor. Handle loops of cord outside the vagina as little as possible (spasm) (GTG 50).</li><li>Position: knee–chest, or left lateral with head down and a pillow under the left hip; Trendelenburg is an option (GTG 50).</li><li><strong>Bladder fill if delivery will be delayed</strong>: Foley, instil 500–750 mL saline, clamp. <strong>Empty the bladder before any delivery attempt</strong> (GTG 50).</li><li>Persistent FHR abnormality despite elevation, especially if birth will be delayed: terbutaline 0.25 mg SQ (LND-01; GTG 50).</li><li>None of these may delay the birth (GTG 50).</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Deliver by the fastest safe route</p>\n<ul><li>Not fully dilated or birth not imminent: cesarean (GTG 50).</li><li>Abnormal FHR: <strong>category 1 cesarean. Standard: decision-to-delivery ≤30 minutes; goal 10 minutes.</strong> Normal FHR: urgent cesarean with continuous monitoring; upgrade at once if the tracing turns (GTG 50).</li><li>Fully dilated and birth can be quick and safe: operative vaginal birth (LND-07), avoiding the cord (GTG 50).</li><li>Anesthesia chooses general vs regional by urgency. Emergency consent per CSM policy. Keep the hand on the presenting part during prep and until the surgeon delivers.</li><li>No FHR: confirm with bedside ultrasound. Fetal death confirmed: no emergency cesarean for the fetus.</li><li>Previable or periviable: counsel; individualize. Alert house supervisor or CMO. MFM via the local MFM call schedule posted in the call lounge.</li><li>Do not delay oxytocin, amniotomy, or cesarean solely to finish 4 hours (LND-05: GBS).</li></ul>\n<p>Baby and paperwork</p>\n<ul><li>Neonatal team at every birth after cord prolapse. Paired cord gases (GTG 50).</li><li>Document: time of diagnosis, who elevated the presenting part and how long, position, bladder volume, terbutaline time, FHR, decision and birth times, route, cord gases, neonatal team, who was present. Event report per CSM policy. Offer the patient a debrief (GTG 50).</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Hand up, hold it. Stop oxytocin. Knee–chest or head-down left lateral; fill the bladder if there will be a delay, and empty it before delivery. Cesarean unless vaginal birth is imminent; category 1 if the tracing is abnormal (decision-to-delivery ≤30 minutes; goal 10 minutes). Neonatal team and paired gases.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Call it \n   Cord felt or seen at or past the presenting part after membranes rupture, or sudden bradycardia or variables after ROM: examine now (speculum or digital) (GTG 50).   Call for help: OBHG hospitalist, anesthesia, charge RN, neonatal team, OR. Prepare for immediate birth (GTG 50). Note the time of diagnosis.    Stop oxytocin  (standard practice).  \n Take the pressure off the cord \n    Elevate the presenting part manually : two gloved fingers push the head up and keep it there until the baby is delivered (GTG 50). Do not push so far that more cord comes down.   Do not try to replace the cord above the presenting part to continue labor. Handle loops of cord outside the vagina as little as possible (spasm) (GTG 50).   Position: knee–chest, or left lateral with head down and a pillow under the left hip; Trendelenburg is an option (GTG 50).    Bladder fill if delivery will be delayed : Foley, instil 500–750 mL saline, clamp.  Empty the bladder before any delivery attempt  (GTG 50).   Persistent FHR abnormality despite elevation, especially if birth will be delayed: terbutaline 0.25 mg SQ (LND-01; GTG 50).   None of these may delay the birth (GTG 50).   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Deliver by the fastest safe route \n   Not fully dilated or birth not imminent: cesarean (GTG 50).   Abnormal FHR:  category 1 cesarean. Standard: decision-to-delivery ≤30 minutes; goal 10 minutes.  Normal FHR: urgent cesarean with continuous monitoring; upgrade at once if the tracing turns (GTG 50).   Fully dilated and birth can be quick and safe: operative vaginal birth (LND-07), avoiding the cord (GTG 50).   Anesthesia chooses general vs regional by urgency. Emergency consent per CSM policy. Keep the hand on the presenting part during prep and until the surgeon delivers.   No FHR: confirm with bedside ultrasound. Fetal death confirmed: no emergency cesarean for the fetus.   Previable or periviable: counsel; individualize. Alert house supervisor or CMO. MFM via the local MFM call schedule posted in the call lounge.   Do not delay oxytocin, amniotomy, or cesarean solely to finish 4 hours (LND-05: GBS).  \n Baby and paperwork \n   Neonatal team at every birth after cord prolapse. Paired cord gases (GTG 50).   Document: time of diagnosis, who elevated the presenting part and how long, position, bladder volume, terbutaline time, FHR, decision and birth times, route, cord gases, neonatal team, who was present. Event report per CSM policy. Offer the patient a debrief (GTG 50).  \n BOTTOM LINE. Hand up, hold it. Stop oxytocin. Knee–chest or head-down left lateral; fill the bladder if there will be a delay, and empty it before delivery. Cesarean unless vaginal birth is imminent; category 1 if the tracing is abnormal (decision-to-delivery ≤30 minutes; goal 10 minutes). Neonatal team and paired gases. "}, {"id": "LND-10", "kind": "card", "title": "Uterine rupture / TOLAC emergency (ACOG PB 205)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md", "html": "<p>Before labor: TOLAC readiness (PB 205, LND-08)</p>\n<ul><li>TOLAC belongs at a facility that can perform emergency cesarean. ACOG and international guidelines recommend resources for emergency cesarean be immediately available. Where they are not, discuss the hospital's obstetric, pediatric, anesthesia, and OR staff availability (PB 205). At CSM, \"immediately available\" = able to respond within 10 minutes.</li><li>The facility must be able to perform emergency cesarean for an immediate threat. Discuss real operating-room and anesthesia availability. Planned home birth is contraindicated.</li><li>Continuous electronic fetal monitoring. Epidural is allowed and does not mask rupture. No misoprostol at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery. Foley is acceptable (LND-08).</li><li>Same watch for any scarred uterus in labor (induction with a scar, prior myomectomy) and for high-risk unscarred uteri on oxytocin.</li></ul>\n<p>Recognize it</p>\n<ul><li><strong>FHR abnormality is the most common sign</strong>: up to 70% of ruptures, most often a prolonged deceleration or bradycardia (PB 205).</li><li>Others: increased contractions or loss of tone, vaginal bleeding, loss of fetal station, new intense uterine pain (PB 205). Also: hematuria, shoulder pain, hypotension or tachycardia, palpable fetal parts.</li><li>Most rupture signs are FHR abnormalities. Do not wait for loss of station or a nonfunctioning IUPC.</li><li>Excessive bleeding or hypovolemia after VBAC: examine the whole genital tract for rupture (PB 205).</li></ul>\n<p>Act</p>\n<ul><li>Call it: stat cesarean or laparotomy. OBHG hospitalist, anesthesia, OR, neonatal team. Stop oxytocin. Two large-bore IVs, type and cross, blood to the room.</li><li>Do not spend time on intrauterine resuscitation that will not fix a rupture. Category III or a prolonged deceleration with rupture signs: move.</li><li>Unstable mother: activate MTP per PP-01 (~1:1:1 RBC:FFP:plt; cryo early if fibrinogen is low). If either device fails, or you suspect inversion, rupture, or accreta — operating room. No JADA or Bakri for a rupture.</li><li>TXA (PP-01): 1 g IV over 10 min (1 g in 100 mL NS); repeat 1 g once if bleeding continues after 30 min (max 2 g in 24 h).</li><li>Arrest: SYS-04.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>At surgery</p>\n<ul><li>Deliver, then find the defect: extent, extension into the broad ligament, bladder, or cervix; inspect the bladder for hematuria.</li><li>Repair when the defect is clean, bleeding is controlled, and she is stable; surgeon's judgment. Hysterectomy for uncontrollable bleeding, an unreconstructable defect, or instability. Call the private attending for surgery or transfusion; backup physician or urology as needed.</li><li>After: VTE prophylaxis per the SYS-02 tree; enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily only when the tree calls for it. Rh status. Debrief the team.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Counsel for the next pregnancy</p>\n<ul><li>Repeat rupture: about 6% if the scar was confined to the lower segment; up to 32% (most recent estimate 15%) if it involved the upper segment (PB 205).</li><li>Repeat cesarean before labor; consider 36+0–37+0, individualized (PB 205, ANT-10). Write the scar location and repair in the operative note and tell her.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. TOLAC needs a team that can cut now. The tracing is usually the first sign; do not wait for pain or lost station. Stat cesarean, MTP and house TXA, no intrauterine device. Repair or hysterectomy by stability. Next time: repeat cesarean before labor, consider 36 to 37 weeks.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Before labor: TOLAC readiness (PB 205, LND-08) \n   TOLAC belongs at a facility that can perform emergency cesarean. ACOG and international guidelines recommend resources for emergency cesarean be immediately available. Where they are not, discuss the hospital's obstetric, pediatric, anesthesia, and OR staff availability (PB 205). At CSM, \"immediately available\" = able to respond within 10 minutes.   The facility must be able to perform emergency cesarean for an immediate threat. Discuss real operating-room and anesthesia availability. Planned home birth is contraindicated.   Continuous electronic fetal monitoring. Epidural is allowed and does not mask rupture. No misoprostol at term after cesarean or major uterine surgery. Foley is acceptable (LND-08).   Same watch for any scarred uterus in labor (induction with a scar, prior myomectomy) and for high-risk unscarred uteri on oxytocin.  \n Recognize it \n    FHR abnormality is the most common sign : up to 70% of ruptures, most often a prolonged deceleration or bradycardia (PB 205).   Others: increased contractions or loss of tone, vaginal bleeding, loss of fetal station, new intense uterine pain (PB 205). Also: hematuria, shoulder pain, hypotension or tachycardia, palpable fetal parts.   Most rupture signs are FHR abnormalities. Do not wait for loss of station or a nonfunctioning IUPC.   Excessive bleeding or hypovolemia after VBAC: examine the whole genital tract for rupture (PB 205).  \n Act \n   Call it: stat cesarean or laparotomy. OBHG hospitalist, anesthesia, OR, neonatal team. Stop oxytocin. Two large-bore IVs, type and cross, blood to the room.   Do not spend time on intrauterine resuscitation that will not fix a rupture. Category III or a prolonged deceleration with rupture signs: move.   Unstable mother: activate MTP per PP-01 (~1:1:1 RBC:FFP:plt; cryo early if fibrinogen is low). If either device fails, or you suspect inversion, rupture, or accreta — operating room. No JADA or Bakri for a rupture.   TXA (PP-01): 1 g IV over 10 min (1 g in 100 mL NS); repeat 1 g once if bleeding continues after 30 min (max 2 g in 24 h).   Arrest: SYS-04.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n At surgery \n   Deliver, then find the defect: extent, extension into the broad ligament, bladder, or cervix; inspect the bladder for hematuria.   Repair when the defect is clean, bleeding is controlled, and she is stable; surgeon's judgment. Hysterectomy for uncontrollable bleeding, an unreconstructable defect, or instability. Call the private attending for surgery or transfusion; backup physician or urology as needed.   After: VTE prophylaxis per the SYS-02 tree; enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily only when the tree calls for it. Rh status. Debrief the team.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Counsel for the next pregnancy \n   Repeat rupture: about 6% if the scar was confined to the lower segment; up to 32% (most recent estimate 15%) if it involved the upper segment (PB 205).   Repeat cesarean before labor; consider 36+0–37+0, individualized (PB 205, ANT-10). Write the scar location and repair in the operative note and tell her.  \n BOTTOM LINE. TOLAC needs a team that can cut now. The tracing is usually the first sign; do not wait for pain or lost station. Stat cesarean, MTP and house TXA, no intrauterine device. Repair or hysterectomy by stability. Next time: repeat cesarean before labor, consider 36 to 37 weeks. "}, {"id": "ANT-01", "kind": "card", "title": "PROM / PPROM (ACOG PB 217)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Confirm and sort</p>\n<ul><li>Sterile speculum. Pool / fern / nitrazine. Avoid digital examinations if you are not inducing.</li><li>Cannot miss: IAI (fever is not required — LND-04), abruption, cord prolapse, undiagnosed previa. Not PROM: leukorrhea, urine, semen.</li></ul>\n<p>By gestational age</p>\n<ul><li>≥37w: induction is recommended (oxytocin); if she prefers and there is no contraindication, expectant management for 12 to 24 h may be offered (PB 217). GBS positive: IAP and induce now, no expectant period (PB 217). No latency antibiotics.</li><li>34+0–36+6: expectant or deliver — shared decision (CO 831). No latency antibiotics. No tocolysis. Stop expectant at 37+0. Late-preterm ACS only if birth is 24h–7d away and no prior course — do not delay delivery for the shot.</li><li>24+0 to 33+6: expectant if stable. 7-day latency antibiotics. One ACS course; one rescue course may be considered if still &lt;34w, delivery is likely within 7 days, and the first course was &gt;14 days ago (CO 713, ANT-06). Magnesium if birth anticipated &lt;32w; dose per CSM order set. GBS: vaginal-rectal culture at presentation; IAP as indicated (PB 217).</li><li>Periviable: counsel with MFM/neonatology. Latency may start ~20w. ACS / GBS / magnesium from 23w tied to the resuscitation plan.</li><li>Previable or periviable PPROM: alert house supervisor or CMO. MFM via the local MFM call schedule posted in the call lounge.</li><li>Latency (formulary + this card): amp 2 g IV q6h ×48h then amoxicillin 250 q8h to day 7 <strong>PLUS azithromycin 1 g PO ×1</strong>. Azithromycin, not erythromycin. No amoxicillin–clavulanate (NEC). Do not continue past 7 days.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. &lt;34w and stable: admit, 7-day latency, ACS, magnesium if &lt;32w. 34 to 36w: choose with her, no latency drugs. Term: induction recommended; brief expectant management if she prefers and GBS is not positive. IAI: deliver at any gestational age.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Confirm and sort \n  Sterile speculum. Pool / fern / nitrazine. Avoid digital examinations if you are not inducing.  Cannot miss: IAI (fever is not required — LND-04), abruption, cord prolapse, undiagnosed previa. Not PROM: leukorrhea, urine, semen.  \n By gestational age \n  ≥37w: induction is recommended (oxytocin); if she prefers and there is no contraindication, expectant management for 12 to 24 h may be offered (PB 217). GBS positive: IAP and induce now, no expectant period (PB 217). No latency antibiotics.  34+0–36+6: expectant or deliver — shared decision (CO 831). No latency antibiotics. No tocolysis. Stop expectant at 37+0. Late-preterm ACS only if birth is 24h–7d away and no prior course — do not delay delivery for the shot.  24+0 to 33+6: expectant if stable. 7-day latency antibiotics. One ACS course; one rescue course may be considered if still &lt;34w, delivery is likely within 7 days, and the first course was &gt;14 days ago (CO 713, ANT-06). Magnesium if birth anticipated &lt;32w; dose per CSM order set. GBS: vaginal-rectal culture at presentation; IAP as indicated (PB 217).  Periviable: counsel with MFM/neonatology. Latency may start ~20w. ACS / GBS / magnesium from 23w tied to the resuscitation plan.  Previable or periviable PPROM: alert house supervisor or CMO. MFM via the local MFM call schedule posted in the call lounge.  Latency (formulary + this card): amp 2 g IV q6h ×48h then amoxicillin 250 q8h to day 7  PLUS azithromycin 1 g PO ×1 . Azithromycin, not erythromycin. No amoxicillin–clavulanate (NEC). Do not continue past 7 days.  \n BOTTOM LINE. &lt;34w and stable: admit, 7-day latency, ACS, magnesium if &lt;32w. 34 to 36w: choose with her, no latency drugs. Term: induction recommended; brief expectant management if she prefers and GBS is not positive. IAI: deliver at any gestational age. "}, {"id": "ANT-02", "kind": "card", "title": "Preterm labor (ACOG PB 171)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Regular contractions plus cervical change, or already ≥2 cm. Fewer than 10% deliver within 7 days of a clinical diagnosis.</li><li>Contractions, cervix &lt;2 cm, no change: observe. Do not tocolyze a test. fFN or a short cervix alone has a poor positive predictive value.</li><li>Do not tocolyze IAI, abruption, preeclampsia with severe features, or a nonreassuring tracing.</li><li>ACS now — first dose even if the second looks unlikely (ANT-06).</li><li>Tocolysis ≤48h only to complete steroids, magnesium, or transfer. First-line: nifedipine, indomethacin (&lt;32w), or short-course beta-agonist.</li><li>Magnesium for neuroprotection if birth is anticipated &lt;32w. If she is already on magnesium, add indomethacin rather than stacking nifedipine.</li><li>No bed rest, no hydration protocol, no maintenance tocolysis, no oral terbutaline at discharge. No antibiotics to prolong intact-membrane preterm labor.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. No cervical change → observe. True preterm labor &lt;34w → steroids, 48 hours of a real tocolytic, magnesium if &lt;32w. Then stop.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Regular contractions plus cervical change, or already ≥2 cm. Fewer than 10% deliver within 7 days of a clinical diagnosis.  Contractions, cervix &lt;2 cm, no change: observe. Do not tocolyze a test. fFN or a short cervix alone has a poor positive predictive value.  Do not tocolyze IAI, abruption, preeclampsia with severe features, or a nonreassuring tracing.  ACS now — first dose even if the second looks unlikely (ANT-06).  Tocolysis ≤48h only to complete steroids, magnesium, or transfer. First-line: nifedipine, indomethacin (&lt;32w), or short-course beta-agonist.  Magnesium for neuroprotection if birth is anticipated &lt;32w. If she is already on magnesium, add indomethacin rather than stacking nifedipine.  No bed rest, no hydration protocol, no maintenance tocolysis, no oral terbutaline at discharge. No antibiotics to prolong intact-membrane preterm labor.  \n BOTTOM LINE. No cervical change → observe. True preterm labor &lt;34w → steroids, 48 hours of a real tocolytic, magnesium if &lt;32w. Then stop. "}, {"id": "ANT-03", "kind": "card", "title": "Cerclage (ACOG CPG 12)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Offer the stitch</p>\n<ul><li>History-indicated: singleton with a classic cervical-insufficiency loss — offer at 12–14w, or watch cervical length 16–24w and stitch if &lt;25 mm.</li><li>Ultrasound-indicated: singleton + prior spontaneous preterm birth + CL &lt;25 mm at 16–24w → cerclage or vaginal progesterone.</li><li>Exam-indicated: dilated in the second trimester, singleton or twin, no IAI or labor → offer. Consider peri-procedure indomethacin + cefazolin.</li><li>Transabdominal cerclage: one failed vaginal history- or ultrasound-indicated cerclage with birth &lt;28w. Future births by cesarean.</li></ul>\n<p>Do not stitch</p>\n<ul><li>Twins with a short closed cervix. Suspected IAI or labor. Ongoing heavy bleeding.</li><li>Low-risk singleton with an isolated short cervix: default is vaginal progesterone.</li></ul>\n<p>PPROM with a stitch in place</p>\n<ul><li>Individualize. Removal reduces chorioamnionitis and also shortens latency. Retention buys hours to days and more infection.</li><li>Elective removal 36+0–37+6. May leave to 39+6 if planned cesarean (remove in the operating room).</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Classic insufficiency history → offer 12–14w. Prior spontaneous preterm birth + CL &lt;25 mm → stitch or vaginal progesterone. Dilated and not infected → exam-indicated. Twins with a closed short cervix → no stitch.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Offer the stitch \n  History-indicated: singleton with a classic cervical-insufficiency loss — offer at 12–14w, or watch cervical length 16–24w and stitch if &lt;25 mm.  Ultrasound-indicated: singleton + prior spontaneous preterm birth + CL &lt;25 mm at 16–24w → cerclage or vaginal progesterone.  Exam-indicated: dilated in the second trimester, singleton or twin, no IAI or labor → offer. Consider peri-procedure indomethacin + cefazolin.  Transabdominal cerclage: one failed vaginal history- or ultrasound-indicated cerclage with birth &lt;28w. Future births by cesarean.  \n Do not stitch \n  Twins with a short closed cervix. Suspected IAI or labor. Ongoing heavy bleeding.  Low-risk singleton with an isolated short cervix: default is vaginal progesterone.  \n PPROM with a stitch in place \n  Individualize. Removal reduces chorioamnionitis and also shortens latency. Retention buys hours to days and more infection.  Elective removal 36+0–37+6. May leave to 39+6 if planned cesarean (remove in the operating room).  \n BOTTOM LINE. Classic insufficiency history → offer 12–14w. Prior spontaneous preterm birth + CL &lt;25 mm → stitch or vaginal progesterone. Dilated and not infected → exam-indicated. Twins with a closed short cervix → no stitch. "}, {"id": "ANT-04", "kind": "card", "title": "Gestational HTN / preeclampsia (PB 222 + 2024 CPU)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Write the diagnosis</p>\n<ul><li>gHTN: ≥140/90 ×2, 4 hours apart, after 20w, no proteinuria. Up to 50% progress. Severe-range gHTN is managed as preeclampsia with severe features.</li><li>Preeclampsia: gHTN + proteinuria or a severe feature (platelets &lt;100k, creatinine &gt;1.1 or doubled, AST/ALT ≥2×, pulmonary edema, new cerebral or visual symptoms).</li><li>Proteinuria is not required when a severe feature is present.</li></ul>\n<p>Treat and deliver</p>\n<ul><li>Severe-range BP: confirm on a recheck within 15 minutes, then treat within 30 to 60 minutes of confirmation (formulary). IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, or oral IR nifedipine are equal first-line options; follow that agent's CO 767 ladder, including the switch step, then emergency consult. Magnesium for seizure prophylaxis when severe features are present and around delivery.</li><li>Without severe features: deliver at 37+0. With severe features, stable: 34+0. Sooner if eclampsia, pulmonary edema, DIC, nonreassuring tracing, abruption, uncontrolled BP, or progressing HELLP.</li><li>Aspirin 81 mg prophylaxis for high-risk patients, start 12–16w (may start through 28w if missed).</li></ul>\n<p>sFlt-1/PlGF ratio (2024 CPU)</p>\n<ul><li>Use only in hospitalized patients ~23–35w when estimating short-term risk of progression to severe features.</li><li>Ratio ≥40 is not a diagnosis. Ratio &lt;40 is not clearance. Do not discharge on a number.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — severe-range BP habits</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Do not excuse a severe-range BP (pushing, pain, bad news, cuff position). Trust the high number until proven resolved; recheck q15 min.</li><li>Treat first, name second — do not delay antihypertensives to finalize the label.</li><li>Persistent severe-range = still ≥160 or ≥110 on a recheck within 15 minutes (CO 767: persistent 15 minutes or more is a hypertensive emergency; AIM: two measurements within 15 minutes). Do not wait an hour to confirm. After severe HDP schedule follow-up within 3–5 days (± home BP).</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. 160/110 is an emergency. Confirm within 15 minutes; drug in within 60 minutes of confirmation. Labetalol, hydralazine, or IR nifedipine: any first-line agent, on its ladder. Magnesium if severe features. 34 weeks versus 37 weeks is the clock. The biomarker does not replace the diagnosis.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Write the diagnosis \n  gHTN: ≥140/90 ×2, 4 hours apart, after 20w, no proteinuria. Up to 50% progress. Severe-range gHTN is managed as preeclampsia with severe features.  Preeclampsia: gHTN + proteinuria or a severe feature (platelets &lt;100k, creatinine &gt;1.1 or doubled, AST/ALT ≥2×, pulmonary edema, new cerebral or visual symptoms).  Proteinuria is not required when a severe feature is present.  \n Treat and deliver \n  Severe-range BP: confirm on a recheck within 15 minutes, then treat within 30 to 60 minutes of confirmation (formulary). IV labetalol, IV hydralazine, or oral IR nifedipine are equal first-line options; follow that agent's CO 767 ladder, including the switch step, then emergency consult. Magnesium for seizure prophylaxis when severe features are present and around delivery.  Without severe features: deliver at 37+0. With severe features, stable: 34+0. Sooner if eclampsia, pulmonary edema, DIC, nonreassuring tracing, abruption, uncontrolled BP, or progressing HELLP.  Aspirin 81 mg prophylaxis for high-risk patients, start 12–16w (may start through 28w if missed).  \n sFlt-1/PlGF ratio (2024 CPU) \n  Use only in hospitalized patients ~23–35w when estimating short-term risk of progression to severe features.  Ratio ≥40 is not a diagnosis. Ratio &lt;40 is not clearance. Do not discharge on a number.  \n   OBHG pearl — severe-range BP habits  \n  Do not excuse a severe-range BP (pushing, pain, bad news, cuff position). Trust the high number until proven resolved; recheck q15 min.  Treat first, name second — do not delay antihypertensives to finalize the label.  Persistent severe-range = still ≥160 or ≥110 on a recheck within 15 minutes (CO 767: persistent 15 minutes or more is a hypertensive emergency; AIM: two measurements within 15 minutes). Do not wait an hour to confirm. After severe HDP schedule follow-up within 3–5 days (± home BP).   \n BOTTOM LINE. 160/110 is an emergency. Confirm within 15 minutes; drug in within 60 minutes of confirmation. Labetalol, hydralazine, or IR nifedipine: any first-line agent, on its ladder. Magnesium if severe features. 34 weeks versus 37 weeks is the clock. The biomarker does not replace the diagnosis. "}, {"id": "ANT-05", "kind": "card", "title": "Diabetes in pregnancy (ACOG PB 190 + 201)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>GDM A1 = diet-controlled. GDM A2 = medication. Pregestational = type 1 or type 2 diagnosed before pregnancy (or overt diabetes in early pregnancy).</li><li>Delivery timing (CO 831): GDM A1 39+0–40+6. GDM A2 well controlled 39+0–39+6. Poorly controlled GDM individualize. Pregestational, well controlled, no vascular disease 39+0–39+6. Vascular disease, poor control, or prior stillbirth 36+0–38+6.</li><li><strong>Labor, induction or scheduled cesarean:</strong> use the intrapartum algorithm. CSM chart: no insulin at 61–90 mg/dL (type 1 keeps a 0.5 units/h floor); below 70 = low, rescue per the CSM hypoglycemia protocol. The CSM nomogram sets the insulin rate and D5LR or LR by each FSBG; hourly FSBG while the drip runs; type 1 never goes without basal. (National reference: ACOG PB 201 labor target 70–110.) <button type=\"button\" class=\"linkbtn\" data-open=\"FD-ANT-05\">Open: Diabetes in labor: insulin and IV fluids</button></li><li>Diet-controlled GDM (A1) or oral agents ONLY (no home insulin): no insulin drip; sliding scale insulin as needed per provider order; FSBG every 4 h until active labor, then every 2 h; recheck 1 h after any sliding-scale dose. Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG). Any home insulin (with or without metformin/glyburide): drip group. Call provider now for any single FSBG &gt;180, any ketones, or vomiting/illness. The provider decides next steps: DKA screen (beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, BMP) and the CSM insulin drip only by provider order. If the drip is ordered, move to the drip group (hourly FSBG, CSM nomogram). Do not start the drip from this chart without an order. Glyburide and after-dose recheck rules: see the algorithm.</li><li>Shoulder counseling: consider scheduled cesarean at EFW ≥4500 g with diabetes, ≥5000 g without (LND-06). Counseling threshold, not an absolute safety cutoff.</li><li>Steroids: do not withhold indicated steroids solely for hyperglycemia. After betamethasone, glucose rises within about 12 h and can stay high up to 5 days: increase FSBG checks (at least 72 h, longer if still high), insulin changes per the CSM provider/pharmacy order set, and in labor follow the nomogram (higher rates expected; no bolus). Patients without diabetes: check FSBG if symptomatic or per provider. SMFM Consult #58: no late-preterm (34+0–36+6) steroids in pregestational diabetes (ANT-06).</li><li>Postpartum: notify the provider at delivery to start the CSM Postpartum Diabetes Management Order Set (doses there). Screen A1/A2 with a 75-g 2-hour OGTT at 4–12 weeks postpartum.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. A1 can wait to 40+6. A2 and well-controlled pregestational diabetes: 39 weeks. In labor: CSM nomogram, below 70 = low, hourly FSBG on the drip, type 1 never zero basal. EFW ≥4500 g with diabetes: consider cesarean (counseling, not a limit).</p>", "flags": [], "text": " GDM A1 = diet-controlled. GDM A2 = medication. Pregestational = type 1 or type 2 diagnosed before pregnancy (or overt diabetes in early pregnancy). Delivery timing (CO 831): GDM A1 39+0–40+6. GDM A2 well controlled 39+0–39+6. Poorly controlled GDM individualize. Pregestational, well controlled, no vascular disease 39+0–39+6. Vascular disease, poor control, or prior stillbirth 36+0–38+6. Labor, induction or scheduled cesarean: use the intrapartum algorithm. CSM chart: no insulin at 61–90 mg/dL (type 1 keeps a 0.5 units/h floor); below 70 = low, rescue per the CSM hypoglycemia protocol. The CSM nomogram sets the insulin rate and D5LR or LR by each FSBG; hourly FSBG while the drip runs; type 1 never goes without basal. (National reference: ACOG PB 201 labor target 70–110.) Open: Diabetes in labor: insulin and IV fluids Diet-controlled GDM (A1) or oral agents ONLY (no home insulin): no insulin drip; sliding scale insulin as needed per provider order; FSBG every 4 h until active labor, then every 2 h; recheck 1 h after any sliding-scale dose. Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG). Any home insulin (with or without metformin/glyburide): drip group. Call provider now for any single FSBG >180, any ketones, or vomiting/illness. The provider decides next steps: DKA screen (beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, BMP) and the CSM insulin drip only by provider order. If the drip is ordered, move to the drip group (hourly FSBG, CSM nomogram). Do not start the drip from this chart without an order. Glyburide and after-dose recheck rules: see the algorithm. Shoulder counseling: consider scheduled cesarean at EFW ≥4500 g with diabetes, ≥5000 g without (LND-06). Counseling threshold, not an absolute safety cutoff. Steroids: do not withhold indicated steroids solely for hyperglycemia. After betamethasone, glucose rises within about 12 h and can stay high up to 5 days: increase FSBG checks (at least 72 h, longer if still high), insulin changes per the CSM provider/pharmacy order set, and in labor follow the nomogram (higher rates expected; no bolus). Patients without diabetes: check FSBG if symptomatic or per provider. SMFM Consult #58: no late-preterm (34+0–36+6) steroids in pregestational diabetes (ANT-06). Postpartum: notify the provider at delivery to start the CSM Postpartum Diabetes Management Order Set (doses there). Screen A1/A2 with a 75-g 2-hour OGTT at 4–12 weeks postpartum. BOTTOM LINE. A1 can wait to 40+6. A2 and well-controlled pregestational diabetes: 39 weeks. In labor: CSM nomogram, below 70 = low, hourly FSBG on the drip, type 1 never zero basal. EFW ≥4500 g with diabetes: consider cesarean (counseling, not a limit). ", "source_note": "ANT-05 landing card revised 11 Nov 2026 per ALG-DM-INTRAPARTUM spec DM-0 and Doc verdict F10 (generated from js/dm-intrapartum.js)."}, {"id": "ANT-06", "kind": "card", "title": "Antenatal corticosteroids (ACOG CO 713)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>24+0–33+6: one course if delivery is likely within 7 days — including PPROM and twins.</li><li>From 23+0: consider, tied to the family's resuscitation decision.</li><li>34+0–36+6: one course of betamethasone if no prior course and delivery likely within 7 days. Expect more neonatal hypoglycemia. Do not delay indicated delivery (CO 831).</li><li>Betamethasone 12 mg IM ×2, 24 hours apart, or dexamethasone 6 mg IM q12h ×4. Do not shorten the interval.</li><li>Rescue: one repeat course if still &lt;34w, delivery again likely within 7 days, and the prior course was &gt;14 days ago (as early as 7 days if the scenario demands).</li><li>No weekly serial courses. Rescue with PPROM: insufficient evidence for or against.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. 24–33+6 and delivery this week: give the course. Late preterm only if never dosed. Rescue once, not on a calendar. Do not hold an indicated birth for the second injection.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  24+0–33+6: one course if delivery is likely within 7 days — including PPROM and twins.  From 23+0: consider, tied to the family's resuscitation decision.  34+0–36+6: one course of betamethasone if no prior course and delivery likely within 7 days. Expect more neonatal hypoglycemia. Do not delay indicated delivery (CO 831).  Betamethasone 12 mg IM ×2, 24 hours apart, or dexamethasone 6 mg IM q12h ×4. Do not shorten the interval.  Rescue: one repeat course if still &lt;34w, delivery again likely within 7 days, and the prior course was &gt;14 days ago (as early as 7 days if the scenario demands).  No weekly serial courses. Rescue with PPROM: insufficient evidence for or against.  \n BOTTOM LINE. 24–33+6 and delivery this week: give the course. Late preterm only if never dosed. Rescue once, not on a calendar. Do not hold an indicated birth for the second injection. "}, {"id": "ANT-07", "kind": "card", "title": "Placenta accreta spectrum (ACOG OCC 7)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Assume it</p>\n<ul><li>Previa plus prior cesarean. Risk climbs with each scar. Cesarean-scar pregnancy is PAS until proven otherwise.</li><li>Ultrasound lacunae and turbulent flow help. A normal scan does not clear her if the history is loud. MRI is not first-line.</li><li>Previable PAS diagnosis: counseling includes termination (OCC 7). Previable PAS or a termination question: alert house supervisor or CMO.</li></ul>\n<p>Planned / stable PAS — do not default to a scheduled hysterectomy at CSM</p>\n<ul><li>Transfer planned/stable PAS to a PAS-experienced level III or IV center. Multidisciplinary team. MTP-capable blood bank. Do <strong>not</strong> start a scheduled PAS hysterectomy at CSM as the default.</li><li>Clock at that center: 34+0 to 35+6 (OCC 7, CO 831); earlier for persistent bleeding, preeclampsia, labor, ROM, fetal compromise, or maternal illness (OCC 7). No amniocentesis for maturity. ACS if delivery before 37w. Do not wait past 36+0.</li><li>Default operation at the PAS center: cesarean hysterectomy, placenta left in situ. Do not attempt to extract the placenta. Conservative management is investigational.</li><li>Routine iliac balloons are not recommended.</li><li>No intrauterine balloon or device with the placenta in situ; do not use one to delay hysterectomy. JADA (house first) or Bakri belongs to PP-01 after a placenta that separated normally.</li></ul>\n<p>Unplanned hemorrhage at CSM</p>\n<ul><li>Found at cesarean before the baby, no bleeding or urgency: pause until surgical expertise arrives. Alert anesthesia (consider general), add IV access, order blood, alert critical care, bring cell salvage. Notify backup physician for possible assistance. No team possible and both stable: stabilize and transfer (OCC 7).</li><li>Placenta will not separate: stop. Do not remove or force the placenta; leave it in situ. Call for help; alert anesthesia. Notify backup physician for possible assistance. Activate MTP (PP-01). Rapid uterine closure, then hysterectomy as resources mobilize; packing, TXA, and transfusion to temporize (OCC 7).</li><li><strong>Keep and finish at CSM if she is unstable.</strong> Transfer only if she is stable <strong>AND</strong> this facility cannot complete the case.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Previa plus scar = assume PAS. Planned/stable: transfer to a PAS-experienced III/IV; clock 34+0 to 35+6 there. Unexpected PAS: pause if no urgency; leave the placenta in situ; notify backup physician for possible assistance; MTP; finish here if unstable. Do not force extraction.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Assume it \n  Previa plus prior cesarean. Risk climbs with each scar. Cesarean-scar pregnancy is PAS until proven otherwise.  Ultrasound lacunae and turbulent flow help. A normal scan does not clear her if the history is loud. MRI is not first-line.  Previable PAS diagnosis: counseling includes termination (OCC 7). Previable PAS or a termination question: alert house supervisor or CMO.  \n Planned / stable PAS — do not default to a scheduled hysterectomy at CSM \n  Transfer planned/stable PAS to a PAS-experienced level III or IV center. Multidisciplinary team. MTP-capable blood bank. Do  not  start a scheduled PAS hysterectomy at CSM as the default.  Clock at that center: 34+0 to 35+6 (OCC 7, CO 831); earlier for persistent bleeding, preeclampsia, labor, ROM, fetal compromise, or maternal illness (OCC 7). No amniocentesis for maturity. ACS if delivery before 37w. Do not wait past 36+0.  Default operation at the PAS center: cesarean hysterectomy, placenta left in situ. Do not attempt to extract the placenta. Conservative management is investigational.  Routine iliac balloons are not recommended.  No intrauterine balloon or device with the placenta in situ; do not use one to delay hysterectomy. JADA (house first) or Bakri belongs to PP-01 after a placenta that separated normally.  \n Unplanned hemorrhage at CSM \n  Found at cesarean before the baby, no bleeding or urgency: pause until surgical expertise arrives. Alert anesthesia (consider general), add IV access, order blood, alert critical care, bring cell salvage. Notify backup physician for possible assistance. No team possible and both stable: stabilize and transfer (OCC 7).  Placenta will not separate: stop. Do not remove or force the placenta; leave it in situ. Call for help; alert anesthesia. Notify backup physician for possible assistance. Activate MTP (PP-01). Rapid uterine closure, then hysterectomy as resources mobilize; packing, TXA, and transfusion to temporize (OCC 7).   Keep and finish at CSM if she is unstable.  Transfer only if she is stable  AND  this facility cannot complete the case.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Previa plus scar = assume PAS. Planned/stable: transfer to a PAS-experienced III/IV; clock 34+0 to 35+6 there. Unexpected PAS: pause if no urgency; leave the placenta in situ; notify backup physician for possible assistance; MTP; finish here if unstable. Do not force extraction. "}, {"id": "ANT-08", "kind": "card", "title": "Hyperemesis in Pregnancy (ACOG PB 189)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Working differential</p>\n<ul><li>Most likely: NVP continuum (PUQE mild ≤6 / moderate 7–12 / severe ≥13). Hyperemesis gravidarum is clinical — no single definition; usual criteria are persistent vomiting not explained by other disease + starvation (often large ketonuria) + weight loss typically ≥5% of prepregnancy weight. Electrolyte / thyroid / liver abnormalities may be present.</li><li>Cannot miss: onset after 9 weeks (rethink the label), appendicitis, cholecystitis / hepatitis, pancreatitis, SBO, PUD, pyelo / DKA, ovarian torsion, molar pregnancy / multifetal, intracranial process, drug toxicity. Abnormal neuro exam is not “just NVP” until Wernicke / CPM are excluded.</li><li>Less likely tonight but live: primary thyroid disease (goiter is not gestational transient thyrotoxicosis), migraine, vestibular disease.</li></ul>\n<p>Confirm and sort</p>\n<ul><li>Timing: NVP almost always starts before 9 weeks. First vomit after 9 weeks → work the differential (Box 1 in PB 189).</li><li>Labs when dehydrating / admitting / severe: CBC, electrolytes, LFTs, UA (ketones), weight vs prepregnancy. Thyroid tests only if goiter or suspected primary disease — gestational transient thyrotoxicosis gets supportive care, <strong>not</strong> antithyroid drugs (Level A).</li><li>Imaging: not routine for classic NVP; use when the differential is live (US for mole / twins / torsion; RUQ US; CT/MRI only with a real neurologic or surgical question).</li></ul>\n<p>Actions — escalate the ladder (Figure 1); do not skip first-line</p>\n<ul><li>Nonpharmacologic first: small frequent meals, avoid triggers, convert prenatal vitamin → folic acid only, ginger 250 mg PO QID, optional P6 wrist bands (evidence mixed).</li><li>First-line pharmacotherapy (Level A): pyridoxine 10–25 mg PO TID–QID <strong>alone or with</strong> doxylamine 12.5 mg PO TID–QID; <strong>or</strong> pyridoxine 10 mg / doxylamine 10 mg combo (up to 4 tabs/day); <strong>or</strong> delayed-release 20/20 (up to 2 tabs/day). Start early — may prevent progression to hyperemesis (Level C).</li><li>Persistent, not dehydrated: add (alpha order) dimenhydrinate 25–50 mg q4–6h (cap 200 mg/day if also on doxylamine), diphenhydramine 25–50 mg q4–6h, prochlorperazine 25 mg PR q12h, or promethazine 12.5–25 mg PO/PR q4–6h.</li><li>Persistent, not dehydrated — next tier: metoclopramide 5–10 mg q6–8h PO/IM, ondansetron 4 mg PO q8h, promethazine PO/PR/IM, or trimethobenzamide 200 mg IM q6–8h.</li><li>Dehydrated: IV fluids. Thiamine <strong>100 mg IV with first bag</strong>, then 100 mg IV daily ×2–3 days + multivitamins if vomiting &gt;3 weeks — before or with dextrose, to prevent Wernicke (Figure 1 footnote). Then IV antiemetics (alpha): dimenhydrinate 50 mg IV q4–6h, metoclopramide 5–10 mg IV q8h, ondansetron 8 mg IV over 15 min q12h (<strong>do not exceed 16 mg IV single dose</strong> — QT / torsade), or promethazine 12.5–25 mg IV q4–6h.</li><li>Refractory: chlorpromazine <strong>or</strong> methylprednisolone 16 mg PO/IV q8h ×3 days, taper over ~2 weeks to lowest effective dose, <strong>total steroid course ≤6 weeks</strong> (Level B — last resort; avoid &lt;10 weeks — cleft signal).</li><li>Nutrition: if still losing weight / dehydrated despite meds → <strong>enteral</strong> (NG/ND) first-line. TPN only if enteral fails. <strong>No routine PICC</strong> — last resort only (Level C).</li></ul>\n<p>Traps</p>\n<ul><li>Calling gestational transient thyrotoxicosis “Graves” and starting PTU/methimazole.</li><li>Ondansetron as first step, or stacking ondansetron + phenothiazines / QT drugs (Box 2). Counsel first-trimester ondansetron: data inconsistent; absolute risk small; document shared decision.</li><li>Dopamine antagonist + phenothiazine → EPS / NMS risk.</li><li>Glucose before thiamine in prolonged vomiting.</li><li>Subcutaneous metoclopramide/ondansetron pumps as default — not cost-effective vs intermittent care + admit when needed.</li><li>Psychologizing severe NVP; withholding treatment because “morning sickness is normal.”</li><li>Managing mole or surgical abdomen as hyperemesis.</li></ul>\n<p>Who stays / consults</p>\n<ul><li>Admit: cannot keep down liquids, clinical dehydration, electrolyte derangement, weight loss with starvation, failed outpatient ladder, unreliable follow-up, or Wernicke concern.</li><li>MFM / nutrition: refractory hyperemesis needing tube feeds or TPN consideration.</li><li>Medicine / neurology: focal neuro findings, suspected Wernicke, arrhythmia on QT-prolonging drugs.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Onset after 9 weeks is not NVP until proven. Pyridoxine ± doxylamine first (Level A). Escalate Figure 1; thiamine before dextrose if vomiting &gt;3 weeks. Enteral before TPN. No routine PICC. No antithyroid drugs for transient gestational thyrotoxicosis.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Working differential \n  Most likely: NVP continuum (PUQE mild ≤6 / moderate 7–12 / severe ≥13). Hyperemesis gravidarum is clinical — no single definition; usual criteria are persistent vomiting not explained by other disease + starvation (often large ketonuria) + weight loss typically ≥5% of prepregnancy weight. Electrolyte / thyroid / liver abnormalities may be present.  Cannot miss: onset after 9 weeks (rethink the label), appendicitis, cholecystitis / hepatitis, pancreatitis, SBO, PUD, pyelo / DKA, ovarian torsion, molar pregnancy / multifetal, intracranial process, drug toxicity. Abnormal neuro exam is not “just NVP” until Wernicke / CPM are excluded.  Less likely tonight but live: primary thyroid disease (goiter is not gestational transient thyrotoxicosis), migraine, vestibular disease.  \n Confirm and sort \n  Timing: NVP almost always starts before 9 weeks. First vomit after 9 weeks → work the differential (Box 1 in PB 189).  Labs when dehydrating / admitting / severe: CBC, electrolytes, LFTs, UA (ketones), weight vs prepregnancy. Thyroid tests only if goiter or suspected primary disease — gestational transient thyrotoxicosis gets supportive care,  not  antithyroid drugs (Level A).  Imaging: not routine for classic NVP; use when the differential is live (US for mole / twins / torsion; RUQ US; CT/MRI only with a real neurologic or surgical question).  \n Actions — escalate the ladder (Figure 1); do not skip first-line \n  Nonpharmacologic first: small frequent meals, avoid triggers, convert prenatal vitamin → folic acid only, ginger 250 mg PO QID, optional P6 wrist bands (evidence mixed).  First-line pharmacotherapy (Level A): pyridoxine 10–25 mg PO TID–QID  alone or with  doxylamine 12.5 mg PO TID–QID;  or  pyridoxine 10 mg / doxylamine 10 mg combo (up to 4 tabs/day);  or  delayed-release 20/20 (up to 2 tabs/day). Start early — may prevent progression to hyperemesis (Level C).  Persistent, not dehydrated: add (alpha order) dimenhydrinate 25–50 mg q4–6h (cap 200 mg/day if also on doxylamine), diphenhydramine 25–50 mg q4–6h, prochlorperazine 25 mg PR q12h, or promethazine 12.5–25 mg PO/PR q4–6h.  Persistent, not dehydrated — next tier: metoclopramide 5–10 mg q6–8h PO/IM, ondansetron 4 mg PO q8h, promethazine PO/PR/IM, or trimethobenzamide 200 mg IM q6–8h.  Dehydrated: IV fluids. Thiamine  100 mg IV with first bag , then 100 mg IV daily ×2–3 days + multivitamins if vomiting &gt;3 weeks — before or with dextrose, to prevent Wernicke (Figure 1 footnote). Then IV antiemetics (alpha): dimenhydrinate 50 mg IV q4–6h, metoclopramide 5–10 mg IV q8h, ondansetron 8 mg IV over 15 min q12h ( do not exceed 16 mg IV single dose  — QT / torsade), or promethazine 12.5–25 mg IV q4–6h.  Refractory: chlorpromazine  or  methylprednisolone 16 mg PO/IV q8h ×3 days, taper over ~2 weeks to lowest effective dose,  total steroid course ≤6 weeks  (Level B — last resort; avoid <10 weeks — cleft signal).  Nutrition: if still losing weight / dehydrated despite meds →  enteral  (NG/ND) first-line. TPN only if enteral fails.  No routine PICC  — last resort only (Level C).  \n Traps \n  Calling gestational transient thyrotoxicosis “Graves” and starting PTU/methimazole.  Ondansetron as first step, or stacking ondansetron + phenothiazines / QT drugs (Box 2). Counsel first-trimester ondansetron: data inconsistent; absolute risk small; document shared decision.  Dopamine antagonist + phenothiazine → EPS / NMS risk.  Glucose before thiamine in prolonged vomiting.  Subcutaneous metoclopramide/ondansetron pumps as default — not cost-effective vs intermittent care + admit when needed.  Psychologizing severe NVP; withholding treatment because “morning sickness is normal.”  Managing mole or surgical abdomen as hyperemesis.  \n Who stays / consults \n  Admit: cannot keep down liquids, clinical dehydration, electrolyte derangement, weight loss with starvation, failed outpatient ladder, unreliable follow-up, or Wernicke concern.  MFM / nutrition: refractory hyperemesis needing tube feeds or TPN consideration.  Medicine / neurology: focal neuro findings, suspected Wernicke, arrhythmia on QT-prolonging drugs.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Onset after 9 weeks is not NVP until proven. Pyridoxine ± doxylamine first (Level A). Escalate Figure 1; thiamine before dextrose if vomiting &gt;3 weeks. Enteral before TPN. No routine PICC. No antithyroid drugs for transient gestational thyrotoxicosis. "}, {"id": "ANT-09", "kind": "card", "title": "Outpatient antenatal surveillance (ACOG CO 828)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Start when stillbirth risk is high enough that twice-weekly NST / BPP is more useful than additional visits. Classic starts: FGR, preeclampsia, pregestational diabetes, A2 GDM, cholestasis, late term, decreased fetal movement, APS, cyanotic cardiac disease.</li><li>Initiation week is condition-specific (often 32–34w for many maternal conditions; earlier for FGR, hydrops, or poorly controlled diabetes).</li><li>Most indicated surveillance is twice weekly. Do not freelance a weekly plan on preeclampsia or FGR.</li><li>A reactive NST or BPP 8/8 is reassuring for that sitting. It is not a 7-day pass if the mother is worsening.</li><li>Oligohydramnios, recurrent late decelerations, or BPP ≤4 → come in.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Match the indication to a start week and a twice-weekly default. A reactive NST does not cancel a new symptom.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Start when stillbirth risk is high enough that twice-weekly NST / BPP is more useful than additional visits. Classic starts: FGR, preeclampsia, pregestational diabetes, A2 GDM, cholestasis, late term, decreased fetal movement, APS, cyanotic cardiac disease.  Initiation week is condition-specific (often 32–34w for many maternal conditions; earlier for FGR, hydrops, or poorly controlled diabetes).  Most indicated surveillance is twice weekly. Do not freelance a weekly plan on preeclampsia or FGR.  A reactive NST or BPP 8/8 is reassuring for that sitting. It is not a 7-day pass if the mother is worsening.  Oligohydramnios, recurrent late decelerations, or BPP ≤4 → come in.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Match the indication to a start week and a twice-weekly default. A reactive NST does not cancel a new symptom. "}, {"id": "ANT-11", "kind": "card", "title": "Eclamptic seizure (PB 222 + CO 767)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md", "html": "<p>During the seizure: ABCs first</p>\n<ul><li>Call for help. Prevent injury (padded rails; nothing in the mouth). Left lateral position, suction, prevent aspiration. Oxygen; SpO2, BP, and HR (PB 222).</li><li>Most eclamptic seizures are self-limited. Magnesium does not stop the seizure; it prevents the next one (PB 222). Start it as soon as she is safe.</li><li><strong>Do not deliver during a seizure.</strong> Prolonged decelerations or bradycardia are expected; maternal resuscitation usually normalizes the tracing. Deliver only after maternal stabilization (PB 222).</li></ul>\n<p>Magnesium (house regimen, formulary + ANT-04)</p>\n<ul><li>Magnesium sulfate seizure: 4–6 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1–2 g/h. 5 g IM each buttock if no IV. Continue 24h after birth / last seizure.</li><li>No IV: 10 g IM (5 g each buttock), then 5 g IM q4h (PB 222).</li><li>Creatinine 1.0–1.5 mg/dL or urine &lt;30 mL/h for &gt;4 h: maintenance 1 g/h, check levels (PB 222: q4h).</li><li>Recurrent seizure on magnesium: a further 2–4 g IV over 5 min (PB 222).</li><li>Refractory (still seizing 20 min after the bolus, or more than two recurrences): CSM agents: diazepam 5–10 mg IV, lorazepam 4 mg IV (both repeatable in 10–15 min), and phenytoin load 15–20 mg/kg IV (typical ~1000 mg) at ≤50 mg/min on a cardiac monitor; maintenance per pharmacy (historic regimen 500 mg PO ~10 h later). Intubate if needed; ICU; head imaging.</li><li>Benzodiazepines with magnesium: risk of respiratory depression; airway ready.</li><li>Extreme agitation only, to place lines: clonazepam 1 mg IV, diazepam 10 mg IV, or midazolam, cautiously; they blunt laryngeal reflexes and can cause apnea (PB 222).</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Toxicity watch</p>\n<ul><li>Reflexes, respiratory rate, SpO2, and urine output (Foley, strict I/O). Reflexes lost at about 9 mg/dL, respiratory depression at 12, cardiac arrest at 30 (PB 222).</li><li>Mg toxicity: loss of reflexes → stop. Calcium gluconate 1 g IV over 3 min. Check creatinine; lower infusion if oliguric.</li><li>Respiratory depression: airway and ventilation. No pulse: SYS-04 (stop magnesium; calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL, arrest dose).</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Magnesium contraindicated or unavailable</p>\n<ul><li>Myasthenia gravis, hypocalcemia, moderate-to-severe renal failure, cardiac ischemia, heart block, or myocarditis: a benzodiazepine or phenytoin (PB 222). PB 222 gives no first-line dose for this use; agent and dose with pharmacy and neurology.</li></ul>\n<p>Blood pressure (house ladders, CO 767)</p>\n<ul><li>Treat SBP ≥160 or DBP ≥110 confirmed on a recheck within 15 min (persistent 15 min or more = hypertensive emergency). Give the first-line agent within 30 to 60 min <strong>of confirmation</strong>, as soon as possible.</li><li><strong>First-line: any of three, equally appropriate (CO 767).</strong> Climb one ladder; stop as soon as BP is below threshold.</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Labetalol IV</strong> (Box 3): 20 mg over 2 min → recheck 10 min → 40 mg → 10 min → 80 mg → 10 min → switch to hydralazine 10 mg IV → 20 min → still severe: emergency consult. Max cumulative 300 mg (PB 222); CMQCC 220 mg; use pharmacy cap. Avoid asthma, heart disease or CHF, heart block, bradycardia.</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Hydralazine IV</strong> (Box 2): 5 or 10 mg over 2 min → recheck 20 min → 10 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV → 10 min → labetalol 40 mg IV and emergency consult. Max 20 mg (PB 222).</li><li class=\"sub\"><strong>Nifedipine IR PO</strong> (Box 1): 10 mg → recheck 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV and emergency consult. Swallow whole; never SL or punctured. Max 180 mg/day (PB 222).</li><li>After a seizure, oral nifedipine only once she is awake and can swallow safely; otherwise an IV ladder. Magnesium is not an antihypertensive: the BP clock keeps running.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Deliver after stabilization</p>\n<ul><li>Eclampsia precludes expectant management at any gestational age; deliver after maternal stabilization (PB 222 Box 4). It is not by itself an indication for cesarean.</li><li>Route by gestational age, presentation, and cervix. Before 30 weeks, not in labor, unfavorable Bishop: cesarean may be preferable. Laboring well: labor may continue (PB 222).</li><li>Before 34 weeks: give steroids (ANT-06), but do not hold a needed delivery to finish the course (PB 222).</li><li>Magnesium continues during cesarean and for 24 h after. General anesthesia: control the hypertensive response to intubation.</li><li>Labs: CBC with platelets, creatinine, AST/ALT, LDH, coagulation panel if bleeding or low platelets (HELLP, abruption, DIC).</li><li>Think again: seizure more than 48–72 h postpartum, a focal deficit, or seizure on magnesium → image for PRES, stroke, or venous thrombosis (PB 222).</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Airway, left side, oxygen; do not deliver mid-seizure. House magnesium load and maintenance; 2–4 g IV for a recurrence. Lost reflexes: stop, calcium gluconate 1 g. Severe BP: one CO 767 ladder within 60 minutes of confirmation. Stabilize the mother, then deliver.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " During the seizure: ABCs first \n   Call for help. Prevent injury (padded rails; nothing in the mouth). Left lateral position, suction, prevent aspiration. Oxygen; SpO2, BP, and HR (PB 222).   Most eclamptic seizures are self-limited. Magnesium does not stop the seizure; it prevents the next one (PB 222). Start it as soon as she is safe.    Do not deliver during a seizure.  Prolonged decelerations or bradycardia are expected; maternal resuscitation usually normalizes the tracing. Deliver only after maternal stabilization (PB 222).  \n Magnesium (house regimen, formulary + ANT-04) \n   Magnesium sulfate seizure: 4–6 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1–2 g/h. 5 g IM each buttock if no IV. Continue 24h after birth / last seizure.   No IV: 10 g IM (5 g each buttock), then 5 g IM q4h (PB 222).   Creatinine 1.0–1.5 mg/dL or urine <30 mL/h for >4 h: maintenance 1 g/h, check levels (PB 222: q4h).   Recurrent seizure on magnesium: a further 2–4 g IV over 5 min (PB 222).   Refractory (still seizing 20 min after the bolus, or more than two recurrences): CSM agents: diazepam 5–10 mg IV, lorazepam 4 mg IV (both repeatable in 10–15 min), and phenytoin load 15–20 mg/kg IV (typical ~1000 mg) at ≤50 mg/min on a cardiac monitor; maintenance per pharmacy (historic regimen 500 mg PO ~10 h later). Intubate if needed; ICU; head imaging.   Benzodiazepines with magnesium: risk of respiratory depression; airway ready.   Extreme agitation only, to place lines: clonazepam 1 mg IV, diazepam 10 mg IV, or midazolam, cautiously; they blunt laryngeal reflexes and can cause apnea (PB 222).   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Toxicity watch \n   Reflexes, respiratory rate, SpO2, and urine output (Foley, strict I/O). Reflexes lost at about 9 mg/dL, respiratory depression at 12, cardiac arrest at 30 (PB 222).   Mg toxicity: loss of reflexes → stop. Calcium gluconate 1 g IV over 3 min. Check creatinine; lower infusion if oliguric.   Respiratory depression: airway and ventilation. No pulse: SYS-04 (stop magnesium; calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL, arrest dose).   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Magnesium contraindicated or unavailable \n   Myasthenia gravis, hypocalcemia, moderate-to-severe renal failure, cardiac ischemia, heart block, or myocarditis: a benzodiazepine or phenytoin (PB 222). PB 222 gives no first-line dose for this use; agent and dose with pharmacy and neurology.  \n Blood pressure (house ladders, CO 767) \n   Treat SBP ≥160 or DBP ≥110 confirmed on a recheck within 15 min (persistent 15 min or more = hypertensive emergency). Give the first-line agent within 30 to 60 min  of confirmation , as soon as possible.    First-line: any of three, equally appropriate (CO 767).  Climb one ladder; stop as soon as BP is below threshold.    Labetalol IV  (Box 3): 20 mg over 2 min → recheck 10 min → 40 mg → 10 min → 80 mg → 10 min → switch to hydralazine 10 mg IV → 20 min → still severe: emergency consult. Max cumulative 300 mg (PB 222); CMQCC 220 mg; use pharmacy cap. Avoid asthma, heart disease or CHF, heart block, bradycardia.    Hydralazine IV  (Box 2): 5 or 10 mg over 2 min → recheck 20 min → 10 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV → 10 min → labetalol 40 mg IV and emergency consult. Max 20 mg (PB 222).    Nifedipine IR PO  (Box 1): 10 mg → recheck 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → 20 mg → 20 min → switch to labetalol 20 mg IV and emergency consult. Swallow whole; never SL or punctured. Max 180 mg/day (PB 222).   After a seizure, oral nifedipine only once she is awake and can swallow safely; otherwise an IV ladder. Magnesium is not an antihypertensive: the BP clock keeps running.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Deliver after stabilization \n   Eclampsia precludes expectant management at any gestational age; deliver after maternal stabilization (PB 222 Box 4). It is not by itself an indication for cesarean.   Route by gestational age, presentation, and cervix. Before 30 weeks, not in labor, unfavorable Bishop: cesarean may be preferable. Laboring well: labor may continue (PB 222).   Before 34 weeks: give steroids (ANT-06), but do not hold a needed delivery to finish the course (PB 222).   Magnesium continues during cesarean and for 24 h after. General anesthesia: control the hypertensive response to intubation.   Labs: CBC with platelets, creatinine, AST/ALT, LDH, coagulation panel if bleeding or low platelets (HELLP, abruption, DIC).   Think again: seizure more than 48–72 h postpartum, a focal deficit, or seizure on magnesium → image for PRES, stroke, or venous thrombosis (PB 222).  \n BOTTOM LINE. Airway, left side, oxygen; do not deliver mid-seizure. House magnesium load and maintenance; 2–4 g IV for a recurrence. Lost reflexes: stop, calcium gluconate 1 g. Severe BP: one CO 767 ladder within 60 minutes of confirmation. Stabilize the mother, then deliver. "}, {"id": "ANT-TRAUMA", "kind": "card", "title": "Trauma in pregnancy (fall, MVC, blunt abdominal injury)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/drafts/ANT-TRAUMA_trauma_in_pregnancy_DRAFT_2026-11-13.md", "html": "<p><strong>PROVISIONAL</strong> — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.</p>\n<p>Scope: OBED evaluation of a pregnant patient after minor-to-moderate blunt trauma (fall, MVC, assault, direct abdominal blow). Major trauma = ED/trauma team primary; OB consults. Excludes penetrating trauma, burns, and electrical injury (consult only).</p>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mother first: primary survey (ABCDE) before the fetus. At ≥20 weeks, displace the uterus to the left / left tilt if supine.</li>\n<li>Viable gestation (≥22–24 wk per local viability policy): continuous EFM + toco for at least 4–6 h after the event.</li>\n<li>Extend monitoring to 24 h if ANY: contractions ≥1 per 10 min (≥6/h), vaginal bleeding, uterine tenderness or pain, ROM, abnormal FHR (category II/III), or a serious maternal mechanism or injury.</li>\n<li>Ultrasound misses most abruptions; a normal scan does NOT rule one out. Monitoring is the test.</li>\n<li>Every pregnant trauma patient gets a private IPV screen.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>RED FLAGS → CALL OB ATTENDING NOW / TRAUMA ACTIVATION</p>\n<p>Maternal instability (SBP &lt;90, HR &gt;120, altered mental status), category III tracing or persistent fetal bradycardia, heavy vaginal bleeding, rigid or tender uterus, suspected uterine rupture, high-energy mechanism (high-speed MVC, ejection, pedestrian struck, fall from height).</p>\n<p>WORKUP</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Vitals; primary and secondary survey; document mechanism and time.</li>\n<li>Labs: CBC, type and screen. If abruption suspected: PT/INR, PTT, fibrinogen (fibrinogen &lt;200 mg/dL in pregnancy is concerning for abruption/DIC).</li>\n<li>Rh-negative: Kleihauer-Betke (or flow cytometry) to quantify fetomaternal hemorrhage; RhIG within 72 h regardless (see doses).</li>\n<li>Ultrasound: viability, FHR, GA/growth, placental location (rule out previa before any digital exam), fluid.</li>\n<li>Exam: fundal height and tenderness; speculum exam for bleeding/ROM; digital cervical exam only after previa is excluded.</li>\n<li>Imaging: do not withhold indicated maternal imaging (including CT) because of pregnancy; fetal dose from a standard trauma CT is well below the threshold for harm. Shield is not required.</li>\n<li>Tetanus: Tdap if indicated by wound/immunization status (Tdap is safe in pregnancy).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>DISPOSITION</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Home: 4–6 h of reassuring monitoring (category I, contractions &lt;1/10 min, no bleeding, tenderness or ROM), stable maternal exam. Give abruption precautions (bleeding, contractions, abdominal pain, fluid leak, decreased fetal movement) and clinic follow-up.</li>\n<li>Observe/admit: any extended-monitoring criterion above, abnormal labs, or maternal injury needing admission.</li>\n<li>Previable (&lt;22–24 wk): document FHT by Doppler/US; maternal evaluation drives disposition; Rh status still applies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>IF ABRUPTION OR PRETERM LABOR SUSPECTED</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Deliver per maternal/fetal status; follow ANT-02 (preterm labor) and hemorrhage cards as applicable.</li>\n<li>Antenatal corticosteroids if 24w0d–33w6d and delivery likely within 7 days (late-preterm per house ANT card).</li>\n<li>Magnesium for fetal neuroprotection if delivery imminent &lt;32 wk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>DOSES (to be checked against CSM pharmacy build)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>RhIG 300 mcg IM within 72 h (covers ~30 mL fetal whole blood); additional vials per KB calculation.</li>\n<li>Betamethasone 12 mg IM q24h x 2.</li>\n<li>Magnesium sulfate (neuroprotection) 4 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1 g/h.</li>\n<li>Tdap 0.5 mL IM if indicated.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>CODING (draft — verify with OBHG coding)</p>\n<p>O9A.21x (injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes complicating pregnancy; 1=1st, 2=2nd, 3=3rd trimester) + specific injury code (e.g., S39.91XA unspecified abdominal injury) + external cause (e.g., W19.XXXA fall) + Z3A.xx. OBED 99283–99285 by MDM; prolonged monitoring does not by itself change the E/M level.</p>\n<p class=\"pearl\">TEACHING PEARL. Abruption after trauma can show up hours later. The tocodynamometer is more sensitive than ultrasound: frequent contractions in the first 4 hours are the strongest predictor. \"Reassuring scan\" never shortens the monitoring window.</p>\n<p>SOURCES (verify citation numbers before publishing)</p>\n<ul>\n<li>SOGC Clinical Practice Guideline No. 325: Guidelines for the Management of a Pregnant Trauma Patient (Jain V, et al. J Obstet Gynaecol Can 2015;37(6):553–571).</li>\n<li>EAST Practice Management Guideline: Practice Management Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Injury in the Pregnant Patient (Barraco RD, et al. J Trauma 2010;69(1):211–214).</li>\n<li>ACOG Committee Opinion 723: Guidelines for Diagnostic Imaging During Pregnancy and Lactation (2017, reaffirmed).</li>\n<li>ACOG Practice Bulletin 181: Prevention of Rh D Alloimmunization (2017).</li>\n<li>ACOG Committee Opinion 518: Intimate Partner Violence (2012, reaffirmed).</li>\n<li>ACS ATLS, 10th ed., Trauma in Pregnancy chapter.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Evidence: largely observational/consensus (low–moderate); 4–6 h minimum monitoring is consensus across SOGC/EAST.</p>\n", "flags": ["PROVISIONAL — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.", "Pharmacy check needed for RhIG, betamethasone, magnesium sulfate (Mg), and Tdap doses."], "text": "PROVISIONAL\n — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.\nScope: OBED evaluation of a pregnant patient after minor-to-moderate blunt trauma (fall, MVC, assault, direct abdominal blow). Major trauma = ED/trauma team primary; OB consults. Excludes penetrating trauma, burns, and electrical injury (consult only).\nBOTTOM LINE\n• Mother first: primary survey (ABCDE) before the fetus. At ≥20 weeks, displace the uterus to the left / left tilt if supine.\n• Viable gestation (≥22–24 wk per local viability policy): continuous EFM + toco for at least 4–6 h after the event.\n• Extend monitoring to 24 h if ANY: contractions ≥1 per 10 min (≥6/h), vaginal bleeding, uterine tenderness or pain, ROM, abnormal FHR (category II/III), or a serious maternal mechanism or injury.\n• Ultrasound misses most abruptions; a normal scan does NOT rule one out. Monitoring is the test.\n• Every pregnant trauma patient gets a private IPV screen.\nRED FLAGS → CALL OB ATTENDING NOW / TRAUMA ACTIVATION\nMaternal instability (SBP <90, HR >120, altered mental status), category III tracing or persistent fetal bradycardia, heavy vaginal bleeding, rigid or tender uterus, suspected uterine rupture, high-energy mechanism (high-speed MVC, ejection, pedestrian struck, fall from height).\nWORKUP\n• Vitals; primary and secondary survey; document mechanism and time.\n• Labs: CBC, type and screen. If abruption suspected: PT/INR, PTT, fibrinogen (fibrinogen <200 mg/dL in pregnancy is concerning for abruption/DIC).\n• Rh-negative: Kleihauer-Betke (or flow cytometry) to quantify fetomaternal hemorrhage; RhIG within 72 h regardless (see doses).\n• Ultrasound: viability, FHR, GA/growth, placental location (rule out previa before any digital exam), fluid.\n• Exam: fundal height and tenderness; speculum exam for bleeding/ROM; digital cervical exam only after previa is excluded.\n• Imaging: do not withhold indicated maternal imaging (including CT) because of pregnancy; fetal dose from a standard trauma CT is well below the threshold for harm. Shield is not required.\n• Tetanus: Tdap if indicated by wound/immunization status (Tdap is safe in pregnancy).\nDISPOSITION\n• Home: 4–6 h of reassuring monitoring (category I, contractions <1/10 min, no bleeding, tenderness or ROM), stable maternal exam. Give abruption precautions (bleeding, contractions, abdominal pain, fluid leak, decreased fetal movement) and clinic follow-up.\n• Observe/admit: any extended-monitoring criterion above, abnormal labs, or maternal injury needing admission.\n• Previable (<22–24 wk): document FHT by Doppler/US; maternal evaluation drives disposition; Rh status still applies.\nIF ABRUPTION OR PRETERM LABOR SUSPECTED\n• Deliver per maternal/fetal status; follow ANT-02 (preterm labor) and hemorrhage cards as applicable.\n• Antenatal corticosteroids if 24w0d–33w6d and delivery likely within 7 days (late-preterm per house ANT card).\n• Magnesium for fetal neuroprotection if delivery imminent <32 wk.\nDOSES (to be checked against CSM pharmacy build)\n• RhIG 300 mcg IM within 72 h (covers ~30 mL fetal whole blood); additional vials per KB calculation.\n• Betamethasone 12 mg IM q24h x 2.\n• Magnesium sulfate (neuroprotection) 4 g IV load over 20–30 min, then 1 g/h.\n• Tdap 0.5 mL IM if indicated.\nCODING (draft — verify with OBHG coding)\nO9A.21x (injury, poisoning and certain other consequences of external causes complicating pregnancy; 1=1st, 2=2nd, 3=3rd trimester) + specific injury code (e.g., S39.91XA unspecified abdominal injury) + external cause (e.g., W19.XXXA fall) + Z3A.xx. OBED 99283–99285 by MDM; prolonged monitoring does not by itself change the E/M level.\nTEACHING PEARL. Abruption after trauma can show up hours later. The tocodynamometer is more sensitive than ultrasound: frequent contractions in the first 4 hours are the strongest predictor. \"Reassuring scan\" never shortens the monitoring window.\nSOURCES (verify citation numbers before publishing)\n• SOGC Clinical Practice Guideline No. 325: Guidelines for the Management of a Pregnant Trauma Patient (Jain V, et al. J Obstet Gynaecol Can 2015;37(6):553–571).\n• EAST Practice Management Guideline: Practice Management Guidelines for the Diagnosis and Management of Injury in the Pregnant Patient (Barraco RD, et al. J Trauma 2010;69(1):211–214).\n• ACOG Committee Opinion 723: Guidelines for Diagnostic Imaging During Pregnancy and Lactation (2017, reaffirmed).\n• ACOG Practice Bulletin 181: Prevention of Rh D Alloimmunization (2017).\n• ACOG Committee Opinion 518: Intimate Partner Violence (2012, reaffirmed).\n• ACS ATLS, 10th ed., Trauma in Pregnancy chapter.\nEvidence: largely observational/consensus (low–moderate); 4–6 h minimum monitoring is consensus across SOGC/EAST.", "lastReviewed": "Provisional — Nov 2026 (not yet reviewed)"}, {"id": "ANT-STI", "kind": "card", "title": "STIs on Labor and Delivery (syphilis, HSV, GC/CT, trich, BV, VVC)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/drafts/ANT-STI_STIs_on_labor_and_delivery_DRAFT_2026-11-13.md", "html": "<p><strong>PROVISIONAL</strong> — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.</p>\n<p>Scope: L&amp;D/OBED admission of a pregnant patient (labor, ROM, induction, triage) with known, suspected or UNKNOWN status for syphilis, genital HSV, gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, BV or vulvovaginal candidiasis. Brief \"also check at admission\" section for HIV and hepatitis B (detailed management = separate cards/HHS guidance). Excludes nonpregnant GYN STI care, PID, and neonatal treatment beyond the handoff (peds/Red Book owns the newborn).</p>\n<p><strong>ADMISSION \"UNKNOWN STATUS\" CHECKLIST (every L&amp;D admission)</strong></p>\n<table class=\"ctab\">\n<thead><tr><th>Check</th><th>If missing / unknown at admission</th><th>Law / guideline</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Syphilis (1st visit, ≥28 wk, delivery)</td><td>Draw RPR (or lab's reverse-algorithm screen) ON ADMISSION for every delivery. No mother or newborn leaves without maternal syphilis status documented.</td><td>Texas HSC §81.090(c) (delivery test required); ACOG 2024; CDC 2021</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HIV (1st visit + 3rd trimester ≥28 wk)</td><td>No 3rd-trimester result in chart → EXPEDITED HIV test now (result &lt;6 h).</td><td>Texas HSC §81.090(c-1)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Hepatitis B (HBsAg)</td><td>Draw HBsAg on admission for every delivery.</td><td>Texas HSC §81.090(c)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GC/CT NAAT</td><td>Not done in pregnancy AND at risk (&lt;25 y, new/multiple partners, partner with STI, no prenatal care) → NAAT (vaginal swab or urine) now; peds told result is pending.</td><td>CDC 2021</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HSV history</td><td>Ask EVERY patient about genital HSV and prodrome (burning, tingling, vulvar pain); examine vulva/perineum/cervix.</td><td>CDC 2021; ACOG PB 220</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Partner treated?</td><td>Ask for GC/CT/trich/syphilis diagnoses this pregnancy — reinfection is the usual failure.</td><td>CDC 2021</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE</p>\n<ul><li>Only TWO things on this card change the WAY she delivers: active genital HSV lesions or prodrome at labor/ROM (→ cesarean), and HIV with viral load &gt;1,000 or unknown (→ IV ZDV ± cesarean; separate card). Nothing else here is an indication for cesarean.</li><li>Syphilis does not change mode of delivery, but it is the highest-stakes item: status must be known before mother or baby goes home, and every untreated/undocumented case is treated with benzathine penicillin G NOW (no alternatives in pregnancy; desensitize if allergic).</li><li>Untreated GC/CT at delivery: treat mother, tell peds before birth if possible (untreated maternal GC → newborn ceftriaxone). Erythromycin eye ointment prevents gonococcal, NOT chlamydial, ophthalmia.</li><li>Trich, BV, VVC: treat if symptomatic; none of them changes intrapartum management or should delay delivery. Treatment can be finished postpartum.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>AT A GLANCE — CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT vs CAN WAIT</strong></p>\n<table class=\"ctab\">\n<thead><tr><th>Infection</th><th>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT</th><th>CAN WAIT UNTIL POSTPARTUM</th><th>PEDS HANDOFF</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Syphilis</td><td>Admission serology is mandatory; treat untreated/undocumented NOW (do not wait for delivery). Mode of delivery unchanged.</td><td>Titer follow-up, partner services, health dept report (do not delay).</td><td>ALWAYS: maternal RPR titer at delivery, treatment dates/drug/stage, whether started ≥30 d before delivery.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>HSV</td><td>YES: active genital lesions or prodrome at labor/ROM → cesarean.</td><td>Suppression counseling for next pregnancy.</td><td>YES if lesions at delivery, first-episode 3rd trimester, or vaginal birth with lesions.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>GC</td><td>Treat if positive/untreated (ceftriaxone). Mode unchanged.</td><td>Retest 3 mo; EPT for partner.</td><td>YES if untreated/positive → neonatal ceftriaxone.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>CT</td><td>Treat if positive/untreated (azithromycin). Mode unchanged.</td><td>Test of cure ~4 wk; retest 3 mo; EPT.</td><td>YES if untreated/positive → monitor infant (no presumptive treatment).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Trich</td><td>No.</td><td>Yes — treat any time incl. postpartum.</td><td>Not routinely.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>BV</td><td>No; not a reason to delay delivery.</td><td>Yes (symptomatic only).</td><td>No.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>VVC</td><td>No.</td><td>Yes; fluconazole only after delivery.</td><td>No (thrush follow-up routine).</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p><strong>1. SYPHILIS</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: serology on admission (all deliveries — Texas law); treatment of untreated/undocumented syphilis is started on L&amp;D, not deferred. MODE OF DELIVERY: unchanged. CAN WAIT: titer follow-up and partner services (but report and refer before discharge).</p>\n<ol><li>Screening in Texas: syphilis at first visit, third trimester (not earlier than 28 wk) and on admission for delivery (HSC §81.090). ACOG (2024, reaffirmed Oct 2025) also endorses universal 3-time screening. CDC 2021 text is risk-based for 28 wk/delivery — state law and ACOG govern here.</li><li>Know your lab's algorithm:<ul><li>Traditional: nontreponemal (RPR/VDRL) first → reactive → treponemal confirm (TP-PA, EIA/CIA).</li><li>Reverse-sequence: treponemal immunoassay (EIA/CIA) first → reactive → reflex quantitative RPR. Treponemal tests stay positive for life in most patients, so a reactive EIA/CIA cannot distinguish old treated from current infection.</li></ul></li><li>Discordant results:<ul><li>EIA/CIA + / RPR − → second, different treponemal test (TP-PA preferred), same specimen if possible.<ul><li>TP-PA + → current or past syphilis. Adequately treated before, no new risk → no treatment. No documented treatment → stage and treat.</li><li>TP-PA − → probable false-positive EIA/CIA in a low-risk patient; repeat serology within 4 wk can be considered if follow-up is reliable. If follow-up unlikely → treat by stage.</li></ul></li><li>Prior DOCUMENTED positive treponemal test, negative treponemal test today → do not use today's result to clear her. Ask lab which assay; repeat with a different treponemal method (e.g., TP-PA if today's was EIA/CIA); repeat quantitative RPR; get treatment records (prior clinic, outside lab, Texas DSHS/local health department). If adequate treatment cannot be documented → treat as latent of unknown duration. (Expert extrapolation: CDC 2021 states seropositive pregnant women are \"considered infected unless an adequate treatment history is clearly documented\"; it does not specifically address treponemal seroreversion.)</li><li>Rapid treponemal test only at delivery (late/no prenatal care) → confirm with lab RPR + treponemal; do not delay newborn evaluation.</li></ul></li><li>Staging (CDC): primary (chancre), secondary (rash incl. palms/soles, condyloma lata, mucous patches), early latent (infection within the past 12 months, documented), late latent (&gt;12 months) or latent of unknown duration (most L&amp;D cases). Neurologic/ocular/otic signs → neurosyphilis pathway, ID consult.</li><li>Treatment (benzathine penicillin G only — see DOSES):<ul><li>Primary, secondary, early latent: 2.4 MU IM x1. In pregnancy a second 2.4 MU dose 1 wk later CAN be given (CDC 2021).</li><li>Late latent / unknown duration: 2.4 MU IM weekly x3 (7.2 MU total). Optimal interval 7 days; &gt;9 days between doses is NOT acceptable in pregnancy → restart the full series. If she misses day 7, contact and treat within 2 days.</li><li>Penicillin allergy → penicillin skin test / graded challenge and desensitization, then penicillin. NO acceptable alternative in pregnancy (doxycycline/tetracycline avoided 2nd–3rd trimester; azithro/erythromycin don't treat the fetus; ceftriaxone data insufficient).</li><li>Bicillin L-A supply: national shortage ongoing; FDA has allowed temporary importation of Lentocilin (benzathine benzylpenicillin, 1.2 MU vials — product, diluent and volume differ; pharmacy prepares). CDC asks that benzathine penicillin be reserved for pregnant patients and infants. NEVER substitute Bicillin C-R (benzathine-procaine).</li></ul></li><li>Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, myalgia, headache within 24 h; most often early syphilis): at &gt;20 wk can cause contractions, decreased FM, or fetal heart rate changes. Practical L&amp;D plan: give the dose with the patient on L&amp;D or with clear return precautions; continuous/extended EFM if viable and in labor or otherwise monitored; antipyretics for symptoms. Concern for JH must not delay treatment. (Fetal monitoring after treatment is house/expert practice; CDC 2021 says counsel and seek care for fever, contractions, decreased FM.)</li><li>Second half of pregnancy: fetal ultrasound for congenital syphilis signs (hepatomegaly, ascites, hydrops, placentomegaly, anemia/MCA) — do not delay treatment for it; abnormal findings → MFM.</li><li>Adequacy for the newborn: appropriate penicillin regimen for stage, started ≥30 days before delivery, no evidence of reinfection/relapse. Delivery &lt;30 days after treatment start, clinical signs at delivery, or a ≥4-fold titer rise = inadequate maternal treatment → newborn gets a fuller evaluation.</li><li>Other: offer HIV testing; report to health department (Texas reportable); partners treated presumptively if exposed to early syphilis within 90 days. EPT is NOT used for syphilis.</li></ol>\n<p>PEDS HANDOFF (every reactive mother): maternal quantitative RPR at delivery (same lab/test type as infant's), prior titers, stage, drug/dates/doses, ≥30-day status. Newborn RPR is drawn on infant serum, not cord blood.</p>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>2. GENITAL HERPES (HSV)</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: YES — active genital lesions or prodrome at labor or ROM → cesarean. CAN WAIT: nothing about the decision; suppression planning for future pregnancies.</p>\n<ol><li>Every patient in labor: ask about prodrome and history; examine vulva, perineum, vagina, cervix. Confirm suspicious lesions by HSV NAAT/PCR (type-specific). Do not swab asymptomatic women \"blindly.\"</li><li>Cesarean is indicated for active genital lesions or prodromal symptoms at delivery (ACOG Level B). Cesarean lowers, but does not eliminate, transmission.</li><li>History of HSV, no lesions, no prodrome → vaginal birth. ACOG: no change to usual management, including FSE and operative vaginal delivery when indicated.</li><li>Recurrent NONgenital lesions (back, thigh, buttock) → cover with occlusive dressing, vaginal birth OK, but examine the genital area carefully first (ACOG Level C).</li><li>ROM at/near term with active lesions or prodrome → cesarean as soon as personnel/equipment are ready. There is no ROM duration beyond which cesarean stops helping.</li><li>Preterm PROM with active lesions → balance prematurity vs neonatal HSV; remote from term (especially recurrent disease) expectant management with antiviral therapy is supported; no consensus GA cut-off; steroid decision individualized. MFM involvement.</li><li>Primary or nonprimary FIRST-episode genital HSV in the third trimester = highest neonatal risk (CDC: 30–50% for acquisition near delivery; ACOG: ~40–80% if a primary outbreak is present at vaginal delivery). Treat with antivirals; continuing until delivery may be considered; cesarean MAY BE OFFERED even without lesions at labor because of prolonged shedding (ACOG Level C). MFM/ID consult (CDC).</li><li>Recurrent HSV at onset of labor without lesions → transmission risk &lt;1%.</li><li>Suppression from 36 wk for anyone with a clinical history of genital herpes (reduces recurrence at term, cesarean for HSV and shedding; does not fully prevent neonatal HSV).</li><li>Invasive monitoring: when there are ACTIVE lesions/first-episode infection and vaginal birth is happening anyway (e.g., precipitous, patient declines cesarean), avoid FSE/scalp sampling and operative vaginal delivery where possible (expert practice; see uncertainty note). Transabdominal procedures are not contraindicated.</li><li>Fever + markedly elevated transaminases in pregnancy without another cause → think disseminated HSV; empiric IV acyclovir pending blood HSV PCR (CDC).</li><li>Postpartum: breastfeeding OK unless a breast lesion; strict hand hygiene; cover lesions. Acyclovir/valacyclovir compatible with breastfeeding.</li></ol>\n<p>PEDS HANDOFF: notify peds for any lesions at delivery, any vaginal birth with lesions/prodrome, ROM with lesions, or first-episode infection in the 3rd trimester — state primary vs recurrent (drives the AAP algorithm: ~24 h surface + blood HSV PCR; empiric IV acyclovir when maternal infection is first-episode/primary).</p>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>3. GONORRHEA / CHLAMYDIA (GC/CT)</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: test (NAAT) and treat if positive/untreated; does NOT change mode of delivery. CAN WAIT: CT test of cure (~4 wk), 3-month retest, partner EPT (start it before discharge).</p>\n<ol><li>Unknown status in labor + at risk or no prenatal care → vaginal swab or urine NAAT on admission; make sure the result is followed after discharge and communicated to peds.</li><li>Positive or known-untreated GC → ceftriaxone 500 mg IM x1 (1 g if ≥150 kg — CDC adult weight rule) PLUS chlamydia treatment if CT not excluded. Cephalosporin anaphylaxis → ID/STD clinical consult (gentamicin cautioned in pregnancy).</li><li>Positive or known-untreated CT in pregnancy → azithromycin 1 g PO x1 (alternative amoxicillin 500 mg PO TID x7 d). Doxycycline is contraindicated in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters. Postpartum (no longer pregnant) the standard nonpregnant regimen is doxycycline 100 mg BID x7 d (CDC); short-term doxycycline is considered acceptable in lactation (LactMed — not a CDC statement).</li><li>Empiric treatment in labor: reasonable for a high-risk patient with untreated prior positive, symptomatic cervicitis, or partner with known GC/CT when results won't return before discharge. (House judgment; CDC 2021 does not define an intrapartum empiric rule.)</li><li>Follow-up: CT in pregnancy → test of cure ~4 wk after treatment (NAAT not before 4 wk), then retest 3 mo. GC → retest 3 mo (TOC only for pharyngeal GC, 7–14 d).</li><li>Partner treatment / EPT: EPT is permissible in Texas (CDC legal-status page, status as of 24 Jun 2009; 22 TAC §190.8(1)(L)(i) physicians, §222.4(e) APRNs; pharmacists may dispense without partner name). Texas DSHS recommends offering EPT for GC/CT. Partner GC EPT = cefixime 800 mg PO x1 (+ CT coverage if CT not excluded); CT EPT = doxycycline 100 mg BID x7 d (or azithromycin 1 g if adherence concern) for the nonpregnant partner. Not for syphilis.</li><li>Report GC/CT per Texas requirements.</li></ol>\n<p>PEDS HANDOFF:</p>\n<ul><li>Untreated maternal GC at delivery → newborn tested at exposed sites and treated presumptively: ceftriaxone 25–50 mg/kg IV/IM x1, max 250 mg (caution with hyperbilirubinemia/prematurity; cefotaxime if IV calcium) — peds orders.</li><li>Untreated maternal CT → presumptive neonatal treatment NOT recommended; peds monitors for conjunctivitis (5–12 d) and pneumonia (1–3 mo).</li><li>Universal ocular prophylaxis: erythromycin 0.5% ointment both eyes after every birth (vaginal or cesarean) — USPSTF Grade A; Texas law (HSC §81.091; 25 TAC §97.136: within 2 h of birth; parental objection documented in infant chart). It does NOT prevent chlamydial ophthalmia. If erythromycin unavailable and infant at GC risk → ceftriaxone dose above.</li></ul>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>4. TRICHOMONIASIS</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO. CAN WAIT: yes — treat any time, including postpartum.</p>\n<ol><li>Symptomatic pregnant women: test (NAAT preferred; wet mount insensitive) and treat at any stage. Routine screening of asymptomatic pregnant women: benefit not established (CDC). Screen at first prenatal visit if HIV+.</li><li>Treat with metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d (CDC women's regimen; metronidazole is low-risk in pregnancy). Avoid tinidazole in pregnancy. Alcohol avoidance not required (CDC).</li><li>Breastfeeding: compatible with the 7-day regimen (lower milk levels than the single 2 g dose).</li><li>Partners: concurrent treatment (EPT may have a role where permitted — permitted in Texas). Abstain until both treated. Retest ~3 mo. Test for other STIs incl. HIV and syphilis.</li></ol>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>5. BACTERIAL VAGINOSIS</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO; not a reason to delay delivery or induction. CAN WAIT: yes.</p>\n<ol><li>Treat SYMPTOMATIC pregnant women (associated with PROM, preterm birth, intra-amniotic infection, endometritis). Do not screen/treat asymptomatic women for preterm-birth prevention (CDC).</li><li>Options in pregnancy: metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d, OR metronidazole 0.75% gel 5 g intravaginally daily x5 d, OR clindamycin 2% cream 5 g intravaginally at bedtime x7 d; alternatives oral clindamycin 300 mg BID x7 d or clindamycin ovules. Avoid tinidazole, secnidazole and single-dose vaginal products in pregnancy.</li><li>Partner treatment not recommended. Test for other STIs/HIV.</li></ol>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>6. VULVOVAGINAL CANDIDIASIS (YEAST)</strong></p>\n<p>CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO. CAN WAIT: yes.</p>\n<ol><li>In pregnancy: topical azole for 7 days only (e.g., clotrimazole 1% or miconazole 2% cream 5 g intravaginally nightly x7 d; terconazole 0.4% x7 d). Do NOT use oral fluconazole in pregnancy (even single 150 mg — associated with miscarriage/anomalies; CDC).</li><li>Postpartum: fluconazole 150 mg PO x1 is acceptable, including during breastfeeding (LactMed).</li><li>Treat only symptomatic disease with confirmation (KOH/wet mount); culture-positive asymptomatic colonization is not treated. Partners not treated.</li></ol>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>ALSO CHECK AT ADMISSION (brief; related, not STI-card core)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>HIV — CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: no 3rd-trimester result → expedited HIV test (Texas: result &lt;6 h; if not done before delivery, newborn tested &lt;2 h after birth). Reactive expedited test in labor → start IV zidovudine immediately (do not wait for confirmation), call peds for infant ARV prophylaxis, and consult the National Perinatal HIV Hotline (1-888-448-8765). IV ZDV also if known HIV with RNA &gt;1,000 copies/mL or unknown within 4 wk of birth. Mode-of-delivery and ROM decisions → HIV card/HHS guidance. Breastfeeding decision → HHS shared decision-making; ID input.</li><li>Hepatitis B — does NOT change mode of delivery. HBsAg on every delivery admission (Texas). Infant handoff:<ul><li>Mother HBsAg-positive → infant HepB vaccine + HBIG within 12 h of birth.</li><li>Mother HBsAg UNKNOWN → infant HepB vaccine within 12 h; ≥2,000 g: HBIG only if mother returns positive (as soon as possible, no later than 7 days); &lt;2,000 g: vaccine + HBIG within 12 h if status still unknown. Expedite maternal HBsAg.</li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>DOSES (to be checked against CSM pharmacy build)</strong></p>\n<p>Maternal (pregnancy):</p>\n<ul><li>Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM x1 (primary/secondary/early latent; optional repeat at 1 wk in pregnancy) or weekly x3 (late latent/unknown duration). Pharmacy to confirm product (Bicillin L-A vs imported Lentocilin) and volume. Never Bicillin C-R.</li><li>Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM x1 (1 g IM if ≥150 kg).</li><li>Azithromycin 1 g PO x1 (CT in pregnancy). Alternative: amoxicillin 500 mg PO TID x7 d.</li><li>Doxycycline 100 mg PO BID x7 d — POSTPARTUM/nonpregnant only (contraindicated 2nd–3rd trimester).</li><li>Acyclovir 400 mg PO TID or valacyclovir 500 mg PO BID — suppression from 36 wk.</li><li>First-episode HSV in pregnancy: acyclovir 400 mg PO TID x7–10 d or valacyclovir 1 g PO BID x7–10 d (extend if not healed). Severe/disseminated: acyclovir 5–10 mg/kg IV q8h (renal adjust).</li><li>Metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d (trich; BV). Metronidazole 0.75% gel 5 g PV daily x5 d (BV). Clindamycin 2% cream 5 g PV qHS x7 d (BV).</li><li>Clotrimazole 1% / miconazole 2% cream 5 g PV daily x7 d (VVC in pregnancy). Fluconazole 150 mg PO x1 — postpartum only.</li><li>Zidovudine IV per HHS intrapartum table (2 mg/kg load over 1 h, then 1 mg/kg/h until delivery) — confirm current HHS table and CSM build.</li></ul>\n<p>Neonatal (peds orders; listed for handoff awareness only):</p>\n<ul><li>Erythromycin 0.5% ophthalmic ointment, both eyes, once (Texas: within 2 h).</li><li>Ceftriaxone 25–50 mg/kg IV/IM x1, max 250 mg (infant of untreated GC mother).</li><li>Hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG 0.5 mL IM per maternal HBsAg/weight rules above.</li></ul>\n<p>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</p>\n<p><strong>CODING (draft — verify with OBHG coding)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Syphilis: O98.11- (pregnancy, by trimester) / O98.12 (childbirth) / O98.13 (puerperium) + A5x stage code (e.g., A53.0 latent unspecified; A51.5 early latent; A52.8 late latent).</li><li>Gonorrhea: O98.21- / O98.22 / O98.23 + A54.x.</li><li>Chlamydia, genital HSV, trichomoniasis (other predominantly sexually transmitted): O98.31- / O98.32 / O98.33 + A56.x, A60.0x, A59.0x.</li><li>BV: O23.59- (other genital tract infection in pregnancy) + N76.0 (+ B96.89) — verify current mapping.</li><li>VVC: B37.31 (acute candidal vulvovaginitis) with pregnancy chapter code — verify O98.81- vs O23.59- with coding.</li><li>HIV: O98.71- / O98.72 + B20 or Z21. Hepatitis B carrier: O98.41- / O98.42 + B18.1 or Z22.51 (verify).</li><li>Encounter/screening: Z3A.xx; Z20.2 (contact/exposure to predominantly sexually transmitted infection); Z11.3 screening.</li><li>Cesarean for active HSV: delivery codes per op note; the HSV O98.32 code supports the indication.</li><li>OBED 99283–99285 by MDM; inpatient E/M per admission. Desensitization procedure/IM injection codes per facility (96372 administration).</li></ul>\n<p class=\"pearl\">TEACHING PEARL. A negative test today doesn't erase a positive test from last year. Treponemal antibodies usually last for life, so a \"new\" negative after a documented positive is more likely an assay or specimen issue than a cure. In pregnancy the default is simple: if you can't document adequate penicillin treatment, give it — the clock that matters is ≥30 days before delivery. The only STI on this card that sends her to the OR is genital HSV (lesion or prodrome); everything else is treated around a normal labor plan.</p>\n<p><strong>UNCERTAIN / CONFLICTING POINTS (flag for Michael)</strong></p>\n<ol><li>Syphilis 28-wk/delivery screening: CDC 2021 text is risk-based; ACOG 2024 and Texas law require/recommend universal. Card follows Texas law + ACOG.</li><li>Prior positive treponemal → negative today: no explicit CDC/ACOG rule for seroreversion; card's \"repeat with different method, seek records, treat if undocumented\" is expert extrapolation.</li><li>Jarisch-Herxheimer fetal monitoring: CDC advises counseling/return precautions; formal post-dose EFM duration is not specified — house to decide.</li><li>HSV neonatal risk with primary infection: CDC 30–50% vs ACOG 40–80% (different denominators). Cesarean for third-trimester first episode WITHOUT lesions is \"may be offered\" (Level C), not mandatory.</li><li>FSE/operative vaginal with HSV: ACOG says no change for history without lesions; avoidance applies only to active/first-episode disease and is expert practice, not an ACOG recommendation.</li><li>Empiric intrapartum GC/CT treatment while NAAT pending: not defined by CDC; house judgment.</li><li>Doxycycline and fluconazole in lactation: supported by LactMed, not by the CDC STI guideline text.</li><li>Hep B \"unknown\" infant: HBIG is NOT automatic for ≥2,000 g (vaccine within 12 h; HBIG ≤7 d only if mother positive). Corrected from the brief.</li><li>Ocular prophylaxis timing: Texas rule within 2 h vs CDC/USPSTF &lt;24 h — Texas is stricter; follow Texas.</li><li>Bicillin supply/imported product: pharmacy must confirm which benzathine penicillin product and volume CSM stocks.</li></ol>\n<p><strong>SOURCES (verified 13 Nov 2026 unless marked)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>CDC. Workowski KA, Bachmann LH, Chan PA, et al. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021. MMWR Recomm Rep 2021;70(No. RR-4):1–187 (erratum 2023). Still CDC's current full edition — no 2025/2026 replacement found; online sections reviewed: Syphilis, Syphilis During Pregnancy, Latent Syphilis, Congenital Syphilis, Gonococcal (adults, neonates; page updated 2022), Chlamydia, Genital Herpes, Trichomoniasis, BV, VVC. VERIFIED.</li><li>CDC. Laboratory Recommendations for Syphilis Testing, United States, 2024. MMWR Recomm Rep 2024;73(No. RR-1):1–32. VERIFIED (citation; content not re-read for this card).</li><li>CDC Doxy-PEP guidelines (2024) — not applicable in pregnancy; not used. Noted only.</li><li>ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 220. Management of Genital Herpes in Pregnancy. Obstet Gynecol 2020;135(5):e193–e202 (replaces PB 82). VERIFIED (full text read).</li><li>ACOG Practice Advisory. Screening for Syphilis in Pregnancy. April 2024, reaffirmed October 2025 (Practice Advisory, not a \"Clinical Practice Update\"). VERIFIED. Plus ACOG Syphilis Serologic Test and Treatment Algorithm and Physician FAQ. VERIFIED.</li><li>ACOG Committee Opinion No. 752. Prenatal and Perinatal HIV Testing. Obstet Gynecol 2018. VERIFIED (number/title).</li><li>Texas Health and Safety Code §81.090 (prenatal/delivery syphilis, HIV, HBV testing; expedited HIV &lt;6 h; newborn testing &lt;2 h) and Texas DSHS \"Syphilis, HIV, and Hepatitis B Testing and Pregnancy\" clinician sheet. VERIFIED.</li><li>Texas Health and Safety Code §81.091 and 25 TAC §97.136 (ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis, erythromycin 0.5% within 2 h). VERIFIED via statute summaries.</li><li>CDC Legal Status of EPT — Texas: permissible (22 TAC §190.8(1)(L)(i), §222.4(e), §291.29(b)(3), §291.33(c)(7)); CDC map updated 1 Jul 2026. Texas DSHS EPT guidance (GC/CT; not syphilis; cefixime for GC EPT). VERIFIED.</li><li>USPSTF. Ocular Prophylaxis for Gonococcal Ophthalmia Neonatorum, reaffirmation, JAMA 2019 (Grade A). VERIFIED.</li><li>HHS Panel. Recommendations for the Use of Antiretroviral Drugs During Pregnancy… Intrapartum Care (clinicalinfo.hiv.gov), accessed 13 Nov 2026. VERIFIED (IV ZDV indications); dosing table not re-read — pharmacy to confirm.</li><li>CDC Perinatal Hepatitis B algorithm (HBsAg unavailable at delivery) and ACIP. Schillie S, et al. MMWR Recomm Rep 2018;67(No. RR-1). VERIFIED via CDC pages/search summary.</li><li>CDC/FDA Bicillin L-A shortage updates (Lentocilin temporary importation; shelf-life extension Sept 2026). VERIFIED via CDC page notices.</li><li>Kimberlin DW, Baley J; AAP Committee on Infectious Diseases. Guidance on management of asymptomatic neonates born to women with active genital herpes lesions. Pediatrics 2013;131(2):e635–46. VERIFIED (citation).</li><li>AAP Red Book 2024–2027, 33rd ed. VERIFIED (edition exists); specific chapter content not reviewed — UNVERIFIED for content.</li><li>NIH LactMed: doxycycline, fluconazole, metronidazole. VERIFIED via search summaries (entries not opened in full).</li></ul>\n<p>Evidence: HSV cesarean for lesions — ACOG Level B; suppression from 36 wk — RCT-based (Level B); nongenital lesions/first-episode cesarean — Level C. Syphilis penicillin in pregnancy — decades of clinical experience, limited RCT data on optimal regimen (CDC). GC/CT/trich/BV/VVC regimens — RCT/meta-analysis-based CDC recommendations (moderate–high). Hep B and HIV intrapartum — high-quality (ACIP/HHS AI–AII).</p>\n", "flags": ["PROVISIONAL — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.", "Pharmacy check needed for benzathine penicillin product and volume (Bicillin L-A vs imported Lentocilin; never Bicillin C-R), the IV ZDV table, and all doses on this card.", "Coding: verify the BV and yeast pregnancy-chapter codes and the hep B carrier code."], "text": "PROVISIONAL — pending Michael review and CSM pharmacy check. Not live.\nScope: L&D/OBED admission of a pregnant patient (labor, ROM, induction, triage) with known, suspected or UNKNOWN status for syphilis, genital HSV, gonorrhea, chlamydia, trichomoniasis, BV or vulvovaginal candidiasis. Brief \"also check at admission\" section for HIV and hepatitis B (detailed management = separate cards/HHS guidance). Excludes nonpregnant GYN STI care, PID, and neonatal treatment beyond the handoff (peds/Red Book owns the newborn).\nADMISSION \"UNKNOWN STATUS\" CHECKLIST (every L&D admission)\n | Check | If missing / unknown at admission | Law / guideline\n | Syphilis (1st visit, ≥28 wk, delivery) | Draw RPR (or lab's reverse-algorithm screen) ON ADMISSION for every delivery. No mother or newborn leaves without maternal syphilis status documented. | Texas HSC §81.090(c) (delivery test required); ACOG 2024; CDC 2021\n | HIV (1st visit + 3rd trimester ≥28 wk) | No 3rd-trimester result in chart → EXPEDITED HIV test now (result <6 h). | Texas HSC §81.090(c-1)\n | Hepatitis B (HBsAg) | Draw HBsAg on admission for every delivery. | Texas HSC §81.090(c)\n | GC/CT NAAT | Not done in pregnancy AND at risk (<25 y, new/multiple partners, partner with STI, no prenatal care) → NAAT (vaginal swab or urine) now; peds told result is pending. | CDC 2021\n | HSV history | Ask EVERY patient about genital HSV and prodrome (burning, tingling, vulvar pain); examine vulva/perineum/cervix. | CDC 2021; ACOG PB 220\n | Partner treated? | Ask for GC/CT/trich/syphilis diagnoses this pregnancy — reinfection is the usual failure. | CDC 2021\nBOTTOM LINE\n• Only TWO things on this card change the WAY she delivers: active genital HSV lesions or prodrome at labor/ROM (→ cesarean), and HIV with viral load >1,000 or unknown (→ IV ZDV ± cesarean; separate card). Nothing else here is an indication for cesarean.\n• Syphilis does not change mode of delivery, but it is the highest-stakes item: status must be known before mother or baby goes home, and every untreated/undocumented case is treated with benzathine penicillin G NOW (no alternatives in pregnancy; desensitize if allergic).\n• Untreated GC/CT at delivery: treat mother, tell peds before birth if possible (untreated maternal GC → newborn ceftriaxone). Erythromycin eye ointment prevents gonococcal, NOT chlamydial, ophthalmia.\n• Trich, BV, VVC: treat if symptomatic; none of them changes intrapartum management or should delay delivery. Treatment can be finished postpartum.\nAT A GLANCE — CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT vs CAN WAIT\n | Infection | CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT | CAN WAIT UNTIL POSTPARTUM | PEDS HANDOFF\n | Syphilis | Admission serology is mandatory; treat untreated/undocumented NOW (do not wait for delivery). Mode of delivery unchanged. | Titer follow-up, partner services, health dept report (do not delay). | ALWAYS: maternal RPR titer at delivery, treatment dates/drug/stage, whether started ≥30 d before delivery.\n | HSV | YES: active genital lesions or prodrome at labor/ROM → cesarean. | Suppression counseling for next pregnancy. | YES if lesions at delivery, first-episode 3rd trimester, or vaginal birth with lesions.\n | GC | Treat if positive/untreated (ceftriaxone). Mode unchanged. | Retest 3 mo; EPT for partner. | YES if untreated/positive → neonatal ceftriaxone.\n | CT | Treat if positive/untreated (azithromycin). Mode unchanged. | Test of cure ~4 wk; retest 3 mo; EPT. | YES if untreated/positive → monitor infant (no presumptive treatment).\n | Trich | No. | Yes — treat any time incl. postpartum. | Not routinely.\n | BV | No; not a reason to delay delivery. | Yes (symptomatic only). | No.\n | VVC | No. | Yes; fluconazole only after delivery. | No (thrush follow-up routine).\n1. SYPHILIS\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: serology on admission (all deliveries — Texas law); treatment of untreated/undocumented syphilis is started on L&D, not deferred. MODE OF DELIVERY: unchanged. CAN WAIT: titer follow-up and partner services (but report and refer before discharge).\n• Screening in Texas: syphilis at first visit, third trimester (not earlier than 28 wk) and on admission for delivery (HSC §81.090). ACOG (2024, reaffirmed Oct 2025) also endorses universal 3-time screening. CDC 2021 text is risk-based for 28 wk/delivery — state law and ACOG govern here.\n• Know your lab's algorithm:• Traditional: nontreponemal (RPR/VDRL) first → reactive → treponemal confirm (TP-PA, EIA/CIA).\n• Reverse-sequence: treponemal immunoassay (EIA/CIA) first → reactive → reflex quantitative RPR. Treponemal tests stay positive for life in most patients, so a reactive EIA/CIA cannot distinguish old treated from current infection.\n• Discordant results:• EIA/CIA + / RPR − → second, different treponemal test (TP-PA preferred), same specimen if possible.• TP-PA + → current or past syphilis. Adequately treated before, no new risk → no treatment. No documented treatment → stage and treat.\n• TP-PA − → probable false-positive EIA/CIA in a low-risk patient; repeat serology within 4 wk can be considered if follow-up is reliable. If follow-up unlikely → treat by stage.\n• Prior DOCUMENTED positive treponemal test, negative treponemal test today → do not use today's result to clear her. Ask lab which assay; repeat with a different treponemal method (e.g., TP-PA if today's was EIA/CIA); repeat quantitative RPR; get treatment records (prior clinic, outside lab, Texas DSHS/local health department). If adequate treatment cannot be documented → treat as latent of unknown duration. (Expert extrapolation: CDC 2021 states seropositive pregnant women are \"considered infected unless an adequate treatment history is clearly documented\"; it does not specifically address treponemal seroreversion.)\n• Rapid treponemal test only at delivery (late/no prenatal care) → confirm with lab RPR + treponemal; do not delay newborn evaluation.\n• Staging (CDC): primary (chancre), secondary (rash incl. palms/soles, condyloma lata, mucous patches), early latent (infection within the past 12 months, documented), late latent (>12 months) or latent of unknown duration (most L&D cases). Neurologic/ocular/otic signs → neurosyphilis pathway, ID consult.\n• Treatment (benzathine penicillin G only — see DOSES):• Primary, secondary, early latent: 2.4 MU IM x1. In pregnancy a second 2.4 MU dose 1 wk later CAN be given (CDC 2021).\n• Late latent / unknown duration: 2.4 MU IM weekly x3 (7.2 MU total). Optimal interval 7 days; >9 days between doses is NOT acceptable in pregnancy → restart the full series. If she misses day 7, contact and treat within 2 days.\n• Penicillin allergy → penicillin skin test / graded challenge and desensitization, then penicillin. NO acceptable alternative in pregnancy (doxycycline/tetracycline avoided 2nd–3rd trimester; azithro/erythromycin don't treat the fetus; ceftriaxone data insufficient).\n• Bicillin L-A supply: national shortage ongoing; FDA has allowed temporary importation of Lentocilin (benzathine benzylpenicillin, 1.2 MU vials — product, diluent and volume differ; pharmacy prepares). CDC asks that benzathine penicillin be reserved for pregnant patients and infants. NEVER substitute Bicillin C-R (benzathine-procaine).\n• Jarisch-Herxheimer reaction (fever, myalgia, headache within 24 h; most often early syphilis): at >20 wk can cause contractions, decreased FM, or fetal heart rate changes. Practical L&D plan: give the dose with the patient on L&D or with clear return precautions; continuous/extended EFM if viable and in labor or otherwise monitored; antipyretics for symptoms. Concern for JH must not delay treatment. (Fetal monitoring after treatment is house/expert practice; CDC 2021 says counsel and seek care for fever, contractions, decreased FM.)\n• Second half of pregnancy: fetal ultrasound for congenital syphilis signs (hepatomegaly, ascites, hydrops, placentomegaly, anemia/MCA) — do not delay treatment for it; abnormal findings → MFM.\n• Adequacy for the newborn: appropriate penicillin regimen for stage, started ≥30 days before delivery, no evidence of reinfection/relapse. Delivery <30 days after treatment start, clinical signs at delivery, or a ≥4-fold titer rise = inadequate maternal treatment → newborn gets a fuller evaluation.\n• Other: offer HIV testing; report to health department (Texas reportable); partners treated presumptively if exposed to early syphilis within 90 days. EPT is NOT used for syphilis.\nPEDS HANDOFF (every reactive mother): maternal quantitative RPR at delivery (same lab/test type as infant's), prior titers, stage, drug/dates/doses, ≥30-day status. Newborn RPR is drawn on infant serum, not cord blood.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\n2. GENITAL HERPES (HSV)\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: YES — active genital lesions or prodrome at labor or ROM → cesarean. CAN WAIT: nothing about the decision; suppression planning for future pregnancies.\n• Every patient in labor: ask about prodrome and history; examine vulva, perineum, vagina, cervix. Confirm suspicious lesions by HSV NAAT/PCR (type-specific). Do not swab asymptomatic women \"blindly.\"\n• Cesarean is indicated for active genital lesions or prodromal symptoms at delivery (ACOG Level B). Cesarean lowers, but does not eliminate, transmission.\n• History of HSV, no lesions, no prodrome → vaginal birth. ACOG: no change to usual management, including FSE and operative vaginal delivery when indicated.\n• Recurrent NONgenital lesions (back, thigh, buttock) → cover with occlusive dressing, vaginal birth OK, but examine the genital area carefully first (ACOG Level C).\n• ROM at/near term with active lesions or prodrome → cesarean as soon as personnel/equipment are ready. There is no ROM duration beyond which cesarean stops helping.\n• Preterm PROM with active lesions → balance prematurity vs neonatal HSV; remote from term (especially recurrent disease) expectant management with antiviral therapy is supported; no consensus GA cut-off; steroid decision individualized. MFM involvement.\n• Primary or nonprimary FIRST-episode genital HSV in the third trimester = highest neonatal risk (CDC: 30–50% for acquisition near delivery; ACOG: ~40–80% if a primary outbreak is present at vaginal delivery). Treat with antivirals; continuing until delivery may be considered; cesarean MAY BE OFFERED even without lesions at labor because of prolonged shedding (ACOG Level C). MFM/ID consult (CDC).\n• Recurrent HSV at onset of labor without lesions → transmission risk <1%.\n• Suppression from 36 wk for anyone with a clinical history of genital herpes (reduces recurrence at term, cesarean for HSV and shedding; does not fully prevent neonatal HSV).\n• Invasive monitoring: when there are ACTIVE lesions/first-episode infection and vaginal birth is happening anyway (e.g., precipitous, patient declines cesarean), avoid FSE/scalp sampling and operative vaginal delivery where possible (expert practice; see uncertainty note). Transabdominal procedures are not contraindicated.\n• Fever + markedly elevated transaminases in pregnancy without another cause → think disseminated HSV; empiric IV acyclovir pending blood HSV PCR (CDC).\n• Postpartum: breastfeeding OK unless a breast lesion; strict hand hygiene; cover lesions. Acyclovir/valacyclovir compatible with breastfeeding.\nPEDS HANDOFF: notify peds for any lesions at delivery, any vaginal birth with lesions/prodrome, ROM with lesions, or first-episode infection in the 3rd trimester — state primary vs recurrent (drives the AAP algorithm: ~24 h surface + blood HSV PCR; empiric IV acyclovir when maternal infection is first-episode/primary).\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\n3. GONORRHEA / CHLAMYDIA (GC/CT)\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: test (NAAT) and treat if positive/untreated; does NOT change mode of delivery. CAN WAIT: CT test of cure (~4 wk), 3-month retest, partner EPT (start it before discharge).\n• Unknown status in labor + at risk or no prenatal care → vaginal swab or urine NAAT on admission; make sure the result is followed after discharge and communicated to peds.\n• Positive or known-untreated GC → ceftriaxone 500 mg IM x1 (1 g if ≥150 kg — CDC adult weight rule) PLUS chlamydia treatment if CT not excluded. Cephalosporin anaphylaxis → ID/STD clinical consult (gentamicin cautioned in pregnancy).\n• Positive or known-untreated CT in pregnancy → azithromycin 1 g PO x1 (alternative amoxicillin 500 mg PO TID x7 d). Doxycycline is contraindicated in the 2nd and 3rd trimesters. Postpartum (no longer pregnant) the standard nonpregnant regimen is doxycycline 100 mg BID x7 d (CDC); short-term doxycycline is considered acceptable in lactation (LactMed — not a CDC statement).\n• Empiric treatment in labor: reasonable for a high-risk patient with untreated prior positive, symptomatic cervicitis, or partner with known GC/CT when results won't return before discharge. (House judgment; CDC 2021 does not define an intrapartum empiric rule.)\n• Follow-up: CT in pregnancy → test of cure ~4 wk after treatment (NAAT not before 4 wk), then retest 3 mo. GC → retest 3 mo (TOC only for pharyngeal GC, 7–14 d).\n• Partner treatment / EPT: EPT is permissible in Texas (CDC legal-status page, status as of 24 Jun 2009; 22 TAC §190.8(1)(L)(i) physicians, §222.4(e) APRNs; pharmacists may dispense without partner name). Texas DSHS recommends offering EPT for GC/CT. Partner GC EPT = cefixime 800 mg PO x1 (+ CT coverage if CT not excluded); CT EPT = doxycycline 100 mg BID x7 d (or azithromycin 1 g if adherence concern) for the nonpregnant partner. Not for syphilis.\n• Report GC/CT per Texas requirements.\nPEDS HANDOFF:\n• Untreated maternal GC at delivery → newborn tested at exposed sites and treated presumptively: ceftriaxone 25–50 mg/kg IV/IM x1, max 250 mg (caution with hyperbilirubinemia/prematurity; cefotaxime if IV calcium) — peds orders.\n• Untreated maternal CT → presumptive neonatal treatment NOT recommended; peds monitors for conjunctivitis (5–12 d) and pneumonia (1–3 mo).\n• Universal ocular prophylaxis: erythromycin 0.5% ointment both eyes after every birth (vaginal or cesarean) — USPSTF Grade A; Texas law (HSC §81.091; 25 TAC §97.136: within 2 h of birth; parental objection documented in infant chart). It does NOT prevent chlamydial ophthalmia. If erythromycin unavailable and infant at GC risk → ceftriaxone dose above.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\n4. TRICHOMONIASIS\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO. CAN WAIT: yes — treat any time, including postpartum.\n• Symptomatic pregnant women: test (NAAT preferred; wet mount insensitive) and treat at any stage. Routine screening of asymptomatic pregnant women: benefit not established (CDC). Screen at first prenatal visit if HIV+.\n• Treat with metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d (CDC women's regimen; metronidazole is low-risk in pregnancy). Avoid tinidazole in pregnancy. Alcohol avoidance not required (CDC).\n• Breastfeeding: compatible with the 7-day regimen (lower milk levels than the single 2 g dose).\n• Partners: concurrent treatment (EPT may have a role where permitted — permitted in Texas). Abstain until both treated. Retest ~3 mo. Test for other STIs incl. HIV and syphilis.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\n5. BACTERIAL VAGINOSIS\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO; not a reason to delay delivery or induction. CAN WAIT: yes.\n• Treat SYMPTOMATIC pregnant women (associated with PROM, preterm birth, intra-amniotic infection, endometritis). Do not screen/treat asymptomatic women for preterm-birth prevention (CDC).\n• Options in pregnancy: metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d, OR metronidazole 0.75% gel 5 g intravaginally daily x5 d, OR clindamycin 2% cream 5 g intravaginally at bedtime x7 d; alternatives oral clindamycin 300 mg BID x7 d or clindamycin ovules. Avoid tinidazole, secnidazole and single-dose vaginal products in pregnancy.\n• Partner treatment not recommended. Test for other STIs/HIV.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\n6. VULVOVAGINAL CANDIDIASIS (YEAST)\nCHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: NO. CAN WAIT: yes.\n• In pregnancy: topical azole for 7 days only (e.g., clotrimazole 1% or miconazole 2% cream 5 g intravaginally nightly x7 d; terconazole 0.4% x7 d). Do NOT use oral fluconazole in pregnancy (even single 150 mg — associated with miscarriage/anomalies; CDC).\n• Postpartum: fluconazole 150 mg PO x1 is acceptable, including during breastfeeding (LactMed).\n• Treat only symptomatic disease with confirmation (KOH/wet mount); culture-positive asymptomatic colonization is not treated. Partners not treated.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\nALSO CHECK AT ADMISSION (brief; related, not STI-card core)\n• HIV — CHANGES INTRAPARTUM MANAGEMENT: no 3rd-trimester result → expedited HIV test (Texas: result <6 h; if not done before delivery, newborn tested <2 h after birth). Reactive expedited test in labor → start IV zidovudine immediately (do not wait for confirmation), call peds for infant ARV prophylaxis, and consult the National Perinatal HIV Hotline (1-888-448-8765). IV ZDV also if known HIV with RNA >1,000 copies/mL or unknown within 4 wk of birth. Mode-of-delivery and ROM decisions → HIV card/HHS guidance. Breastfeeding decision → HHS shared decision-making; ID input.\n• Hepatitis B — does NOT change mode of delivery. HBsAg on every delivery admission (Texas). Infant handoff:• Mother HBsAg-positive → infant HepB vaccine + HBIG within 12 h of birth.\n• Mother HBsAg UNKNOWN → infant HepB vaccine within 12 h; ≥2,000 g: HBIG only if mother returns positive (as soon as possible, no later than 7 days); <2,000 g: vaccine + HBIG within 12 h if status still unknown. Expedite maternal HBsAg.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\nDOSES (to be checked against CSM pharmacy build)\nMaternal (pregnancy):\n• Benzathine penicillin G 2.4 million units IM x1 (primary/secondary/early latent; optional repeat at 1 wk in pregnancy) or weekly x3 (late latent/unknown duration). Pharmacy to confirm product (Bicillin L-A vs imported Lentocilin) and volume. Never Bicillin C-R.\n• Ceftriaxone 500 mg IM x1 (1 g IM if ≥150 kg).\n• Azithromycin 1 g PO x1 (CT in pregnancy). Alternative: amoxicillin 500 mg PO TID x7 d.\n• Doxycycline 100 mg PO BID x7 d — POSTPARTUM/nonpregnant only (contraindicated 2nd–3rd trimester).\n• Acyclovir 400 mg PO TID or valacyclovir 500 mg PO BID — suppression from 36 wk.\n• First-episode HSV in pregnancy: acyclovir 400 mg PO TID x7–10 d or valacyclovir 1 g PO BID x7–10 d (extend if not healed). Severe/disseminated: acyclovir 5–10 mg/kg IV q8h (renal adjust).\n• Metronidazole 500 mg PO BID x7 d (trich; BV). Metronidazole 0.75% gel 5 g PV daily x5 d (BV). Clindamycin 2% cream 5 g PV qHS x7 d (BV).\n• Clotrimazole 1% / miconazole 2% cream 5 g PV daily x7 d (VVC in pregnancy). Fluconazole 150 mg PO x1 — postpartum only.\n• Zidovudine IV per HHS intrapartum table (2 mg/kg load over 1 h, then 1 mg/kg/h until delivery) — confirm current HHS table and CSM build.\nNeonatal (peds orders; listed for handoff awareness only):\n• Erythromycin 0.5% ophthalmic ointment, both eyes, once (Texas: within 2 h).\n• Ceftriaxone 25–50 mg/kg IV/IM x1, max 250 mg (infant of untreated GC mother).\n• Hepatitis B vaccine and HBIG 0.5 mL IM per maternal HBsAg/weight rules above.\nDoses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.\nCODING (draft — verify with OBHG coding)\n• Syphilis: O98.11- (pregnancy, by trimester) / O98.12 (childbirth) / O98.13 (puerperium) + A5x stage code (e.g., A53.0 latent unspecified; A51.5 early latent; A52.8 late latent).\n• Gonorrhea: O98.21- / O98.22 / O98.23 + A54.x.\n• Chlamydia, genital HSV, trichomoniasis (other predominantly sexually transmitted): O98.31- / O98.32 / O98.33 + A56.x, A60.0x, A59.0x.\n• BV: O23.59- (other genital tract infection in pregnancy) + N76.0 (+ B96.89) — verify current mapping.\n• VVC: B37.31 (acute candidal vulvovaginitis) with pregnancy chapter code — verify O98.81- vs O23.59- with coding.\n• HIV: O98.71- / O98.72 + B20 or Z21. Hepatitis B carrier: O98.41- / O98.42 + B18.1 or Z22.51 (verify).\n• Encounter/screening: Z3A.xx; Z20.2 (contact/exposure to predominantly sexually transmitted infection); Z11.3 screening.\n• Cesarean for active HSV: delivery codes per op note; the HSV O98.32 code supports the indication.\n• OBED 99283–99285 by MDM; inpatient E/M per admission. Desensitization procedure/IM injection codes per facility (96372 administration).\nTEACHING PEARL. A negative test today doesn't erase a positive test from last year. Treponemal antibodies usually last for life, so a \"new\" negative after a documented positive is more likely an assay or specimen issue than a cure. In pregnancy the default is simple: if you can't document adequate penicillin treatment, give it — the clock that matters is ≥30 days before delivery. The only STI on this card that sends her to the OR is genital HSV (lesion or prodrome); everything else is treated around a normal labor plan.\nUNCERTAIN / CONFLICTING POINTS (flag for Michael)\n• Syphilis 28-wk/delivery screening: CDC 2021 text is risk-based; ACOG 2024 and Texas law require/recommend universal. Card follows Texas law + ACOG.\n• Prior positive treponemal → negative today: no explicit CDC/ACOG rule for seroreversion; card's \"repeat with different method, seek records, treat if undocumented\" is expert extrapolation.\n• Jarisch-Herxheimer fetal monitoring: CDC advises counseling/return precautions; formal post-dose EFM duration is not specified — house to decide.\n• HSV neonatal risk with primary infection: CDC 30–50% vs ACOG 40–80% (different denominators). Cesarean for third-trimester first episode WITHOUT lesions is \"may be offered\" (Level C), not mandatory.\n• FSE/operative vaginal with HSV: ACOG says no change for history without lesions; avoidance applies only to active/first-episode disease and is expert practice, not an ACOG recommendation.\n• Empiric intrapartum GC/CT treatment while NAAT pending: not defined by CDC; house judgment.\n• Doxycycline and fluconazole in lactation: supported by LactMed, not by the CDC STI guideline text.\n• Hep B \"unknown\" infant: HBIG is NOT automatic for ≥2,000 g (vaccine within 12 h; HBIG ≤7 d only if mother positive). Corrected from the brief.\n• Ocular prophylaxis timing: Texas rule within 2 h vs CDC/USPSTF <24 h — Texas is stricter; follow Texas.\n• Bicillin supply/imported product: pharmacy must confirm which benzathine penicillin product and volume CSM stocks.\nSOURCES (verified 13 Nov 2026 unless marked)\n• CDC. Workowski KA, Bachmann LH, Chan PA, et al. Sexually Transmitted Infections Treatment Guidelines, 2021. MMWR Recomm Rep 2021;70(No. RR-4):1–187 (erratum 2023). Still CDC's current full edition — no 2025/2026 replacement found; online sections reviewed: Syphilis, Syphilis During Pregnancy, Latent Syphilis, Congenital Syphilis, Gonococcal (adults, neonates; page updated 2022), Chlamydia, Genital Herpes, Trichomoniasis, BV, VVC. VERIFIED.\n• CDC. Laboratory Recommendations for Syphilis Testing, United States, 2024. MMWR Recomm Rep 2024;73(No. RR-1):1–32. VERIFIED (citation; content not re-read for this card).\n• CDC Doxy-PEP guidelines (2024) — not applicable in pregnancy; not used. Noted only.\n• ACOG Practice Bulletin No. 220. Management of Genital Herpes in Pregnancy. Obstet Gynecol 2020;135(5):e193–e202 (replaces PB 82). VERIFIED (full text read).\n• ACOG Practice Advisory. Screening for Syphilis in Pregnancy. April 2024, reaffirmed October 2025 (Practice Advisory, not a \"Clinical Practice Update\"). VERIFIED. Plus ACOG Syphilis Serologic Test and Treatment Algorithm and Physician FAQ. VERIFIED.\n• ACOG Committee Opinion No. 752. Prenatal and Perinatal HIV Testing. Obstet Gynecol 2018. VERIFIED (number/title).\n• Texas Health and Safety Code §81.090 (prenatal/delivery syphilis, HIV, HBV testing; expedited HIV <6 h; newborn testing <2 h) and Texas DSHS \"Syphilis, HIV, and Hepatitis B Testing and Pregnancy\" clinician sheet. VERIFIED.\n• Texas Health and Safety Code §81.091 and 25 TAC §97.136 (ophthalmia neonatorum prophylaxis, erythromycin 0.5% within 2 h). VERIFIED via statute summaries.\n• CDC Legal Status of EPT — Texas: permissible (22 TAC §190.8(1)(L)(i), §222.4(e), §291.29(b)(3), §291.33(c)(7)); CDC map updated 1 Jul 2026. Texas DSHS EPT guidance (GC/CT; not syphilis; cefixime for GC EPT). VERIFIED.\n• USPSTF. Ocular Prophylaxis for Gonococcal Ophthalmia Neonatorum, reaffirmation, JAMA 2019 (Grade A). VERIFIED.\n• HHS Panel. Recommendations for the Use of Antiretroviral Drugs During Pregnancy… Intrapartum Care (clinicalinfo.hiv.gov), accessed 13 Nov 2026. VERIFIED (IV ZDV indications); dosing table not re-read — pharmacy to confirm.\n• CDC Perinatal Hepatitis B algorithm (HBsAg unavailable at delivery) and ACIP. Schillie S, et al. MMWR Recomm Rep 2018;67(No. RR-1). VERIFIED via CDC pages/search summary.\n• CDC/FDA Bicillin L-A shortage updates (Lentocilin temporary importation; shelf-life extension Sept 2026). VERIFIED via CDC page notices.\n• Kimberlin DW, Baley J; AAP Committee on Infectious Diseases. Guidance on management of asymptomatic neonates born to women with active genital herpes lesions. Pediatrics 2013;131(2):e635–46. VERIFIED (citation).\n• AAP Red Book 2024–2027, 33rd ed. VERIFIED (edition exists); specific chapter content not reviewed — UNVERIFIED for content.\n• NIH LactMed: doxycycline, fluconazole, metronidazole. VERIFIED via search summaries (entries not opened in full).\nEvidence: HSV cesarean for lesions — ACOG Level B; suppression from 36 wk — RCT-based (Level B); nongenital lesions/first-episode cesarean — Level C. Syphilis penicillin in pregnancy — decades of clinical experience, limited RCT data on optimal regimen (CDC). GC/CT/trich/BV/VVC regimens — RCT/meta-analysis-based CDC recommendations (moderate–high). Hep B and HIV intrapartum — high-quality (ACIP/HHS AI–AII).", "lastReviewed": "Provisional — Nov 2026 (not yet reviewed)"}, {"id": "GYN-01", "kind": "card", "title": "Tubal ectopic (ACOG PB 193)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Unstable + positive hCG = operating room, not another quantitative. Heterotopic is possible after ART.</li><li>PUL is not an ectopic. Discriminatory zone is conservatively 3500 mIU/mL — an empty uterus below that is not a license for methotrexate.</li><li>Viable IUP hCG rise is often ≥49% / 40% / 33% in 2 days at hCG &lt;1500 / 1500–3000 / &gt;3000. Slower is not automatically ectopic.</li><li>Methotrexate only if stable, reliable follow-up, no rupture, no IUP, and no absolute contraindication (PB 193 Box 1): immunodeficiency; moderate to severe anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia; methotrexate sensitivity; active pulmonary disease; active peptic ulcer disease; clinically important hepatic or renal dysfunction; breastfeeding. Relative: embryonic cardiac activity; hCG &gt;5,000 mIU/mL; ectopic &gt;4 cm on TVUS; refusal of transfusion.</li><li>Single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Need ≥15% hCG drop day 4 to day 7. If not, repeat or operate. Two-dose (50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4) may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG.</li><li>Surgery: unstable, rupture, failed methotrexate, contraindication, or she chooses it.</li><li>Rho(D) immune globulin if Rh-negative and unsensitized (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies); dose per CSM pharmacy.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Unstable → operating room. PUL → observe; do not treat a number. Methotrexate only with a day-4/7 plan that will actually be seen.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Unstable + positive hCG = operating room, not another quantitative. Heterotopic is possible after ART.  PUL is not an ectopic. Discriminatory zone is conservatively 3500 mIU/mL — an empty uterus below that is not a license for methotrexate.  Viable IUP hCG rise is often ≥49% / 40% / 33% in 2 days at hCG &lt;1500 / 1500–3000 / &gt;3000. Slower is not automatically ectopic.  Methotrexate only if stable, reliable follow-up, no rupture, no IUP, and no absolute contraindication (PB 193 Box 1): immunodeficiency; moderate to severe anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia; methotrexate sensitivity; active pulmonary disease; active peptic ulcer disease; clinically important hepatic or renal dysfunction; breastfeeding. Relative: embryonic cardiac activity; hCG &gt;5,000 mIU/mL; ectopic &gt;4 cm on TVUS; refusal of transfusion.  Single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Need ≥15% hCG drop day 4 to day 7. If not, repeat or operate. Two-dose (50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4) may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG.  Surgery: unstable, rupture, failed methotrexate, contraindication, or she chooses it.  Rho(D) immune globulin if Rh-negative and unsensitized (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies); dose per CSM pharmacy.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Unstable → operating room. PUL → observe; do not treat a number. Methotrexate only with a day-4/7 plan that will actually be seen. "}, {"id": "GYN-02", "kind": "card", "title": "Early pregnancy loss (ACOG PB 200)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Diagnostic (SRU): CRL ≥7 mm, no heartbeat. Mean sac diameter ≥25 mm, no embryo. Embryo without heartbeat ≥2 weeks after a sac without a yolk sac, or ≥11 days after a sac with a yolk sac.</li><li>Suspicious only — repeat in 7–10 days: CRL &lt;7 mm no heartbeat; MSD 16–24 mm no embryo.</li><li>If the pregnancy is not clearly intrauterine, this is a PUL (GYN-01). Do not give misoprostol or methotrexate on a maybe.</li><li>Expectant: first trimester only. Up to 8 weeks, about 80% complete. Give a bleeding rule (two maxi pads per hour × 2 hours) and analgesia.</li><li>Medical: misoprostol 800 mcg vaginally; repeat no earlier than 3 hours, typically within 7 days. Add mifepristone 200 mg orally 24 hours before misoprostol when available (Level A).</li><li>Surgery now: hemorrhage, instability, infection, severe anemia, bleeding disorder, or she chooses it. Suction (MVA or electric). One dose of doxycycline 200 mg before the procedure.</li><li>Asymptomatic thick stripe after treatment is not an indication to operate if the sac is gone.</li><li>PB 200: consider Rho(D) immune globulin, especially later first trimester; give after surgical management, at least 50 mcg.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — suspected molar pregnancy</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Surgical evacuation only (suction D&amp;E); avoid medical evacuation (failure, hemorrhage, post-molar GTN risk).</li><li>Before suction: type &amp; cross 2 U, large-bore IV; have TXA 1 g IV, oxytocin, misoprostol 1000 mcg PR, Foley 30–60 mL tamponade, methergine/Hemabate ready.</li><li>After: weekly hCG until 3 consecutive normals; reliable hormonal contraception ≥6 months (IUD relatively contraindicated); refer if hCG fails to normalize.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Prove it is an intrauterine pregnancy and that it is nonviable before you treat. Stable patients choose expectant, misoprostol ± mifepristone, or suction. Unstable or infected → suction.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Diagnostic (SRU): CRL ≥7 mm, no heartbeat. Mean sac diameter ≥25 mm, no embryo. Embryo without heartbeat ≥2 weeks after a sac without a yolk sac, or ≥11 days after a sac with a yolk sac.  Suspicious only — repeat in 7–10 days: CRL &lt;7 mm no heartbeat; MSD 16–24 mm no embryo.  If the pregnancy is not clearly intrauterine, this is a PUL (GYN-01). Do not give misoprostol or methotrexate on a maybe.  Expectant: first trimester only. Up to 8 weeks, about 80% complete. Give a bleeding rule (two maxi pads per hour × 2 hours) and analgesia.  Medical: misoprostol 800 mcg vaginally; repeat no earlier than 3 hours, typically within 7 days. Add mifepristone 200 mg orally 24 hours before misoprostol when available (Level A).  Surgery now: hemorrhage, instability, infection, severe anemia, bleeding disorder, or she chooses it. Suction (MVA or electric). One dose of doxycycline 200 mg before the procedure.  Asymptomatic thick stripe after treatment is not an indication to operate if the sac is gone.  PB 200: consider Rho(D) immune globulin, especially later first trimester; give after surgical management, at least 50 mcg.  \n   OBHG pearl — suspected molar pregnancy  \n  Surgical evacuation only (suction D&amp;E); avoid medical evacuation (failure, hemorrhage, post-molar GTN risk).  Before suction: type &amp; cross 2 U, large-bore IV; have TXA 1 g IV, oxytocin, misoprostol 1000 mcg PR, Foley 30–60 mL tamponade, methergine/Hemabate ready.  After: weekly hCG until 3 consecutive normals; reliable hormonal contraception ≥6 months (IUD relatively contraindicated); refer if hCG fails to normalize.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Prove it is an intrauterine pregnancy and that it is nonviable before you treat. Stable patients choose expectant, misoprostol ± mifepristone, or suction. Unstable or infected → suction. "}, {"id": "PP-01", "kind": "card", "title": "Postpartum hemorrhage (PB 183 + 2025 CPU)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>PPH = Stage 1 (CMQCC V3): QBL ≥500 mL after vaginal birth or ≥1000 mL after cesarean, or abnormal VS (HR ≥110, BP ≤85/45, SpO2 &lt;95%, shock index 0.9, or confusion). Do not wait for 1500 mL or a hematocrit.</li><li>Four T's: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin. Tone is first, but look at the others in the same minute.</li><li>Two large-bore IVs, type and cross, empty the bladder, bimanual examination, weigh the laps.</li><li>Uterotonic #1 while you look for lacerations and retained placenta. TXA 1 g IV when first-line medications fail; best &lt;3 hours from birth.</li><li>2025 CPU: after uterotonics fail, place a device at 1000–1499 mL rather than waiting until 1500 mL. ACOG does not prefer balloon versus vacuum. This service prefers JADA; Bakri is the alternative if JADA is not available or not appropriate.</li></ul>\n<p>JADA (preferred) — vacuum-induced</p>\n<ul><li>After the placenta is out and the cavity is empty. Insert the intrauterine loop to the fundus. Fill the cervical seal balloon with 60 mL sterile fluid, then up to 120 mL to seal the cervix.</li><li>Connect regulated vacuum at 80 ± 10 mmHg. Blood in the canister means the seal is working. Control is often measured in minutes.</li><li>Leave until bleeding is controlled and lochia is appropriate, then wean vacuum and deflate the seal before removal. Vacuum data before 34 weeks are thin — use judgment.</li></ul>\n<p>Bakri balloon (alternative)</p>\n<ul><li>Insert after the placenta is out. Inflate the uterine balloon with 300–500 mL sterile saline (do not exceed the device maximum). Confirm fundal position.</li><li>Watch the drainage port. Vaginal packing may help keep the stem in place. Leave typically 12–24 hours once she is stable, then deflate and remove.</li><li>If either device fails, or you suspect inversion, rupture, or accreta — operating room. IR only if she is stable with slow persistent bleeding.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — Pit + 2 / Stages (District II–ACOG checklist 2026)</strong></p>\n<ul><li><strong>Pit + 2:</strong> open the hemorrhage checklist and name roles when you start a <em>second</em> non-prophylactic treatment (second uterotonic, TXA, or a device) — not only when the floor is covered in blood.</li><li><strong>Stages (escalate or stop):</strong> Stage 1 (CMQCC) = QBL ≥500 mL vaginal or ≥1000 mL cesarean, or abnormal VS, <em>or</em> second treatment (Pit + 2) → access, Foley, massage, uterotonics/TXA, <strong>JADA first</strong> (Bakri if JADA unavailable). Stage 2 = still bleeding toward 1500 mL or &gt;2 uterotonics → second IV, cart, STAT labs, 2 U RBCs, prepare OR. Stage 3 = &gt;1500 mL, 2 U already in, abnormal VS/labs, or occult bleed → OR, MTP 1:1:1, specialty backup; <strong>aortic compression</strong> is a bridge. Stage 4 = cardiovascular collapse / AFE → ACLS + definitive surgery + aggressive MTP.</li><li>Every escalation: restate <strong>cumulative QBL, stage, VS,</strong> and uterotonic contraindications (SBAR for arrivals).</li><li><strong>Concealed hemorrhage:</strong> wrong VS without floor bleeding → focused ultrasound early; do not wait for a puddle.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. ≥500 mL vaginal, ≥1000 mL cesarean, or abnormal VS = PPH (CMQCC Stage 1). Tone, TXA, then a device early. JADA first; Bakri if JADA is not available. Do not wait for 1500 mL.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  PPH = Stage 1 (CMQCC V3): QBL ≥500 mL after vaginal birth or ≥1000 mL after cesarean, or abnormal VS (HR ≥110, BP ≤85/45, SpO2 &lt;95%, shock index 0.9, or confusion). Do not wait for 1500 mL or a hematocrit.  Four T's: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin. Tone is first, but look at the others in the same minute.  Two large-bore IVs, type and cross, empty the bladder, bimanual examination, weigh the laps.  Uterotonic #1 while you look for lacerations and retained placenta. TXA 1 g IV when first-line medications fail; best &lt;3 hours from birth.  2025 CPU: after uterotonics fail, place a device at 1000–1499 mL rather than waiting until 1500 mL. ACOG does not prefer balloon versus vacuum. This service prefers JADA; Bakri is the alternative if JADA is not available or not appropriate.  \n JADA (preferred) — vacuum-induced \n  After the placenta is out and the cavity is empty. Insert the intrauterine loop to the fundus. Fill the cervical seal balloon with 60 mL sterile fluid, then up to 120 mL to seal the cervix.  Connect regulated vacuum at 80 ± 10 mmHg. Blood in the canister means the seal is working. Control is often measured in minutes.  Leave until bleeding is controlled and lochia is appropriate, then wean vacuum and deflate the seal before removal. Vacuum data before 34 weeks are thin — use judgment.  \n Bakri balloon (alternative) \n  Insert after the placenta is out. Inflate the uterine balloon with 300–500 mL sterile saline (do not exceed the device maximum). Confirm fundal position.  Watch the drainage port. Vaginal packing may help keep the stem in place. Leave typically 12–24 hours once she is stable, then deflate and remove.  If either device fails, or you suspect inversion, rupture, or accreta — operating room. IR only if she is stable with slow persistent bleeding.  \n   OBHG pearl — Pit + 2 / Stages (District II–ACOG checklist 2026)  \n   Pit + 2:  open the hemorrhage checklist and name roles when you start a  second  non-prophylactic treatment (second uterotonic, TXA, or a device) — not only when the floor is covered in blood.   Stages (escalate or stop):  Stage 1 (CMQCC) = QBL ≥500 mL vaginal or ≥1000 mL cesarean, or abnormal VS,  or  second treatment (Pit + 2) → access, Foley, massage, uterotonics/TXA,  JADA first  (Bakri if JADA unavailable). Stage 2 = still bleeding toward 1500 mL or &gt;2 uterotonics → second IV, cart, STAT labs, 2 U RBCs, prepare OR. Stage 3 = &gt;1500 mL, 2 U already in, abnormal VS/labs, or occult bleed → OR, MTP 1:1:1, specialty backup;  aortic compression  is a bridge. Stage 4 = cardiovascular collapse / AFE → ACLS + definitive surgery + aggressive MTP.  Every escalation: restate  cumulative QBL, stage, VS,  and uterotonic contraindications (SBAR for arrivals).   Concealed hemorrhage:  wrong VS without floor bleeding → focused ultrasound early; do not wait for a puddle.   \n BOTTOM LINE. ≥500 mL vaginal, ≥1000 mL cesarean, or abnormal VS = PPH (CMQCC Stage 1). Tone, TXA, then a device early. JADA first; Bakri if JADA is not available. Do not wait for 1500 mL. "}, {"id": "PP-02", "kind": "card", "title": "Anemia in pregnancy / postpartum (ACOG PB 233)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<ul><li>Anemia: Hb &lt;11 / Hct &lt;33% in the first and third trimesters; Hb &lt;10.5 / Hct &lt;32% in the second.</li><li>Screen with a CBC in the first trimester and again at 24+0–28+6w.</li><li>Iron deficiency: low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, saturation &lt;18%. No response to oral iron in 7–10 days (no reticulocytosis) → wrong diagnosis, malabsorption, nonadherence, or ongoing loss.</li><li>Low-dose iron from the first trimester for every pregnant patient (Level A).</li><li>Treat iron-deficiency anemia with iron in addition to the prenatal vitamin. Ferrous sulfate once daily — or every other day — is as effective as twice-daily dosing and usually better tolerated. Do not default to BID.</li><li>IV iron after the first trimester or postpartum if she cannot tolerate or does not respond to oral iron, or if deficiency is severe late in pregnancy.</li><li>Transfuse for hypovolemia from blood loss or when an anemic patient who is unstable must go to the operating room.</li><li>Hb &lt;6: consider transfusion for fetal indications — abnormal fetal oxygenation, oligohydramnios, cerebral vasodilation, and fetal death are described at this level.</li><li>Cell salvage is reasonable when large blood loss is expected (previa / PAS).</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Screen twice. Ferrous sulfate once daily or every other day — not BID by default. IV iron if oral therapy fails or she is late and severely deficient. Transfuse for instability or Hb &lt;6 with a compromised fetus.</p>", "flags": [], "text": "  Anemia: Hb &lt;11 / Hct &lt;33% in the first and third trimesters; Hb &lt;10.5 / Hct &lt;32% in the second.  Screen with a CBC in the first trimester and again at 24+0–28+6w.  Iron deficiency: low iron, high TIBC, low ferritin, saturation &lt;18%. No response to oral iron in 7–10 days (no reticulocytosis) → wrong diagnosis, malabsorption, nonadherence, or ongoing loss.  Low-dose iron from the first trimester for every pregnant patient (Level A).  Treat iron-deficiency anemia with iron in addition to the prenatal vitamin. Ferrous sulfate once daily — or every other day — is as effective as twice-daily dosing and usually better tolerated. Do not default to BID.  IV iron after the first trimester or postpartum if she cannot tolerate or does not respond to oral iron, or if deficiency is severe late in pregnancy.  Transfuse for hypovolemia from blood loss or when an anemic patient who is unstable must go to the operating room.  Hb &lt;6: consider transfusion for fetal indications — abnormal fetal oxygenation, oligohydramnios, cerebral vasodilation, and fetal death are described at this level.  Cell salvage is reasonable when large blood loss is expected (previa / PAS).  \n BOTTOM LINE. Screen twice. Ferrous sulfate once daily or every other day — not BID by default. IV iron if oral therapy fails or she is late and severely deficient. Transfuse for instability or Hb &lt;6 with a compromised fetus. "}, {"id": "SYS-01", "kind": "card", "title": "Critical care in pregnancy (ACOG PB 211)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Hour one</p>\n<ul><li>Leading ICU admissions: hypertensive disease and obstetric hemorrhage. Also sepsis, ARDS, DKA, cardiac disease, and neurologic events.</li><li>Sepsis: early antibiotics reduce mortality (Level A). Cultures before the first dose only if they do not delay it. Source the same hour.</li><li>Do not withhold needed medications or diagnostic imaging because she is pregnant. Limit radiation and teratogens when you can — do not skip the study that changes the plan.</li><li>Fluid in measured aliquots, especially in preeclampsia, cardiac disease, or PAS.</li><li>Adult ICU must accept the pregnant patient. Transfer when a higher-level maternal hospital is required and she is stable enough to move. Do not transfer a crashing patient past an available operating room or ICU.</li></ul>\n<p>Maternal cardiac arrest — perimortem cesarean</p>\n<ul><li>Same ACLS ratios, drugs, and defibrillation as any adult. Manual left uterine displacement — do not tilt the board if that weakens compressions.</li><li>Perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest, fundus ≥20 weeks; scalpel is the instrument. Do not wait for an OR.</li><li>Antiseptic splash if available.</li><li>The 4–5 minute teaching point is not a death clock. Faster is better; benefit is still described well beyond 5 minutes. Still proceed if you are late.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Antibiotics early. Do not withhold imaging or drugs for the fetus. Arrest plus fundus ≥20 weeks → perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest. Do not wait for an OR. Stay if this hospital can finish the case.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Hour one \n  Leading ICU admissions: hypertensive disease and obstetric hemorrhage. Also sepsis, ARDS, DKA, cardiac disease, and neurologic events.  Sepsis: early antibiotics reduce mortality (Level A). Cultures before the first dose only if they do not delay it. Source the same hour.  Do not withhold needed medications or diagnostic imaging because she is pregnant. Limit radiation and teratogens when you can — do not skip the study that changes the plan.  Fluid in measured aliquots, especially in preeclampsia, cardiac disease, or PAS.  Adult ICU must accept the pregnant patient. Transfer when a higher-level maternal hospital is required and she is stable enough to move. Do not transfer a crashing patient past an available operating room or ICU.  \n Maternal cardiac arrest — perimortem cesarean \n  Same ACLS ratios, drugs, and defibrillation as any adult. Manual left uterine displacement — do not tilt the board if that weakens compressions.  Perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest, fundus ≥20 weeks; scalpel is the instrument. Do not wait for an OR.  Antiseptic splash if available.  The 4–5 minute teaching point is not a death clock. Faster is better; benefit is still described well beyond 5 minutes. Still proceed if you are late.  \n BOTTOM LINE. Antibiotics early. Do not withhold imaging or drugs for the fetus. Arrest plus fundus ≥20 weeks → perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest. Do not wait for an OR. Stay if this hospital can finish the case. "}, {"id": "SYS-02", "kind": "card", "title": "Thromboembolism in pregnancy (ACOG PB 196 · CMQCC VTE)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-01.md", "html": "<p>Diagnose — do not use D-dimer</p>\n<ul><li>Suspected DVT: compression US of the proximal veins first (Level A). Whole-leg swelling ± flank/buttock pain → iliac imaging or empiric heparin. Repeat US at 3 and 7 days if iliac clot is not suspected and the first study is negative.</li><li>Suspected PE: chest X-ray first. Normal film → V/Q. Abnormal film → CT angiography. Do not withhold imaging because she is pregnant.</li></ul>\n<p>Treat acute VTE</p>\n<ul><li>Adjusted-dose LMWH first-line. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Anti-Xa target 0.6–1.0 IU/mL 4 hours after a twice-daily dose.</li><li>No warfarin for most pregnant patients. No DOACs in pregnancy or lactation. Mechanical valve is the exception — multidisciplinary plan.</li><li>Acute VTE this pregnancy: therapeutic antepartum and at least 6 weeks postpartum.</li></ul>\n<p>Prophylaxis risk (CMQCC Low / Medium / High)</p>\n<ul><li>Major (CS algorithm): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH requiring transfusion / further operation / IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72h current or within last month; chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome).</li><li>Minor: multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion/further op/IR; family history VTE (1st-degree &lt;50); smoker; preeclampsia.</li><li>Cesarean — LOW (not medium/high): mechanical only. MEDIUM (1 major OR ≥2 minor): mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH postpartum until discharge. (CMQCC table “&gt;2 Minor”; house ≥2 per prose/ACCP.) HIGH (high-risk thrombophilia incl APS no prior VTE; prior provoked/idiopathic/estrogen VTE; low-risk thrombophilia + FH VTE OR single prior VTE; already on outpatient LMWH/UFH; multiple prior VTE; prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS): mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks <strong>OR</strong> mechanical + therapeutic (PP dose ≥ antepartum) in hospital and to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; prophylactic often enough for high-risk thrombophilia alone without prior VTE.</li><li>Vaginal — LOW (isolated delivery BMI &gt;40, no medium/high criteria): mechanical only; other vaginal without medium/high → early ambulation. MEDIUM (BMI &gt;40 AND [antepartum hosp ≥72h current/past month OR low-risk thrombophilia]): mechanical + prophylactic during PP hospitalization; consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia. HIGH: same high-risk set as CS → mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks <strong>OR</strong> mechanical + therapeutic to 6 weeks (favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; otherwise prophylactic often enough — bleeding risk / hematology).</li><li>Definitions: High-risk thrombophilia = ATIII def, APS, FVL or PT homozygous/compound het. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, protein C or S deficiency. Family Hx = 1st-degree VTE before age 50. All CS: ambulate, avoid dehydration, mechanical before CS until ambulatory.</li></ul>\n<p>Around birth</p>\n<ul><li>SCDs before every cesarean. Hold prophylactic LMWH ≥12 hours before scheduled induction or cesarean; hold therapeutic ≥24 hours.</li><li>Restart no sooner than 4–6 hours after vaginal birth or 6–12 hours after cesarean. After neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH ≥24 hours after the block and ≥4 hours after catheter removal.</li><li>Warfarin, LMWH, and UFH are compatible with breastfeeding.</li></ul>\n<p>Doses</p>\n<ul><li>Prophylactic enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily; Intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes / hematology — not a CMQCC L/M/H default); Therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (Anti-Xa 0.6–1.0). Hold prophylactic ≥12h / therapeutic ≥24h before scheduled birth.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. No D-dimer. Compression US for the leg; CXR then V/Q or CTA for the chest. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg q12h for acute clot. CMQCC risk band (L/M/H) drives postpartum mechanical ± pharmacologic duration. Hold prophylactic 12 hours / therapeutic 24 hours before scheduled birth; SCDs before every cesarean.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Diagnose — do not use D-dimer \n  Suspected DVT: compression US of the proximal veins first (Level A). Whole-leg swelling ± flank/buttock pain → iliac imaging or empiric heparin. Repeat US at 3 and 7 days if iliac clot is not suspected and the first study is negative.  Suspected PE: chest X-ray first. Normal film → V/Q. Abnormal film → CT angiography. Do not withhold imaging because she is pregnant.  \n Treat acute VTE \n  Adjusted-dose LMWH first-line. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Anti-Xa target 0.6–1.0 IU/mL 4 hours after a twice-daily dose.  No warfarin for most pregnant patients. No DOACs in pregnancy or lactation. Mechanical valve is the exception — multidisciplinary plan.  Acute VTE this pregnancy: therapeutic antepartum and at least 6 weeks postpartum.  \n Prophylaxis risk (CMQCC Low / Medium / High) \n  Major (CS algorithm): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH requiring transfusion / further operation / IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72h current or within last month; chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome).  Minor: multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion/further op/IR; family history VTE (1st-degree &lt;50); smoker; preeclampsia.  Cesarean — LOW (not medium/high): mechanical only. MEDIUM (1 major OR ≥2 minor): mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH postpartum until discharge. (CMQCC table “&gt;2 Minor”; house ≥2 per prose/ACCP.) HIGH (high-risk thrombophilia incl APS no prior VTE; prior provoked/idiopathic/estrogen VTE; low-risk thrombophilia + FH VTE OR single prior VTE; already on outpatient LMWH/UFH; multiple prior VTE; prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS): mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks  OR  mechanical + therapeutic (PP dose ≥ antepartum) in hospital and to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; prophylactic often enough for high-risk thrombophilia alone without prior VTE.  Vaginal — LOW (isolated delivery BMI &gt;40, no medium/high criteria): mechanical only; other vaginal without medium/high → early ambulation. MEDIUM (BMI &gt;40 AND [antepartum hosp ≥72h current/past month OR low-risk thrombophilia]): mechanical + prophylactic during PP hospitalization; consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia. HIGH: same high-risk set as CS → mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks  OR  mechanical + therapeutic to 6 weeks (favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; otherwise prophylactic often enough — bleeding risk / hematology).  Definitions: High-risk thrombophilia = ATIII def, APS, FVL or PT homozygous/compound het. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, protein C or S deficiency. Family Hx = 1st-degree VTE before age 50. All CS: ambulate, avoid dehydration, mechanical before CS until ambulatory.  \n Around birth \n  SCDs before every cesarean. Hold prophylactic LMWH ≥12 hours before scheduled induction or cesarean; hold therapeutic ≥24 hours.  Restart no sooner than 4–6 hours after vaginal birth or 6–12 hours after cesarean. After neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH ≥24 hours after the block and ≥4 hours after catheter removal.  Warfarin, LMWH, and UFH are compatible with breastfeeding.  \n Doses \n  Prophylactic enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily; Intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes / hematology — not a CMQCC L/M/H default); Therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (Anti-Xa 0.6–1.0). Hold prophylactic ≥12h / therapeutic ≥24h before scheduled birth.  \n BOTTOM LINE. No D-dimer. Compression US for the leg; CXR then V/Q or CTA for the chest. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg q12h for acute clot. CMQCC risk band (L/M/H) drives postpartum mechanical ± pharmacologic duration. Hold prophylactic 12 hours / therapeutic 24 hours before scheduled birth; SCDs before every cesarean. "}, {"id": "GYNB-FORMULARY", "kind": "card", "title": "Quick reference formulary (GYN)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Confirm vs house: doses are guideline-card extracts. Cross-check the CSM pharmacy build and antibiogram before you hang the bag. Quinolones are not first-line PID drugs. IDSA cUTI rows do not apply in pregnancy. <strong>If the house cart later disagrees, the cart wins.</strong></p>\n<h5>PID / TOA / cuff</h5>\n<ul><li>PID parenteral (CDC 2021): ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h. Finish 14 d PO. TOA: same IV, stay ≥24 h. PCN-severe: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h + gentamicin 2 mg/kg IV load, then 1.5 mg/kg IV q8h or 5 mg/kg IV q24h.</li><li>PID oral step-down: doxycycline 100 mg PO BID + metronidazole 500 mg PO BID to complete 14 d. Ceftriaxone already given as the single IM/IV load (500 mg IM; 1 g IM if ≥150 kg).</li><li>TOA drainage <strong>(house)</strong>: drain if the collection is <strong>≥6 cm</strong> or if there is no regression after 48–72 h of IV therapy. IR-guided drainage first. OR if she is peritonitic, the collection is not accessible, or IR fails.</li><li>Cuff cellulitis <strong>(house)</strong>: prefer <strong>ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole</strong> (same doses as PID). FQ (cipro + metro) = allergy / fallback only.</li></ul>\n<h5>Acute AUB · ectopic · molar</h5>\n<ul><li>Acute AUB, house first-line: TXA 1.3 g PO TID × 5 d (or 10 mg/kg IV, maximum 600 mg, q8h, CO 557, pending pharmacy), <strong>IV CEE 25 mg q4 to 6h × 24 h</strong> then a progestin, and megestrol 80 mg PO q8h, then taper, are all first-line. Pick one primary; add a second if still bleeding. No progestin with active or past VTE (CO 557). Foley tamponade 26F with 30 mL saline (CO 557) if medical therapy is not holding.</li><li>Ectopic MTX (PB 193): single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Need ≥15% hCG drop day 4 to day 7. Two-dose: 50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4; may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG. Multi-dose: MTX 1 mg/kg IM days 1,3,5,7 + leucovorin 0.1 mg/kg days 2,4,6,8. Rho(D) IG if Rh-negative (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies).</li><li>Molar preop: misoprostol 200–400 mcg only to ripen a closed cervix 2–4 h prior — not as evacuation. No oxytocin before the cannula is in.</li></ul>\n<h5>cUTI / stone (not pregnant) · pregnancy · postop</h5>\n<ul><li>cUTI no sepsis (IDSA 2025): ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h, cefepime 2 g IV q8h, piperacillin-tazobactam 3.375–4.5 g IV q6h, or ciprofloxacin 400 mg IV q12h / 500–750 mg PO BID or levofloxacin 750 mg IV/PO daily. Do not reuse a recently failed class. No FQ if that class used in 12 mo. Narrow on culture.</li><li>cUTI + sepsis (IDSA 2025): add a carbapenem: meropenem 1 g IV q8h (or equivalent house carbapenem). Same no-reuse / no-recent-FQ rules.</li><li>cUTI duration if improving: fluoroquinolone 5–7 d or non-fluoroquinolone 7 d from first effective dose. Same 7 d if GN bacteremia and turning. IV→PO when better.</li><li>Infected obstructing stone: antibiotics now (same cUTI picks) + urgent drainage (stent or PCN). Culture kidney urine. Non-surgical management of the stone until sepsis clears.</li><li>Renal colic, not pregnant (AAFP 2019): ketorolac 30–60 mg IM first-line (15–30 mg IV). Tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily for distal 5–10 mm. Ondansetron 4 mg IV/PO. Do not drown in IV fluid.</li><li>Pregnant pyelo / stone: admit pyelo — culture, ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h. No fluoroquinolone. No aminoglycoside unless no alternative. Colic: no NSAID late pregnancy. US first. Observe if controlled.</li><li>Hysterectomy SSI / ERAS: cefazolin 2 g IV (3 g if ≥120 kg) ×1 pre-incision. Add metronidazole 500 mg IV if bowel entered or vaginal hysterectomy. OOB &gt;2 h. Diet + gum at 4 h. Foley out by 24 h. Scheduled APAP + NSAID.</li><li>VTE default postop: SCDs on in OR. Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily when hemostasis OK if Caprini / house pathway says so. Hold 12 h before epidural removal (24 h if treatment dose).</li></ul>", "flags": [], "text": " Confirm vs house: doses are guideline-card extracts. Cross-check the CSM pharmacy build and antibiogram before you hang the bag. Quinolones are not first-line PID drugs. IDSA cUTI rows do not apply in pregnancy.  If the house cart later disagrees, the cart wins.  \n PID / TOA / cuff \n  PID parenteral (CDC 2021): ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h. Finish 14 d PO. TOA: same IV, stay ≥24 h. PCN-severe: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h + gentamicin 2 mg/kg IV load, then 1.5 mg/kg IV q8h or 5 mg/kg IV q24h.  PID oral step-down: doxycycline 100 mg PO BID + metronidazole 500 mg PO BID to complete 14 d. Ceftriaxone already given as the single IM/IV load (500 mg IM; 1 g IM if ≥150 kg).  TOA drainage  (house) : drain if the collection is  ≥6 cm  or if there is no regression after 48–72 h of IV therapy. IR-guided drainage first. OR if she is peritonitic, the collection is not accessible, or IR fails.  Cuff cellulitis  (house) : prefer  ceftriaxone + doxycycline + metronidazole  (same doses as PID). FQ (cipro + metro) = allergy / fallback only.  \n Acute AUB · ectopic · molar \n  Acute AUB, house first-line: TXA 1.3 g PO TID × 5 d (or 10 mg/kg IV, maximum 600 mg, q8h, CO 557, pending pharmacy),  IV CEE 25 mg q4 to 6h × 24 h  then a progestin, and megestrol 80 mg PO q8h, then taper, are all first-line. Pick one primary; add a second if still bleeding. No progestin with active or past VTE (CO 557). Foley tamponade 26F with 30 mL saline (CO 557) if medical therapy is not holding.  Ectopic MTX (PB 193): single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Need ≥15% hCG drop day 4 to day 7. Two-dose: 50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4; may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG. Multi-dose: MTX 1 mg/kg IM days 1,3,5,7 + leucovorin 0.1 mg/kg days 2,4,6,8. Rho(D) IG if Rh-negative (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies).  Molar preop: misoprostol 200–400 mcg only to ripen a closed cervix 2–4 h prior — not as evacuation. No oxytocin before the cannula is in.  \n cUTI / stone (not pregnant) · pregnancy · postop \n  cUTI no sepsis (IDSA 2025): ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h, cefepime 2 g IV q8h, piperacillin-tazobactam 3.375–4.5 g IV q6h, or ciprofloxacin 400 mg IV q12h / 500–750 mg PO BID or levofloxacin 750 mg IV/PO daily. Do not reuse a recently failed class. No FQ if that class used in 12 mo. Narrow on culture.  cUTI + sepsis (IDSA 2025): add a carbapenem: meropenem 1 g IV q8h (or equivalent house carbapenem). Same no-reuse / no-recent-FQ rules.  cUTI duration if improving: fluoroquinolone 5–7 d or non-fluoroquinolone 7 d from first effective dose. Same 7 d if GN bacteremia and turning. IV→PO when better.  Infected obstructing stone: antibiotics now (same cUTI picks) + urgent drainage (stent or PCN). Culture kidney urine. Non-surgical management of the stone until sepsis clears.  Renal colic, not pregnant (AAFP 2019): ketorolac 30–60 mg IM first-line (15–30 mg IV). Tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily for distal 5–10 mm. Ondansetron 4 mg IV/PO. Do not drown in IV fluid.  Pregnant pyelo / stone: admit pyelo — culture, ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h. No fluoroquinolone. No aminoglycoside unless no alternative. Colic: no NSAID late pregnancy. US first. Observe if controlled.  Hysterectomy SSI / ERAS: cefazolin 2 g IV (3 g if ≥120 kg) ×1 pre-incision. Add metronidazole 500 mg IV if bowel entered or vaginal hysterectomy. OOB &gt;2 h. Diet + gum at 4 h. Foley out by 24 h. Scheduled APAP + NSAID.  VTE default postop: SCDs on in OR. Enoxaparin 40 mg SQ daily when hemostasis OK if Caprini / house pathway says so. Hold 12 h before epidural removal (24 h if treatment dose).  ", "display_id": "GYN formulary"}, {"id": "GYNB-01", "kind": "card", "title": "Tubal ectopic / PUL (ACOG PB 193)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Working differential</p>\n<ul><li>Most likely: tubal ectopic vs failing IUP vs resolving PUL.</li><li>Cannot miss: ruptured ectopic, heterotopic after ART, cornual / interstitial, cesarean-scar pregnancy, undiagnosed IUP you are about to MTX.</li><li>Less likely tonight: hemorrhagic corpus luteum, degenerating fibroid, appendicitis, pyelo — still on the list until the hCG and the scan line up.</li></ul>\n<p>Immediate actions vs workup</p>\n<ul><li>Two large-bore IVs, type and screen, CBC, hCG, Rh. Unstable + positive hCG = OR — do not wait for a discriminatory-zone number.</li><li>TVUS first. A yolk-sac or embryo in the uterus clears tubal ectopic except after ART (heterotopic). A hypoechoic intrauterine ‘sac-like’ structure can be a pseudosac.</li><li>Discriminatory zone is conservatively 3,500 mIU/mL (PB 193). Below that, an empty uterus is not a diagnosis.</li><li>Expected IUP rise often ≥49% / 40% / 33% in 48 h at hCG &lt;1,500 / 1,500–3,000 / &gt;3,000. Plateau or fall is a failing pregnancy — location still unknown.</li></ul>\n<p>Methotrexate — only with a day-4/7 plan that will be seen</p>\n<ul><li>Single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Check hCG day 4 and day 7. Need ≥15% drop day 4 → day 7; then weekly until negative.</li><li>If the drop fails: second dose, or surgery. Two-dose (50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4; hCG day 4 and day 7; &lt;15% drop: dose day 7 and hCG day 11; &lt;15% day 7 to 11: dose day 11 and hCG day 14; no decline after 4 doses: surgery) may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG. Multi-dose (MTX 1 mg/kg days 1,3,5,7 + leucovorin 0.1 mg/kg even days) is the third ACOG protocol.</li><li>Do not give MTX if (absolute, PB 193 Box 1): IUP or heterotopic, immunodeficiency, moderate to severe anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia, MTX sensitivity, active pulmonary disease, active PUD, clinically important hepatic or renal dysfunction, breastfeeding, ruptured ectopic, hemodynamic instability, or she cannot follow up.</li><li>Relative contraindications (PB 193 Box 1): embryonic cardiac activity; high initial hCG (failure 14.3% or higher above 5,000 mIU/mL vs 3.7% below); ectopic &gt;4 cm on TVUS; refusal of transfusion. Weigh surgery.</li><li>Hold folic acid, prenatal vitamins, and NSAIDs during therapy. Photosensitivity counseling. Write ‘no intercourse / no PV exam until hCG negative.’</li><li>Rho(D) immune globulin if Rh-negative and unsensitized (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies); dose per CSM pharmacy.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>OR / GYN now</td><td>Peritonitis, shoulder-tip + free fluid, syncope, Hct falling, live extrauterine embryo with +FHR and pain.</td></tr><tr><td>Gyn Onc / MFM</td><td>Interstitial, cervical, cesarean-scar, or abdominal pregnancy. Do not MTX these on the floor.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine / pharmacy</td><td>Cr, AST/ALT, or ANC out of range before MTX; or she is already on a folate-antagonist interaction.</td></tr><tr><td>Social work / CM</td><td>No ride for day-4/7 labs, no phone, unstable housing, Intimate Partner Violence, language barrier. If follow-up is not real, operate.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Unstable → OR. PUL → serial hCG + US, not a single number. MTX only if she is stable AND someone will see the day-4/7 hCG.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Working differential \n  Most likely: tubal ectopic vs failing IUP vs resolving PUL.  Cannot miss: ruptured ectopic, heterotopic after ART, cornual / interstitial, cesarean-scar pregnancy, undiagnosed IUP you are about to MTX.  Less likely tonight: hemorrhagic corpus luteum, degenerating fibroid, appendicitis, pyelo — still on the list until the hCG and the scan line up.  \n Immediate actions vs workup \n  Two large-bore IVs, type and screen, CBC, hCG, Rh. Unstable + positive hCG = OR — do not wait for a discriminatory-zone number.  TVUS first. A yolk-sac or embryo in the uterus clears tubal ectopic except after ART (heterotopic). A hypoechoic intrauterine ‘sac-like’ structure can be a pseudosac.  Discriminatory zone is conservatively 3,500 mIU/mL (PB 193). Below that, an empty uterus is not a diagnosis.  Expected IUP rise often ≥49% / 40% / 33% in 48 h at hCG &lt;1,500 / 1,500–3,000 / &gt;3,000. Plateau or fall is a failing pregnancy — location still unknown.  \n Methotrexate — only with a day-4/7 plan that will be seen \n  Single-dose 50 mg/m² IM day 1. Check hCG day 4 and day 7. Need ≥15% drop day 4 → day 7; then weekly until negative.  If the drop fails: second dose, or surgery. Two-dose (50 mg/m² IM day 1 and day 4; hCG day 4 and day 7; &lt;15% drop: dose day 7 and hCG day 11; &lt;15% day 7 to 11: dose day 11 and hCG day 14; no decline after 4 doses: surgery) may be considered per PB 193; CSM preference two-dose for high hCG. Multi-dose (MTX 1 mg/kg days 1,3,5,7 + leucovorin 0.1 mg/kg even days) is the third ACOG protocol.  Do not give MTX if (absolute, PB 193 Box 1): IUP or heterotopic, immunodeficiency, moderate to severe anemia, leukopenia, or thrombocytopenia, MTX sensitivity, active pulmonary disease, active PUD, clinically important hepatic or renal dysfunction, breastfeeding, ruptured ectopic, hemodynamic instability, or she cannot follow up.  Relative contraindications (PB 193 Box 1): embryonic cardiac activity; high initial hCG (failure 14.3% or higher above 5,000 mIU/mL vs 3.7% below); ectopic &gt;4 cm on TVUS; refusal of transfusion. Weigh surgery.  Hold folic acid, prenatal vitamins, and NSAIDs during therapy. Photosensitivity counseling. Write ‘no intercourse / no PV exam until hCG negative.’  Rho(D) immune globulin if Rh-negative and unsensitized (PB 181: all ectopic pregnancies); dose per CSM pharmacy.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    OR / GYN now  Peritonitis, shoulder-tip + free fluid, syncope, Hct falling, live extrauterine embryo with +FHR and pain.    Gyn Onc / MFM  Interstitial, cervical, cesarean-scar, or abdominal pregnancy. Do not MTX these on the floor.    Medicine / pharmacy  Cr, AST/ALT, or ANC out of range before MTX; or she is already on a folate-antagonist interaction.    Social work / CM  No ride for day-4/7 labs, no phone, unstable housing, Intimate Partner Violence, language barrier. If follow-up is not real, operate.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Unstable → OR. PUL → serial hCG + US, not a single number. MTX only if she is stable AND someone will see the day-4/7 hCG. ", "display_id": "GYN-01 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-02", "kind": "card", "title": "Acute abnormal uterine bleeding (CO 557 + PB 128 / 136 / 228 + house)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Working differential (FIGO PALM-COEIN after she is stable)</p>\n<ul><li>PALM (structural): Polyp · Adenomyosis · Leiomyoma · Malignancy / hyperplasia.</li><li>COEIN (non-structural): Coagulopathy · Ovulatory dysfunction · Endometrial · Iatrogenic · Not otherwise classified.</li><li>Most likely on this service: ovulatory dysfunction, fibroid / polyp, breakthrough on a new method, endometritis / retained tissue if recently pregnant.</li><li>Cannot miss: pregnancy (ectopic, miscarriage, mole), coagulopathy (vWD, ITP, anticoagulant), cervical / endometrial cancer, trauma / laceration, severe anemia with high-output strain.</li></ul>\n<p>Stabilize, sample, medical first</p>\n<ul><li>Pregnancy test first, every time. Unstable: two large-bore IVs, type and cross, CBC, coags if history suggests, TSH later.</li><li>Exam: laceration, polyp at the os, products, cervical mass. Bimanual for uterus size and adnexa.</li><li>Sample the endometrium if ≥45, or &lt;45 with unopposed estrogen, failed medical therapy, persistent AUB, or obesity / PCOS with years of amenorrhea then heavy bleeding. Pipelle first if available; hysteroscopy beats blind D&amp;C (CO 557).</li></ul>\n<p>Pharmacologic control — TXA, CEE, and megestrol are all first-line</p>\n<p>House first-line (any one is acceptable to start). TXA, <strong>IV CEE</strong>, and megestrol. Pick the agent that matches her contraindications. Add a second first-line agent if she is still bleeding at 4–6 h. Combined OC TID and MPA remain on the card as alternatives when the first-line three are a poor fit.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Regimen</th><th>How</th><th>Do not use if</th></tr><tr><td>TXA, first-line</td><td>1.3 g PO TID × 5 d, or 10 mg/kg IV (maximum 600 mg/dose) q8h (CO 557; pending pharmacy). May combine with CEE or megestrol.</td><td>Current thrombotic disease or acquired color-vision change; caution with past thrombosis and with a combined OC (CO 557).</td></tr><tr><td>IV CEE — first-line</td><td>25 mg IV q4–6h up to 24 h, then a progestin the same day she stops bleeding.</td><td>VTE, MI, stroke, active breast cancer, uncontrolled migraine with aura, smoker &gt;35.</td></tr><tr><td>Megestrol, first-line</td><td>80 mg PO q8h until bleeding slows, then taper to a maintenance progestin (CSM house dose).</td><td>Active or past VTE (CO 557, progestins).</td></tr><tr><td>Combined OC</td><td>35 mcg ethinyl estradiol TID × 7 d, then taper to daily. Antiemetic on board. Alternative, not required first.</td><td>Same estrogen contraindications as CEE.</td></tr><tr><td>MPA</td><td>20 mg PO TID × 7 d (CO 557). Alternate when megestrol is not on the floor.</td><td>Active or past DVT or PE; active or recent arterial thromboembolism; breast cancer; liver disease (CO 557).</td></tr><tr><td>Tamponade</td><td>26F Foley in the cavity, balloon infused with 30 mL saline (CO 557).</td><td>Suspected perforation or pyometra.</td></tr></table>\n<ul><li>Once the bleeding stops, transition to a maintenance method the same admission (LNG-IUD, cyclic or continuous progestin, or combined method if estrogen-eligible). Do not send her out on nothing.</li><li>Leiomyoma (PB 228): medical options exist (hormone, TXA, GnRH analogue / antagonist pathways) — not started at 02:00. Acute bleed still follows this card first.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>IR</td><td>Medical therapy failing and she is a poor OR candidate, or you want uterine artery embolization discussed before hysterectomy.</td></tr><tr><td>Gyn Onc</td><td>Postmenopausal bleeding with a mass, hyperplasia with atypia already on file, or obvious cervical cancer.</td></tr><tr><td>Hematology</td><td>Known vWD / platelet disorder, aPTT up, or heavy bleeding from menarche that was never worked up.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Decompensated heart failure, severe anemia with chest pain, or estrogen is the only option and she has VTE risk.</td></tr><tr><td>Social work / CM</td><td>No insurance for LNG-IUD, Intimate Partner Violence (bleeding after trauma), or she cannot fill a 5-day TXA / megestrol pack. Screen Intimate Partner Violence on every acute AUB.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Pregnancy test. Resuscitate. TXA, CEE, or megestrol first. Sample if ≥45 or unopposed estrogen. Do not send her home on no maintenance method.</p>\n<p>IV tranexamic acid 10 mg/kg (maximum 600 mg per dose) q8h (CO 557 Table 2): confirm with CSM pharmacy before use; not yet in the CSM pharmacy build.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Working differential (FIGO PALM-COEIN after she is stable) \n  PALM (structural): Polyp · Adenomyosis · Leiomyoma · Malignancy / hyperplasia.  COEIN (non-structural): Coagulopathy · Ovulatory dysfunction · Endometrial · Iatrogenic · Not otherwise classified.  Most likely on this service: ovulatory dysfunction, fibroid / polyp, breakthrough on a new method, endometritis / retained tissue if recently pregnant.  Cannot miss: pregnancy (ectopic, miscarriage, mole), coagulopathy (vWD, ITP, anticoagulant), cervical / endometrial cancer, trauma / laceration, severe anemia with high-output strain.  \n Stabilize, sample, medical first \n  Pregnancy test first, every time. Unstable: two large-bore IVs, type and cross, CBC, coags if history suggests, TSH later.  Exam: laceration, polyp at the os, products, cervical mass. Bimanual for uterus size and adnexa.  Sample the endometrium if ≥45, or &lt;45 with unopposed estrogen, failed medical therapy, persistent AUB, or obesity / PCOS with years of amenorrhea then heavy bleeding. Pipelle first if available; hysteroscopy beats blind D&amp;C (CO 557).  \n Pharmacologic control — TXA, CEE, and megestrol are all first-line \n House first-line (any one is acceptable to start). TXA,  IV CEE , and megestrol. Pick the agent that matches her contraindications. Add a second first-line agent if she is still bleeding at 4–6 h. Combined OC TID and MPA remain on the card as alternatives when the first-line three are a poor fit. \n   Regimen  How  Do not use if    TXA, first-line  1.3 g PO TID × 5 d, or 10 mg/kg IV (maximum 600 mg/dose) q8h (CO 557; pending pharmacy). May combine with CEE or megestrol.  Current thrombotic disease or acquired color-vision change; caution with past thrombosis and with a combined OC (CO 557).    IV CEE — first-line  25 mg IV q4–6h up to 24 h, then a progestin the same day she stops bleeding.  VTE, MI, stroke, active breast cancer, uncontrolled migraine with aura, smoker &gt;35.    Megestrol, first-line  80 mg PO q8h until bleeding slows, then taper to a maintenance progestin (CSM house dose).  Active or past VTE (CO 557, progestins).    Combined OC  35 mcg ethinyl estradiol TID × 7 d, then taper to daily. Antiemetic on board. Alternative, not required first.  Same estrogen contraindications as CEE.    MPA  20 mg PO TID × 7 d (CO 557). Alternate when megestrol is not on the floor.  Active or past DVT or PE; active or recent arterial thromboembolism; breast cancer; liver disease (CO 557).    Tamponade  26F Foley in the cavity, balloon infused with 30 mL saline (CO 557).  Suspected perforation or pyometra.   \n  Once the bleeding stops, transition to a maintenance method the same admission (LNG-IUD, cyclic or continuous progestin, or combined method if estrogen-eligible). Do not send her out on nothing.  Leiomyoma (PB 228): medical options exist (hormone, TXA, GnRH analogue / antagonist pathways) — not started at 02:00. Acute bleed still follows this card first.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    IR  Medical therapy failing and she is a poor OR candidate, or you want uterine artery embolization discussed before hysterectomy.    Gyn Onc  Postmenopausal bleeding with a mass, hyperplasia with atypia already on file, or obvious cervical cancer.    Hematology  Known vWD / platelet disorder, aPTT up, or heavy bleeding from menarche that was never worked up.    Medicine  Decompensated heart failure, severe anemia with chest pain, or estrogen is the only option and she has VTE risk.    Social work / CM  No insurance for LNG-IUD, Intimate Partner Violence (bleeding after trauma), or she cannot fill a 5-day TXA / megestrol pack. Screen Intimate Partner Violence on every acute AUB.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Pregnancy test. Resuscitate. TXA, CEE, or megestrol first. Sample if ≥45 or unopposed estrogen. Do not send her home on no maintenance method.  IV tranexamic acid 10 mg/kg (maximum 600 mg per dose) q8h (CO 557 Table 2): confirm with CSM pharmacy before use; not yet in the CSM pharmacy build. ", "display_id": "GYN-02 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-03", "kind": "card", "title": "PID / tubo-ovarian abscess (CDC STI 2021 + house drainage)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Treat on a low threshold</p>\n<ul><li>Sexually active + pelvic pain + no other cause + one of: cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness. Fever, discharge, WBC, ESR/CRP, and GC/CT are supportive — not required.</li><li>Cannot miss: appendicitis, torsion, ectopic, pyelo, diverticulitis. If a surgical emergency is not excluded, she is an admission, not an IM ceftriaxone and a bus pass.</li><li>Test GC/CT NAAT, HIV, RPR. Pregnancy test. Offer trichomonas testing. Do not wait on results to treat.</li></ul>\n<p>Who stays</p>\n<ul><li>Surgical emergency not excluded · TOA · pregnancy · T &gt;38.5 °C or vomiting · cannot take or tolerate orals · failed outpatient · unreliable follow-up · severe illness.</li><li>TOA stays ≥24 h on IV therapy.</li></ul>\n<p>TOA drainage — house rule</p>\n<p>Drain if the collection is <strong>≥6 cm</strong>, or if there is no clinical / imaging regression after 48–72 h of IV antibiotics. IR-guided drainage is preferred. Go to the OR if she is peritonitic, the collection is not IR-accessible, or IR fails.</p>\n<p>Drugs</p>\n<ul><li>Inpatient preferred: ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h. Step down doxy + metro PO to finish 14 days.</li><li>PCN-severe: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h + gentamicin (2 mg/kg IV load, then 1.5 mg/kg IV q8h or 5 mg/kg IV q24h). Step down clinda + doxy or amox-clav per CDC table.</li><li>Outpatient (only if none of the admit criteria): ceftriaxone 500 mg IM (≥150 kg: 1 g) once + doxycycline 100 mg PO BID × 14 d + metronidazole 500 mg PO BID × 14 d.</li><li>Quinolone regimens are fallback only — not first-line on this card.</li><li>IUD in situ: treat, leave the device, reassess 48–72 h. Remove only if she is not improving. Actinomyces on cytology and no symptoms: leave the IUD, do not treat.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>IR first</td><td>TOA ≥6 cm at presentation, or no regression at 48–72 h. Prefer image-guided drainage over an upfront trip to the OR.</td></tr><tr><td>OR</td><td>Peritonitis, suspected rupture, IR not accessible / not available, or she fails drainage.</td></tr><tr><td>General surgery</td><td>Appendicitis or diverticulitis not excluded. Do not own a right-lower-quadrant puzzle overnight without them.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine / ID</td><td>Bacteremia, septic shock, or a drug-resistant isolate on the antibiogram.</td></tr><tr><td>Health dept / CM</td><td>Reportable GC/CT. Treat partners from the prior 60 days. Expedited partner therapy where Texas law allows. HIV PrEP / contraception before discharge.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. One tender finding is enough. CTX + doxy + metro. Leave the IUD. Drain a TOA that is ≥6 cm or not turning at 48–72 h — IR first.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Treat on a low threshold \n  Sexually active + pelvic pain + no other cause + one of: cervical motion, uterine, or adnexal tenderness. Fever, discharge, WBC, ESR/CRP, and GC/CT are supportive — not required.  Cannot miss: appendicitis, torsion, ectopic, pyelo, diverticulitis. If a surgical emergency is not excluded, she is an admission, not an IM ceftriaxone and a bus pass.  Test GC/CT NAAT, HIV, RPR. Pregnancy test. Offer trichomonas testing. Do not wait on results to treat.  \n Who stays \n  Surgical emergency not excluded · TOA · pregnancy · T &gt;38.5 °C or vomiting · cannot take or tolerate orals · failed outpatient · unreliable follow-up · severe illness.  TOA stays ≥24 h on IV therapy.  \n TOA drainage — house rule \n Drain if the collection is  ≥6 cm , or if there is no clinical / imaging regression after 48–72 h of IV antibiotics. IR-guided drainage is preferred. Go to the OR if she is peritonitic, the collection is not IR-accessible, or IR fails. \n Drugs \n  Inpatient preferred: ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h. Step down doxy + metro PO to finish 14 days.  PCN-severe: clindamycin 900 mg IV q8h + gentamicin (2 mg/kg IV load, then 1.5 mg/kg IV q8h or 5 mg/kg IV q24h). Step down clinda + doxy or amox-clav per CDC table.  Outpatient (only if none of the admit criteria): ceftriaxone 500 mg IM (≥150 kg: 1 g) once + doxycycline 100 mg PO BID × 14 d + metronidazole 500 mg PO BID × 14 d.  Quinolone regimens are fallback only — not first-line on this card.  IUD in situ: treat, leave the device, reassess 48–72 h. Remove only if she is not improving. Actinomyces on cytology and no symptoms: leave the IUD, do not treat.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    IR first  TOA ≥6 cm at presentation, or no regression at 48–72 h. Prefer image-guided drainage over an upfront trip to the OR.    OR  Peritonitis, suspected rupture, IR not accessible / not available, or she fails drainage.    General surgery  Appendicitis or diverticulitis not excluded. Do not own a right-lower-quadrant puzzle overnight without them.    Medicine / ID  Bacteremia, septic shock, or a drug-resistant isolate on the antibiogram.    Health dept / CM  Reportable GC/CT. Treat partners from the prior 60 days. Expedited partner therapy where Texas law allows. HIV PrEP / contraception before discharge.   \n BOTTOM LINE. One tender finding is enough. CTX + doxy + metro. Leave the IUD. Drain a TOA that is ≥6 cm or not turning at 48–72 h — IR first. ", "display_id": "GYN-03 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-04", "kind": "card", "title": "Adnexal torsion (ACOG PB 174)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Call it clinically, then keep the ovary</p>\n<ul><li>Pregnancy test first. Classic: unilateral, intermittent, then suddenly worse, nausea out of proportion. A known cyst or dermoid raises the prior.</li><li>Present Doppler does not exclude torsion. Intermittent torsion perfuses between twists. Do not delay the OR for a ‘better’ study or an MRI.</li><li>US findings that help but do not rule out: enlarged ovary, peripheral follicles, free fluid, twisted pedicle (‘whirlpool’). A normal contralateral ovary with an acutely large painful ovary is enough to go.</li><li>Adults: untwist and perform cystectomy if a cyst is the lead point (PB 174). Adolescents: detorse and manage conservatively; cystectomy is not needed at detorsion; consider draining a large cyst and reimage later (CO 783). A dark or blue-black ovary is still conserved; most reperfuse. Oophorectomy is for necrosis that is clearly nonviable after untwist, or for a frankly malignant-appearing mass (then Gyn Onc).</li><li>Isolated paraovarian or paratubal cysts and hydrosalpinges can twist. Same rule: detorse and keep the ovary; remove the lead point in adults; adolescents per CO 783.</li><li>Pregnancy: still detorse. Corpus luteum removed before 10 weeks: micronized progesterone (Prometrium) 200 mg vaginally qhs until 10 to 12 weeks.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>OR now</td><td>High clinical suspicion. Do not ‘observe overnight’ a worsening unilateral syndrome to see if Doppler improves.</td></tr><tr><td>General surgery</td><td>Appendicitis remains live after the pregnancy test and the pelvic US.</td></tr><tr><td>Gyn Onc</td><td>Solid mass, ascites, or metastatic implants at laparoscopy — do not morcellate.</td></tr><tr><td>Pediatrics / peds surg</td><td>Pre-menarchal torsion. Same conserve-the-ovary rule.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Pain plus a mass: exclude pregnancy, then torsion. Present Doppler does not clear her. Untwist first; the dark ovary usually stays.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Call it clinically, then keep the ovary \n  Pregnancy test first. Classic: unilateral, intermittent, then suddenly worse, nausea out of proportion. A known cyst or dermoid raises the prior.  Present Doppler does not exclude torsion. Intermittent torsion perfuses between twists. Do not delay the OR for a ‘better’ study or an MRI.  US findings that help but do not rule out: enlarged ovary, peripheral follicles, free fluid, twisted pedicle (‘whirlpool’). A normal contralateral ovary with an acutely large painful ovary is enough to go.  Adults: untwist and perform cystectomy if a cyst is the lead point (PB 174). Adolescents: detorse and manage conservatively; cystectomy is not needed at detorsion; consider draining a large cyst and reimage later (CO 783). A dark or blue-black ovary is still conserved; most reperfuse. Oophorectomy is for necrosis that is clearly nonviable after untwist, or for a frankly malignant-appearing mass (then Gyn Onc).  Isolated paraovarian or paratubal cysts and hydrosalpinges can twist. Same rule: detorse and keep the ovary; remove the lead point in adults; adolescents per CO 783.  Pregnancy: still detorse. Corpus luteum removed before 10 weeks: micronized progesterone (Prometrium) 200 mg vaginally qhs until 10 to 12 weeks.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    OR now  High clinical suspicion. Do not ‘observe overnight’ a worsening unilateral syndrome to see if Doppler improves.    General surgery  Appendicitis remains live after the pregnancy test and the pelvic US.    Gyn Onc  Solid mass, ascites, or metastatic implants at laparoscopy — do not morcellate.    Pediatrics / peds surg  Pre-menarchal torsion. Same conserve-the-ovary rule.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Pain plus a mass: exclude pregnancy, then torsion. Present Doppler does not clear her. Untwist first; the dark ovary usually stays. ", "display_id": "GYN-04 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-05", "kind": "card", "title": "Adnexal mass (not torsion) (ACOG PB 174)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Imaging first, markers second</p>\n<ul><li>TVUS is the first test. Concerning: &gt;10 cm, papillary or solid nodules, irregular thick septa, ascites, high color flow, nodular omentum.</li><li>Simple unilocular cyst ≤10 cm may be watched even after menopause if the scan is high quality and she is asymptomatic (PB 174). Repeat US in 3 months is a reasonable default; earlier if pain.</li><li>Hemorrhagic cyst and dermoid have characteristic US looks — do not send every one of them to Gyn Onc.</li><li>Ruptured hemorrhagic cyst (CSM house pathway): hemodynamically stable, characteristic US appearance, no torsion concern: observe, analgesia, serial Hgb and vitals. Falling Hgb, worsening hemoperitoneum, or instability: OR. Torsion not excluded: manage as torsion (GYN-04).</li><li>CA-125 is not a screening test. Use it to triage a mass that already looks concerning, especially after menopause. A normal CA-125 does not clear a complex mass. In premenopause it rises with endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, and menses.</li><li>Do not aspirate an undiagnosed adnexal mass. Cytology from a tap does not reliably exclude cancer and spills the cyst.</li></ul>\n<p>Who needs Gyn Onc (PB 174 / SGO-style referral)</p>\n<ul><li>Postmenopausal + elevated CA-125 · malignant US features · ascites · nodular or fixed pelvis · evidence of metastasis · first-degree ovarian / breast / Lynch story with a complex mass.</li><li>If you are already in the pelvis and it looks malignant: freeze, washings, and call Gyn Onc. Do not morcellate an unknown solid mass in a bag at 02:00.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>Gyn Onc</td><td>Any referral-criteria mass. Better a phone call than a surprise stage III.</td></tr><tr><td>General surgery</td><td>The ‘mass’ may be appendiceal, diverticular, or a Krukenberg from an unknown GI primary.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>Massive ascites with respiratory compromise, VTE on the same scan, or she is too sick for even a diagnostic laparoscopy.</td></tr><tr><td>CM / navigator</td><td>Uninsured postmenopausal mass — book Gyn Onc before discharge, not ‘follow up with your gynecologist.’</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Simple cysts ≤10 cm can be observed. Malignant features or postmenopausal + high CA-125 → Gyn Onc. Do not aspirate.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Imaging first, markers second \n  TVUS is the first test. Concerning: &gt;10 cm, papillary or solid nodules, irregular thick septa, ascites, high color flow, nodular omentum.  Simple unilocular cyst ≤10 cm may be watched even after menopause if the scan is high quality and she is asymptomatic (PB 174). Repeat US in 3 months is a reasonable default; earlier if pain.  Hemorrhagic cyst and dermoid have characteristic US looks — do not send every one of them to Gyn Onc.  Ruptured hemorrhagic cyst (CSM house pathway): hemodynamically stable, characteristic US appearance, no torsion concern: observe, analgesia, serial Hgb and vitals. Falling Hgb, worsening hemoperitoneum, or instability: OR. Torsion not excluded: manage as torsion (GYN-04).  CA-125 is not a screening test. Use it to triage a mass that already looks concerning, especially after menopause. A normal CA-125 does not clear a complex mass. In premenopause it rises with endometriosis, adenomyosis, fibroids, and menses.  Do not aspirate an undiagnosed adnexal mass. Cytology from a tap does not reliably exclude cancer and spills the cyst.  \n Who needs Gyn Onc (PB 174 / SGO-style referral) \n  Postmenopausal + elevated CA-125 · malignant US features · ascites · nodular or fixed pelvis · evidence of metastasis · first-degree ovarian / breast / Lynch story with a complex mass.  If you are already in the pelvis and it looks malignant: freeze, washings, and call Gyn Onc. Do not morcellate an unknown solid mass in a bag at 02:00.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    Gyn Onc  Any referral-criteria mass. Better a phone call than a surprise stage III.    General surgery  The ‘mass’ may be appendiceal, diverticular, or a Krukenberg from an unknown GI primary.    Medicine  Massive ascites with respiratory compromise, VTE on the same scan, or she is too sick for even a diagnostic laparoscopy.    CM / navigator  Uninsured postmenopausal mass — book Gyn Onc before discharge, not ‘follow up with your gynecologist.’   \n BOTTOM LINE. Simple cysts ≤10 cm can be observed. Malignant features or postmenopausal + high CA-125 → Gyn Onc. Do not aspirate. ", "display_id": "GYN-05 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-06", "kind": "card", "title": "Pyelo / cUTI / obstructing stone (IDSA 2025 + AAFP 2019)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Four-step empiric pick (IDSA 2025, not pregnant)</p>\n<ul><li>Sepsis: ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h, cefepime 2 g IV q8h, piperacillin-tazobactam 3.375–4.5 g IV q6h, meropenem 1 g IV q8h, or ciprofloxacin 400 mg IV q12h / levofloxacin 750 mg IV daily.</li><li>No sepsis: drop the carbapenem from the first-pass list.</li><li>Do not reuse an agent that previously grew resistant in her urine. Do not use a fluoroquinolone if that class was used in the last 12 months.</li><li>Narrow on culture. IV→PO when she is improving and an effective oral option exists — including Gram-negative bacteremia.</li><li>If improving: fluoroquinolone 5–7 days or non-fluoroquinolone 7 days from the first effective dose.</li></ul>\n<p>Infected obstruction and colic</p>\n<ul><li>UTI + obstructing stone: antibiotics now + urgent drainage (ureteral stent or PCN — neither preferred). Culture the kidney urine. Non-surgical management of the stone until sepsis clears.</li><li>Immediate urology: sepsis, anuria, bilateral obstruction, UTI + obstruction, solitary kidney, pregnancy, age &gt;60 with arteriopathy, analgesia failing (AAFP 2019).</li><li>Not pregnant, no infection: ketorolac 30–60 mg IM (15–30 mg IV) first-line — better than opioids. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily for distal 5–10 mm. Stone &gt;10 mm or significant hydro → urology. Reimage by 14 days if she did not pass it.</li><li>Increasing IV fluid does not treat colic. Nifedipine is a weak MET. Steroids do not help passage.</li></ul>\n<p>Pregnancy</p>\n<ul><li>Pyelo: admit, culture, ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h. No fluoroquinolone. No aminoglycoside unless there is no alternative. MFM if septic or obstructed.</li><li>Stone: US first. Observe if symptoms are controlled. Failed observation: URS or a frequently changed stent / PCN. No NSAID in late pregnancy. Tamsulosin is off-label and thinly studied.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>Urology</td><td>Any immediate-trigger row above. Do not ‘culture and wait’ an infected obstructed kidney.</td></tr><tr><td>IR</td><td>PCN preferred because of anatomy, uncorrected coagulopathy for a stent, or urology asks.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine / ICU</td><td>Septic shock, lactate not clearing, or she needs a pressor.</td></tr><tr><td>MFM</td><td>Pregnant + sepsis, obstructed kidney, or recurrent pyelo.</td></tr><tr><td>CM</td><td>Recurrent stone / rUTI — book urology follow-up and a metabolic workup, not just another 7-day script.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Culture first. Four-step pick. Infected obstruction = antibiotics + drain.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Four-step empiric pick (IDSA 2025, not pregnant) \n  Sepsis: ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h, cefepime 2 g IV q8h, piperacillin-tazobactam 3.375–4.5 g IV q6h, meropenem 1 g IV q8h, or ciprofloxacin 400 mg IV q12h / levofloxacin 750 mg IV daily.  No sepsis: drop the carbapenem from the first-pass list.  Do not reuse an agent that previously grew resistant in her urine. Do not use a fluoroquinolone if that class was used in the last 12 months.  Narrow on culture. IV→PO when she is improving and an effective oral option exists — including Gram-negative bacteremia.  If improving: fluoroquinolone 5–7 days or non-fluoroquinolone 7 days from the first effective dose.  \n Infected obstruction and colic \n  UTI + obstructing stone: antibiotics now + urgent drainage (ureteral stent or PCN — neither preferred). Culture the kidney urine. Non-surgical management of the stone until sepsis clears.  Immediate urology: sepsis, anuria, bilateral obstruction, UTI + obstruction, solitary kidney, pregnancy, age &gt;60 with arteriopathy, analgesia failing (AAFP 2019).  Not pregnant, no infection: ketorolac 30–60 mg IM (15–30 mg IV) first-line — better than opioids. Tamsulosin 0.4 mg daily for distal 5–10 mm. Stone &gt;10 mm or significant hydro → urology. Reimage by 14 days if she did not pass it.  Increasing IV fluid does not treat colic. Nifedipine is a weak MET. Steroids do not help passage.  \n Pregnancy \n  Pyelo: admit, culture, ceftriaxone 1–2 g IV q24h. No fluoroquinolone. No aminoglycoside unless there is no alternative. MFM if septic or obstructed.  Stone: US first. Observe if symptoms are controlled. Failed observation: URS or a frequently changed stent / PCN. No NSAID in late pregnancy. Tamsulosin is off-label and thinly studied.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    Urology  Any immediate-trigger row above. Do not ‘culture and wait’ an infected obstructed kidney.    IR  PCN preferred because of anatomy, uncorrected coagulopathy for a stent, or urology asks.    Medicine / ICU  Septic shock, lactate not clearing, or she needs a pressor.    MFM  Pregnant + sepsis, obstructed kidney, or recurrent pyelo.    CM  Recurrent stone / rUTI — book urology follow-up and a metabolic workup, not just another 7-day script.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Culture first. Four-step pick. Infected obstruction = antibiotics + drain. ", "display_id": "GYN-06 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-07", "kind": "card", "title": "IUD / implant emergencies (PB 186 + CO 672 + CDC)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Missing strings</p>\n<ul><li>Pregnancy test first. If pregnant → GYN-01 logic plus the IUD rules below.</li><li>Not pregnant: cytobrush or IUD hook in clinic / ED if you can see the cervix and she is comfortable. If strings still missing: TVUS. If the device is not in the uterus, film from costal margin to symphysis (or abdominal US) looking for an intraperitoneal device.</li><li>Intraperitoneal or embedded IUD: elective laparoscopic retrieval. Counsel that intra-abdominal copper and LNG devices can cause adhesions — still not an overnight emergency if she is well.</li></ul>\n<p>Pregnancy with an IUD in situ</p>\n<ul><li>Rule out ectopic — pregnancies that occur with an IUD are ectopic more often than unplanned pregnancies without one (PB 193 / 186).</li><li>If the pregnancy is intrauterine and strings are visible (or the device is in the cervix), remove the IUD. Leaving it in raises miscarriage, PTL, and infection risk.</li><li>If strings are not visible, do not go fishing blindly in a desired pregnancy. US localization; leave in situ if it is high and she wants the pregnancy, with close surveillance.</li></ul>\n<p>PID and Actinomyces</p>\n<ul><li>PID: CDC regimen, leave the IUD, reassess 48–72 h (see GYN-03).</li><li>Actinomyces on cytology, asymptomatic: leave the device, no antibiotics.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>GYN OR / laparoscopy</td><td>Known intraperitoneal device with pain, or failed clinic removal of an embedded device.</td></tr><tr><td>General surgery</td><td>Suspected bowel or bladder injury, or the implant is in the neurovascular bundle.</td></tr><tr><td>Family planning / LARC champion</td><td>Difficult removal or she wants same-day replacement.</td></tr><tr><td>CM / Title X</td><td>Device removed and she has no method. Do not discharge on no contraception.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Missing strings: pregnancy test, then US, then film. Pregnant + visible strings → pull. PID: treat and leave the IUD.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Missing strings \n  Pregnancy test first. If pregnant → GYN-01 logic plus the IUD rules below.  Not pregnant: cytobrush or IUD hook in clinic / ED if you can see the cervix and she is comfortable. If strings still missing: TVUS. If the device is not in the uterus, film from costal margin to symphysis (or abdominal US) looking for an intraperitoneal device.  Intraperitoneal or embedded IUD: elective laparoscopic retrieval. Counsel that intra-abdominal copper and LNG devices can cause adhesions — still not an overnight emergency if she is well.  \n Pregnancy with an IUD in situ \n  Rule out ectopic — pregnancies that occur with an IUD are ectopic more often than unplanned pregnancies without one (PB 193 / 186).  If the pregnancy is intrauterine and strings are visible (or the device is in the cervix), remove the IUD. Leaving it in raises miscarriage, PTL, and infection risk.  If strings are not visible, do not go fishing blindly in a desired pregnancy. US localization; leave in situ if it is high and she wants the pregnancy, with close surveillance.  \n PID and Actinomyces \n  PID: CDC regimen, leave the IUD, reassess 48–72 h (see GYN-03).  Actinomyces on cytology, asymptomatic: leave the device, no antibiotics.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    GYN OR / laparoscopy  Known intraperitoneal device with pain, or failed clinic removal of an embedded device.    General surgery  Suspected bowel or bladder injury, or the implant is in the neurovascular bundle.    Family planning / LARC champion  Difficult removal or she wants same-day replacement.    CM / Title X  Device removed and she has no method. Do not discharge on no contraception.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Missing strings: pregnancy test, then US, then film. Pregnant + visible strings → pull. PID: treat and leave the IUD. ", "display_id": "GYN-07 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-08", "kind": "card", "title": "Chronic pelvic pain (ACOG PB 218 + house PT)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Recommendations</p>\n<ul><li>Level A: routine laparoscopic adhesiolysis is not recommended for CPP. Do not book a diagnostic lap at 02:00 to ‘lyse adhesions for pain.’</li><li>Level B: pelvic floor physical therapy, CBT / pain psychology, and selected neuromodulators (SNRI, gabapentin; nortriptyline combinations in neuropathic phenotypes) have evidence. Hormonal suppression if there is a cyclic / endometriosis phenotype.</li><li>Opioids are not first-line. If someone already has them, do not escalate in the ED; use the CDC opioid rules and get her into a multimodal clinic.</li><li>Yoga / acupuncture may be considered for musculoskeletal phenotypes (PB 218).</li><li>Screen: Intimate Partner Violence, depression, pelvic floor myalgia, bladder pain syndrome, IBS, hip / back source. Treat what you find. A normal lap does not mean the pain is not real.</li></ul>\n<p>Pelvic-floor PT — house rule</p>\n<p>Pelvic-floor PT is highly effective for CPP without defined pathology. Place the outpatient referral before discharge. Do not send her out with ‘follow up with your gynecologist’ as the only plan.</p>\n<p>Pharmacologic support that is reasonable on the floor</p>\n<ul><li>Scheduled APAP + NSAID if no contraindication. Heat.</li><li>Neuropathic phenotype: gabapentin titrate (e.g. 100–300 mg QHS → TID as tolerated) or duloxetine 30 → 60 mg daily. Warn about sedation and falls.</li><li>Cyclic phenotype: continuous combined method or progestin (NET, DMPA, LNG-IUD) once red flags are down. GnRH analogue is not an ED start.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>Pelvic-floor PT — before discharge</td><td>CPP without a new surgical diagnosis. This is the default disposition, not an afterthought.</td></tr><tr><td>GI / urology / pain</td><td>Dominant bowel or bladder phenotype, or she is already on high-dose opioids.</td></tr><tr><td>Gyn only if</td><td>A new mass, failed adequate medical therapy for likely endometriosis and she wants surgery with a plan — not tonight.</td></tr><tr><td>Social work / behavioral</td><td>Intimate Partner Violence, opioid misuse, no ride to PT, or she has been to three EDs this month. Multimodal clinic referral rides with the PT order.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Exam and pelvic-floor PT before another laparoscopy. Referral goes in before discharge. No opioids as the plan. Do not lyse adhesions for pain (Level A).</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Recommendations \n  Level A: routine laparoscopic adhesiolysis is not recommended for CPP. Do not book a diagnostic lap at 02:00 to ‘lyse adhesions for pain.’  Level B: pelvic floor physical therapy, CBT / pain psychology, and selected neuromodulators (SNRI, gabapentin; nortriptyline combinations in neuropathic phenotypes) have evidence. Hormonal suppression if there is a cyclic / endometriosis phenotype.  Opioids are not first-line. If someone already has them, do not escalate in the ED; use the CDC opioid rules and get her into a multimodal clinic.  Yoga / acupuncture may be considered for musculoskeletal phenotypes (PB 218).  Screen: Intimate Partner Violence, depression, pelvic floor myalgia, bladder pain syndrome, IBS, hip / back source. Treat what you find. A normal lap does not mean the pain is not real.  \n Pelvic-floor PT — house rule \n Pelvic-floor PT is highly effective for CPP without defined pathology. Place the outpatient referral before discharge. Do not send her out with ‘follow up with your gynecologist’ as the only plan. \n Pharmacologic support that is reasonable on the floor \n  Scheduled APAP + NSAID if no contraindication. Heat.  Neuropathic phenotype: gabapentin titrate (e.g. 100–300 mg QHS → TID as tolerated) or duloxetine 30 → 60 mg daily. Warn about sedation and falls.  Cyclic phenotype: continuous combined method or progestin (NET, DMPA, LNG-IUD) once red flags are down. GnRH analogue is not an ED start.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    Pelvic-floor PT — before discharge  CPP without a new surgical diagnosis. This is the default disposition, not an afterthought.    GI / urology / pain  Dominant bowel or bladder phenotype, or she is already on high-dose opioids.    Gyn only if  A new mass, failed adequate medical therapy for likely endometriosis and she wants surgery with a plan — not tonight.    Social work / behavioral  Intimate Partner Violence, opioid misuse, no ride to PT, or she has been to three EDs this month. Multimodal clinic referral rides with the PT order.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Exam and pelvic-floor PT before another laparoscopy. Referral goes in before discharge. No opioids as the plan. Do not lyse adhesions for pain (Level A). ", "display_id": "GYN-08 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-09", "kind": "card", "title": "Post-hysterectomy + delayed complications (CO 750 + PB 195)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Enhanced Recovery after Surgery (ERAS) night</p>\n<ul><li>Out of bed &gt;2 h the day of surgery. Diet and gum at 4 h if not nauseated. Foley out by 24 h unless a urogynecologic reason exists.</li><li>Scheduled APAP + NSAID. Opioid is the rescue, not the basal. Ondansetron / scopolamine per house ERAS. Incentive spirometry.</li><li>Prophylactic cefazolin was one pre-incision dose (2 g IV; 3 g IV if ≥120 kg). Add metronidazole 500 mg IV if the bowel was entered or it was a vaginal hysterectomy per PB 195. Do not continue cefazolin as ‘treatment’ because the temperature is 38.0 on POD 0.</li><li>SCDs on. Pharmacologic prophylaxis when hemostasis is acceptable and Caprini / house pathway says so.</li></ul>\n<p>Fever, cuff, ureter, bowel, VTE</p>\n<ul><li>POD 0–1 fever is usually atelectasis / cytokine. Examine the calf, lungs, cuff, and urine. Do not CT everyone at 18 hours.</li><li>Cuff cellulitis (POD 4–10, low-grade fever, cuff tender, no peritonitis): prefer <strong>ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h</strong>. Amox-clav is an acceptable oral step-down when she can take PO. <strong>FQ (cipro + metro) = allergy / fallback only</strong> — not first-line on this card.</li><li>Cuff abscess: image, add drainage (exam or IR). Cuff dehiscence / evisceration: warm saline wrap, OR now — do not push bowel back on the floor and hope.</li><li>Delayed ureter (flank pain, rising Cr, watery vaginal leak, no urge): CT urogram and urology now. A normal intra-op cystoscopy does not exclude a later thermal injury.</li><li>Watery leak that might be bladder: tampon test / creatinine on the fluid. Urology.</li><li>Ileus vs leak: persistent ileus + tachycardia + peritonitis is a leak until GS says it is not.</li></ul>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>OR tonight</td><td>Evisceration, unstable bleeding, or peritonitis.</td></tr><tr><td>Urology</td><td>Suspected ureter / bladder injury, anuria, or rising Cr with flank pain.</td></tr><tr><td>IR</td><td>Pelvic hematoma with blush, or drainable cuff / pelvic abscess.</td></tr><tr><td>General surgery</td><td>Suspected enterotomy / anastomotic leak / closed-loop.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine</td><td>PE workup, new AFib, uncontrolled diabetes stalling the pathway, or she cannot come off oxygen.</td></tr><tr><td>CM / home health</td><td>Foley going home, wound vac, no stairs help, or VTE teaching on enoxaparin.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Walk, feed, Foley out. Cuff cellulitis is treated with CTX + doxy + metro; cuff dehiscence goes back to the OR. Flank + watery leak is a ureter until proven otherwise.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Enhanced Recovery after Surgery (ERAS) night \n  Out of bed &gt;2 h the day of surgery. Diet and gum at 4 h if not nauseated. Foley out by 24 h unless a urogynecologic reason exists.  Scheduled APAP + NSAID. Opioid is the rescue, not the basal. Ondansetron / scopolamine per house ERAS. Incentive spirometry.  Prophylactic cefazolin was one pre-incision dose (2 g IV; 3 g IV if ≥120 kg). Add metronidazole 500 mg IV if the bowel was entered or it was a vaginal hysterectomy per PB 195. Do not continue cefazolin as ‘treatment’ because the temperature is 38.0 on POD 0.  SCDs on. Pharmacologic prophylaxis when hemostasis is acceptable and Caprini / house pathway says so.  \n Fever, cuff, ureter, bowel, VTE \n  POD 0–1 fever is usually atelectasis / cytokine. Examine the calf, lungs, cuff, and urine. Do not CT everyone at 18 hours.  Cuff cellulitis (POD 4–10, low-grade fever, cuff tender, no peritonitis): prefer  ceftriaxone 1 g IV q24h + doxycycline 100 mg IV/PO q12h + metronidazole 500 mg IV/PO q12h . Amox-clav is an acceptable oral step-down when she can take PO.  FQ (cipro + metro) = allergy / fallback only  — not first-line on this card.  Cuff abscess: image, add drainage (exam or IR). Cuff dehiscence / evisceration: warm saline wrap, OR now — do not push bowel back on the floor and hope.  Delayed ureter (flank pain, rising Cr, watery vaginal leak, no urge): CT urogram and urology now. A normal intra-op cystoscopy does not exclude a later thermal injury.  Watery leak that might be bladder: tampon test / creatinine on the fluid. Urology.  Ileus vs leak: persistent ileus + tachycardia + peritonitis is a leak until GS says it is not.  \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    OR tonight  Evisceration, unstable bleeding, or peritonitis.    Urology  Suspected ureter / bladder injury, anuria, or rising Cr with flank pain.    IR  Pelvic hematoma with blush, or drainable cuff / pelvic abscess.    General surgery  Suspected enterotomy / anastomotic leak / closed-loop.    Medicine  PE workup, new AFib, uncontrolled diabetes stalling the pathway, or she cannot come off oxygen.    CM / home health  Foley going home, wound vac, no stairs help, or VTE teaching on enoxaparin.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Walk, feed, Foley out. Cuff cellulitis is treated with CTX + doxy + metro; cuff dehiscence goes back to the OR. Flank + watery leak is a ureter until proven otherwise. ", "display_id": "GYN-09 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "GYNB-10", "kind": "card", "title": "Molar pregnancy / GTD (ACOG 2025 + CC 11)", "book": "GYN", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/gyn-book/OB_HOSPITALIST_GYNECOLOGY_Decision_Support_QR.md", "html": "<p>Recognize and evacuate in the hospital</p>\n<ul><li>Complete mole: snowstorm, no fetus, very high hCG, theca-lutein cysts. Partial mole: fetus + molar placenta, hCG often lower. Either can bleed or throw thyroid storm / PEC before 20 weeks.</li><li>Hospital suction (electric or manual vacuum). Do not manage like EPL. Do not use a misoprostol-only induction.</li><li>Misoprostol 200–400 mcg only to ripen a closed cervix 2–4 h prior — it is not the evacuation.</li><li>No oxytocin before the cannula is in. Pre-evac oxytocin is not used to thin the uterus. After the cavity is emptying you may use uterotonics for tone.</li><li>Type and screen, CBC, hCG (dilute if the number looks impossibly low at a huge uterus — hook effect [falsely low / false NEG] at ≥500,000), TSH if symptomatic, CXR if GTN is already suspected.</li><li>Anti-D: shared decision &lt;12 weeks; give ≥12 weeks if Rh-negative.</li></ul>\n<p>hCG follow-up and the GTN call</p>\n<ul><li>Weekly serum hCG on the same assay until three consecutive normals or ≤3 IU/L.</li><li>Partial mole: one extra hCG 1 month after the first normal, then stop. Complete mole: monthly × 3–6 months after normalization.</li><li>Contraception for ≥6 months after remission. Combined hormones are safe. IUD is a relative contraindication until remission (perforation + bleeding confusion).</li><li>GTN: plateau ≥10% × 4 values over 3 weeks, or rise &gt;10% × 3 values over ≥2 weeks, or histologic choriocarcinoma. Six-month persistence is no longer a criterion.</li><li>Low-risk GTN: Gyn Onc may offer single-agent chemo, second curettage, or hysterectomy.</li><li>Phantom hCG (CC 11): serum positive (often &gt;50) and urine negative. Heterophile. Do not give MTX. Repeat on a different assay / after dilution.</li><li>Recurrence after one mole 1–2%; after two moles 15–25%. Check hCG 6–8 weeks after every later pregnancy.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — suspected molar pregnancy (house protocol)</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Surgical evacuation only (suction D&amp;E / suction curettage); avoid medical evacuation.</li><li>Before suction: type &amp; cross 2 U, large-bore IV; have TXA 1 g IV, Foley 30–60 mL tamponade, methergine/Hemabate ready. Do <strong>not</strong> start oxytocin before the cannula is in. Misoprostol 1000 mcg PR = bleeding adjunct only; ripening dose is 200–400 mcg (separate indication).</li><li>After: weekly hCG until 3 consecutive normals; reliable hormonal contraception ≥6 months (IUD relatively contraindicated). Refer Gyn Onc if GTN criteria on this card are met — do not rewrite those criteria here.</li></ul></div>\n<p>Consult / case management</p>\n<table><tr><th>Call</th><th>When</th></tr><tr><td>Gyn Onc</td><td>GTN criteria met, histologic choriocarcinoma, lung mets, or you are considering chemo / second D&amp;C.</td></tr><tr><td>Medicine / ICU</td><td>Thyroid storm, respiratory failure from theca-lutein / trophoblastic emboli, or PEC physiology far too early.</td></tr><tr><td>IR / OR</td><td>Hemorrhage not controlled after suction and uterotonics.</td></tr><tr><td>CM / social / chaplain</td><td>This is a pregnancy loss with cancer-surveillance logistics. Reliable weekly labs, contraception that she will use, and a phone number that works are part of the treatment.</td></tr></table>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Hospital suction. Do not manage like EPL. No oxytocin before the cannula. Weekly hCG, same assay. Rising or plateaued curve → Gyn Onc. Urine-negative ‘hCG’ is phantom until proven otherwise.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Recognize and evacuate in the hospital \n  Complete mole: snowstorm, no fetus, very high hCG, theca-lutein cysts. Partial mole: fetus + molar placenta, hCG often lower. Either can bleed or throw thyroid storm / PEC before 20 weeks.  Hospital suction (electric or manual vacuum). Do not manage like EPL. Do not use a misoprostol-only induction.  Misoprostol 200–400 mcg only to ripen a closed cervix 2–4 h prior — it is not the evacuation.  No oxytocin before the cannula is in. Pre-evac oxytocin is not used to thin the uterus. After the cavity is emptying you may use uterotonics for tone.  Type and screen, CBC, hCG (dilute if the number looks impossibly low at a huge uterus — hook effect [falsely low / false NEG] at ≥500,000), TSH if symptomatic, CXR if GTN is already suspected.  Anti-D: shared decision &lt;12 weeks; give ≥12 weeks if Rh-negative.  \n hCG follow-up and the GTN call \n  Weekly serum hCG on the same assay until three consecutive normals or ≤3 IU/L.  Partial mole: one extra hCG 1 month after the first normal, then stop. Complete mole: monthly × 3–6 months after normalization.  Contraception for ≥6 months after remission. Combined hormones are safe. IUD is a relative contraindication until remission (perforation + bleeding confusion).  GTN: plateau ≥10% × 4 values over 3 weeks, or rise &gt;10% × 3 values over ≥2 weeks, or histologic choriocarcinoma. Six-month persistence is no longer a criterion.  Low-risk GTN: Gyn Onc may offer single-agent chemo, second curettage, or hysterectomy.  Phantom hCG (CC 11): serum positive (often &gt;50) and urine negative. Heterophile. Do not give MTX. Repeat on a different assay / after dilution.  Recurrence after one mole 1–2%; after two moles 15–25%. Check hCG 6–8 weeks after every later pregnancy.  \n   OBHG pearl — suspected molar pregnancy (house protocol)  \n  Surgical evacuation only (suction D&amp;E / suction curettage); avoid medical evacuation.  Before suction: type &amp; cross 2 U, large-bore IV; have TXA 1 g IV, Foley 30–60 mL tamponade, methergine/Hemabate ready. Do  not  start oxytocin before the cannula is in. Misoprostol 1000 mcg PR = bleeding adjunct only; ripening dose is 200–400 mcg (separate indication).  After: weekly hCG until 3 consecutive normals; reliable hormonal contraception ≥6 months (IUD relatively contraindicated). Refer Gyn Onc if GTN criteria on this card are met — do not rewrite those criteria here.   \n Consult / case management \n   Call  When    Gyn Onc  GTN criteria met, histologic choriocarcinoma, lung mets, or you are considering chemo / second D&amp;C.    Medicine / ICU  Thyroid storm, respiratory failure from theca-lutein / trophoblastic emboli, or PEC physiology far too early.    IR / OR  Hemorrhage not controlled after suction and uterotonics.    CM / social / chaplain  This is a pregnancy loss with cancer-surveillance logistics. Reliable weekly labs, contraception that she will use, and a phone number that works are part of the treatment.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Hospital suction. Do not manage like EPL. No oxytocin before the cannula. Weekly hCG, same assay. Rising or plateaued curve → Gyn Onc. Urine-negative ‘hCG’ is phantom until proven otherwise. ", "display_id": "GYN-10 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "SYS-02-EXP", "kind": "card", "title": "VTE · CMQCC expanded text", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/SYS-02-VTE-CMQCC-EXPANDED.md", "html": "<p>Diagnose — do not use D-dimer</p>\n<ul><li>Suspected DVT: compression US of the proximal veins first (Level A). Whole-leg swelling ± flank/buttock pain → iliac imaging or empiric heparin. Repeat US at 3 and 7 days if iliac clot is not suspected and the first study is negative.</li><li>Suspected PE: chest X-ray first. Normal film → V/Q. Abnormal film → CT angiography. Do not withhold imaging because she is pregnant.</li></ul>\n<p>Treat acute VTE</p>\n<ul><li>Adjusted-dose LMWH first-line. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Anti-Xa target 0.6–1.0 IU/mL 4 hours after a twice-daily dose.</li><li>No warfarin for most pregnant patients. No DOACs in pregnancy or lactation. Mechanical valve is the exception — multidisciplinary plan.</li><li>Acute VTE this pregnancy: therapeutic antepartum and at least 6 weeks postpartum.</li></ul>\n<p>Prophylaxis risk (CMQCC Low / Medium / High)</p>\n<p>Major factors (CS algorithm): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH requiring transfusion / further operation / IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72h current or within last month; chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome).</p>\n<p>Minor factors: multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion/further op/IR; family history VTE (1st-degree &lt;50); smoker; preeclampsia.</p>\n<p>Cesarean</p>\n<ul><li>LOW: not medium/high → mechanical only.</li><li>MEDIUM: 1 major OR ≥2 minor → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH postpartum until discharge. (CMQCC table text says “&gt;2 Minor”; house uses ≥2 per CMQCC prose / ACCP.)</li><li>HIGH: high-risk thrombophilia (incl APS) no prior VTE; prior provoked/idiopathic/estrogen VTE; low-risk thrombophilia + FH VTE OR single prior VTE; already on outpatient LMWH/UFH; multiple prior VTE; prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH to 6 weeks <strong>OR</strong> mechanical + therapeutic LMWH/UFH (PP dose ≥ antepartum) in hospital and to 6 weeks.</li></ul>\n<p>Vaginal</p>\n<ul><li>LOW (isolated delivery BMI &gt;40, no medium/high criteria): mechanical only. Other vaginal births without medium/high criteria: early ambulation.</li><li>MEDIUM: BMI &gt;40 AND (antepartum hosp ≥72h current/past month OR low-risk thrombophilia) → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH during PP hospitalization; consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia.</li><li>HIGH: same high-risk set as CS → mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks <strong>OR</strong> mechanical + therapeutic to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; prophylactic often enough for high-risk thrombophilia alone without prior VTE — bleeding risk and hematology judgment decide.</li></ul>\n<p>Definitions (compact): High-risk thrombophilia = ATIII deficiency, APS, FVL or PT gene homozygous/compound het. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, protein C or S deficiency. Family Hx = 1st-degree VTE before age 50.</p>\n<p>All CS: ambulate, avoid dehydration, mechanical prophylaxis before CS until ambulatory.</p>\n<p>Around birth</p>\n<ul><li>SCDs before every cesarean. Hold prophylactic LMWH ≥12 hours before scheduled induction or cesarean; hold therapeutic ≥24 hours.</li><li>Restart no sooner than 4–6 hours after vaginal birth or 6–12 hours after cesarean. After neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH ≥24 hours after the block and ≥4 hours after catheter removal.</li><li>Warfarin, LMWH, and UFH are compatible with breastfeeding.</li></ul>\n<p>Doses (compact)</p>\n<ul><li>Prophylactic enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily; Intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes / hematology — not a CMQCC L/M/H default); Therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (Anti-Xa 0.6–1.0). Hold prophylactic ≥12h / therapeutic ≥24h before scheduled birth.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. No D-dimer. Compression US for the leg; CXR then V/Q or CTA for the chest. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg q12h for acute clot. CMQCC risk band (L/M/H) drives postpartum mechanical ± pharmacologic duration. Hold prophylactic 12 hours / therapeutic 24 hours before scheduled birth; SCDs before every cesarean.</p>\n<p>Cite: CMQCC Maternal VTE Task Force; ACOG PB 196. Decision support only — not unit policy / not the record.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Diagnose — do not use D-dimer \n  Suspected DVT: compression US of the proximal veins first (Level A). Whole-leg swelling ± flank/buttock pain → iliac imaging or empiric heparin. Repeat US at 3 and 7 days if iliac clot is not suspected and the first study is negative.  Suspected PE: chest X-ray first. Normal film → V/Q. Abnormal film → CT angiography. Do not withhold imaging because she is pregnant.  \n Treat acute VTE \n  Adjusted-dose LMWH first-line. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg SC q12h. Anti-Xa target 0.6–1.0 IU/mL 4 hours after a twice-daily dose.  No warfarin for most pregnant patients. No DOACs in pregnancy or lactation. Mechanical valve is the exception — multidisciplinary plan.  Acute VTE this pregnancy: therapeutic antepartum and at least 6 weeks postpartum.  \n Prophylaxis risk (CMQCC Low / Medium / High) \n Major factors (CS algorithm): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH requiring transfusion / further operation / IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72h current or within last month; chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome). \n Minor factors: multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion/further op/IR; family history VTE (1st-degree &lt;50); smoker; preeclampsia. \n Cesarean \n  LOW: not medium/high → mechanical only.  MEDIUM: 1 major OR ≥2 minor → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH postpartum until discharge. (CMQCC table text says “&gt;2 Minor”; house uses ≥2 per CMQCC prose / ACCP.)  HIGH: high-risk thrombophilia (incl APS) no prior VTE; prior provoked/idiopathic/estrogen VTE; low-risk thrombophilia + FH VTE OR single prior VTE; already on outpatient LMWH/UFH; multiple prior VTE; prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH to 6 weeks  OR  mechanical + therapeutic LMWH/UFH (PP dose ≥ antepartum) in hospital and to 6 weeks.  \n Vaginal \n  LOW (isolated delivery BMI &gt;40, no medium/high criteria): mechanical only. Other vaginal births without medium/high criteria: early ambulation.  MEDIUM: BMI &gt;40 AND (antepartum hosp ≥72h current/past month OR low-risk thrombophilia) → mechanical + prophylactic LMWH/UFH during PP hospitalization; consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia.  HIGH: same high-risk set as CS → mechanical + prophylactic to 6 weeks  OR  mechanical + therapeutic to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if already adjusted-dose antepartum or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS; prophylactic often enough for high-risk thrombophilia alone without prior VTE — bleeding risk and hematology judgment decide.  \n Definitions (compact): High-risk thrombophilia = ATIII deficiency, APS, FVL or PT gene homozygous/compound het. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, protein C or S deficiency. Family Hx = 1st-degree VTE before age 50. \n All CS: ambulate, avoid dehydration, mechanical prophylaxis before CS until ambulatory. \n Around birth \n  SCDs before every cesarean. Hold prophylactic LMWH ≥12 hours before scheduled induction or cesarean; hold therapeutic ≥24 hours.  Restart no sooner than 4–6 hours after vaginal birth or 6–12 hours after cesarean. After neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH ≥24 hours after the block and ≥4 hours after catheter removal.  Warfarin, LMWH, and UFH are compatible with breastfeeding.  \n Doses (compact) \n  Prophylactic enoxaparin 40 mg SC daily; Intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes / hematology — not a CMQCC L/M/H default); Therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (Anti-Xa 0.6–1.0). Hold prophylactic ≥12h / therapeutic ≥24h before scheduled birth.  \n BOTTOM LINE. No D-dimer. Compression US for the leg; CXR then V/Q or CTA for the chest. Enoxaparin 1 mg/kg q12h for acute clot. CMQCC risk band (L/M/H) drives postpartum mechanical ± pharmacologic duration. Hold prophylactic 12 hours / therapeutic 24 hours before scheduled birth; SCDs before every cesarean. \n Cite: CMQCC Maternal VTE Task Force; ACOG PB 196. Decision support only — not unit policy / not the record. ", "display_id": "SYS-02 appx"}, {"id": "SYS-04", "kind": "card", "title": "Maternal cardiac arrest / resuscitative hysterotomy (AHA 2025 + PB 211)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md", "html": "<p>Call it and start the clock</p>\n<ul><li>Unresponsive, no normal breathing, no pulse: start high-quality CPR now. Call the code and the OB, anesthesia, and neonatal teams. Say the time aloud; a recorder calls out every minute (SMFM AFE checklist 2026).</li><li>Same ACLS ratios, drugs, and defibrillation as any adult. Manual left uterine displacement — do not tilt the board if that weakens compressions.</li><li>Fundus at or above the umbilicus: continuous manual left uterine displacement from the first compression (AHA 2025). Push hard and fast (100–120/min, at least 5 cm), full recoil, minimal pauses, backboard.</li><li>Detach fetal monitors once CPR starts. Fetal status does not steer this resuscitation (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).</li></ul>\n<p>Airway, access, drugs</p>\n<ul><li>Airway early, by the most experienced person. Expect a difficult airway and aspiration (AHA 2025). 100% oxygen.</li><li>IV or IO above the diaphragm (AHA 2025).</li><li>Standard ACLS drugs and doses, no change for pregnancy: epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO q3–5 min; defibrillate VF / pulseless VT at standard energy (AHA 2025).</li><li>On magnesium: <strong>stop the infusion</strong> (AHA 2025).</li><li>Calcium: give as soon as a reversible cause such as hypermagnesemia is suspected. Dose: 30 mL of 10% calcium gluconate (about 3 g; about 14 mEq), 3x the usual 10 mL dose (about 4.6 mEq).</li><li>Suspected LAST (neuraxial or local anesthetic): stop the local anesthetic. 20% lipid emulsion: &gt;70 kg, 100 mL over 2–3 min, then 200–250 mL over 15–20 min; &lt;70 kg, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 min, then 0.25 mL/kg/min. Still unstable: repeat the bolus and double the infusion; max about 12 mL/kg (ASRA 2020).</li><li>Suspected AFE: activate MTP now (AHA 2025) and open SYS-05.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean)</p>\n<ul><li>Resuscitative hysterotomy / perimortem cesarean at ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) for maternal resuscitation.</li><li>Neonatal viability (~23 weeks, per neonatology) guides neonatal team preparation, not the decision to deliver.</li><li>Prepare at minute 0: OB surgeon, scalpel, neonatal team, warmer.</li><li><strong>No ROSC at 4 minutes: incise. Goal is delivery by 5 minutes</strong> (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).</li><li>Perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest, fundus ≥20 weeks; scalpel is the instrument. Do not wait for an OR.</li><li>Antiseptic splash if available. Do not wait for antibiotics. Move to the OR only if that takes less than 2 minutes (SMFM 2026).</li><li>Compressions and uterine displacement continue until the uterus is empty.</li><li>The 4–5 minute teaching point is not a death clock. Faster is better; benefit is still described well beyond 5 minutes. Still proceed if you are late.</li><li>Uterus below the umbilicus (under 20 weeks): no hysterotomy; standard adult ACLS.</li></ul>\n<p>Find the cause (AHA 2025 A–H; older mnemonic BEAU-CHOPS)</p>\n<ul><li><strong>A</strong>nesthetic (high block, LAST, failed airway) · <strong>B</strong>leeding (PPH, abruption, rupture: PP-01, LND-10) · <strong>C</strong>ardiovascular (MI, cardiomyopathy, dissection, arrhythmia) · <strong>D</strong>rugs (magnesium, opioids, anaphylaxis) · <strong>E</strong>mbolic (AFE: SYS-05; PE: SYS-02) · <strong>F</strong>ever (sepsis: SYS-03 flow) · <strong>G</strong>eneral H's and T's · <strong>H</strong>ypertension (preeclampsia, eclampsia, stroke: ANT-04, ANT-11).</li></ul>\n<p>Code team roles (AHA 2025 team list)</p>\n<ul><li>Team leader who is not the surgeon. Two or more compressors, swap every 2 min. Uterine displacement holder. Airway (anesthesia). Defibrillator and rhythm. IV/IO and drugs (RN with pharmacist). Recorder and timekeeper. OB surgeon plus assistant. Neonatal team. Runner for blood bank and equipment.</li></ul>\n<p>After ROSC or delivery</p>\n<ul><li>Expect atony and DIC: uterotonics, TXA, and MTP per PP-01. Close or pack when stable; finish in the OR. ICU (SYS-01). Refractory arrest: per Critical Care guidelines.</li><li>Document times: arrest, CPR start, each shock and drug, incision, delivery, ROSC. Debrief the team. Support the family and staff.</li></ul>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. CPR, uterine displacement, early airway, IV above the diaphragm. Stop magnesium; calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL. ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) and no ROSC: incise by 4 minutes, deliver by 5, at the bedside with a scalpel. Late is still worth doing.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Call it and start the clock \n   Unresponsive, no normal breathing, no pulse: start high-quality CPR now. Call the code and the OB, anesthesia, and neonatal teams. Say the time aloud; a recorder calls out every minute (SMFM AFE checklist 2026).   Same ACLS ratios, drugs, and defibrillation as any adult. Manual left uterine displacement — do not tilt the board if that weakens compressions.   Fundus at or above the umbilicus: continuous manual left uterine displacement from the first compression (AHA 2025). Push hard and fast (100–120/min, at least 5 cm), full recoil, minimal pauses, backboard.   Detach fetal monitors once CPR starts. Fetal status does not steer this resuscitation (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).  \n Airway, access, drugs \n   Airway early, by the most experienced person. Expect a difficult airway and aspiration (AHA 2025). 100% oxygen.   IV or IO above the diaphragm (AHA 2025).   Standard ACLS drugs and doses, no change for pregnancy: epinephrine 1 mg IV/IO q3–5 min; defibrillate VF / pulseless VT at standard energy (AHA 2025).   On magnesium:  stop the infusion  (AHA 2025).   Calcium: give as soon as a reversible cause such as hypermagnesemia is suspected. Dose: 30 mL of 10% calcium gluconate (about 3 g; about 14 mEq), 3x the usual 10 mL dose (about 4.6 mEq).   Suspected LAST (neuraxial or local anesthetic): stop the local anesthetic. 20% lipid emulsion: >70 kg, 100 mL over 2–3 min, then 200–250 mL over 15–20 min; <70 kg, 1.5 mL/kg over 2–3 min, then 0.25 mL/kg/min. Still unstable: repeat the bolus and double the infusion; max about 12 mL/kg (ASRA 2020).   Suspected AFE: activate MTP now (AHA 2025) and open SYS-05.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem cesarean) \n   Resuscitative hysterotomy / perimortem cesarean at ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) for maternal resuscitation.   Neonatal viability (~23 weeks, per neonatology) guides neonatal team preparation, not the decision to deliver.   Prepare at minute 0: OB surgeon, scalpel, neonatal team, warmer.    No ROSC at 4 minutes: incise. Goal is delivery by 5 minutes  (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).   Perimortem cesarean at the bedside while she is still in arrest, fundus ≥20 weeks; scalpel is the instrument. Do not wait for an OR.   Antiseptic splash if available. Do not wait for antibiotics. Move to the OR only if that takes less than 2 minutes (SMFM 2026).   Compressions and uterine displacement continue until the uterus is empty.   The 4–5 minute teaching point is not a death clock. Faster is better; benefit is still described well beyond 5 minutes. Still proceed if you are late.   Uterus below the umbilicus (under 20 weeks): no hysterotomy; standard adult ACLS.  \n Find the cause (AHA 2025 A–H; older mnemonic BEAU-CHOPS) \n    A nesthetic (high block, LAST, failed airway) ·  B leeding (PPH, abruption, rupture: PP-01, LND-10) ·  C ardiovascular (MI, cardiomyopathy, dissection, arrhythmia) ·  D rugs (magnesium, opioids, anaphylaxis) ·  E mbolic (AFE: SYS-05; PE: SYS-02) ·  F ever (sepsis: SYS-03 flow) ·  G eneral H's and T's ·  H ypertension (preeclampsia, eclampsia, stroke: ANT-04, ANT-11).  \n Code team roles (AHA 2025 team list) \n   Team leader who is not the surgeon. Two or more compressors, swap every 2 min. Uterine displacement holder. Airway (anesthesia). Defibrillator and rhythm. IV/IO and drugs (RN with pharmacist). Recorder and timekeeper. OB surgeon plus assistant. Neonatal team. Runner for blood bank and equipment.  \n After ROSC or delivery \n   Expect atony and DIC: uterotonics, TXA, and MTP per PP-01. Close or pack when stable; finish in the OR. ICU (SYS-01). Refractory arrest: per Critical Care guidelines.   Document times: arrest, CPR start, each shock and drug, incision, delivery, ROSC. Debrief the team. Support the family and staff.  \n BOTTOM LINE. CPR, uterine displacement, early airway, IV above the diaphragm. Stop magnesium; calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL. ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) and no ROSC: incise by 4 minutes, deliver by 5, at the bedside with a scalpel. Late is still worth doing. "}, {"id": "SYS-05", "kind": "card", "title": "Amniotic fluid embolism (SMFM CG 9 + 2026 checklist)", "book": "OB", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/OB_HOSPITALIST_OBSTETRICS_Decision_Support_QR_2026-10-31.md", "html": "<p>Suspect it</p>\n<ul><li>Sudden cardiorespiratory collapse, or hypotension with hypoxia, in labor, at cesarean, or within 30 min of placental delivery; coagulopathy often follows (SMFM CG 9).</li><li><strong>AFE is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion.</strong> No laboratory test confirms or excludes it (SMFM CG 9). Treat on suspicion; do not wait for a test.</li><li>Prodrome: agitation, sense of doom, altered mental status (SMFM CG 9).</li></ul>\n<p>Diagnostic criteria (SMFM / Clark research criteria, 2016): all four</p>\n<ul><li>(1) Sudden cardiorespiratory arrest, or hypotension (SBP &lt;90) plus respiratory compromise (dyspnea, cyanosis, or SpO2 &lt;90%).</li><li>(2) Overt DIC by the ISTH-modified obstetric score, after those first signs and before blood loss alone could explain it.</li><li class=\"sub\">Score: platelets &lt;100,000 = 1, &lt;50,000 = 2; PT/INR rise 25–50% = 1, &gt;50% = 2; fibrinogen &lt;200 mg/dL = 1. Score ≥3 = overt DIC (Clark 2016).</li><li>(3) Onset during labor or within 30 min of delivery of the placenta (Clark 2016).</li><li>(4) No fever ≥38.0 °C in labor.</li><li>These are research criteria. Do not withhold treatment because one is missing.</li></ul>\n<p>Clinical phases</p>\n<ul><li>Initial pulmonary vasoconstriction, then acute RV failure, hypoxia and hypotension; then LV failure; then coagulopathy/DIC, with or after the collapse. Rarely, coagulopathy is the only finding (SMFM CG 9).</li></ul>\n<p>Differential</p>\n<ul><li>PE, anaphylaxis, sepsis, high spinal/LAST, eclampsia, hemorrhage/abruption, uterine rupture, MI/peripartum cardiomyopathy, aortic dissection, air embolism, transfusion reaction.</li></ul>\n<p>Immediate resuscitation</p>\n<ul><li>Call the code: OB, anesthesia, critical care, neonatal teams. Timekeeper calls each minute. Detach fetal monitors once CPR starts (SMFM 2026).</li><li>No pulse: high-quality CPR, left uterine displacement, airway and oxygenation (SYS-04). <strong>Avoid hypoxia</strong>; hypoxia, acidosis, and hypercapnia raise pulmonary vascular resistance (SMFM CG 9).</li><li>Perimortem delivery per SYS-04. Resuscitative hysterotomy / perimortem cesarean at ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) for maternal resuscitation. Neonatal viability (~23 weeks, per neonatology) guides neonatal team preparation, not the decision to deliver. No ROSC at 4 min: incise; deliver by 5 min (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).</li><li>Calcium: give as soon as a reversible cause such as hypermagnesemia is suspected. Dose: 30 mL of 10% calcium gluconate (about 3 g; about 14 mEq), 3x the usual 10 mL dose (about 4.6 mEq).</li><li><strong>Avoid fluid overload</strong>: 500 mL boluses and reassess. Volume as blood products, not crystalloid (SMFM 2026).</li><li>A-OK*: atropine 1 mg IV, ondansetron 8 mg IV, ketorolac 30 mg IV.</li><li>Ketorolac: avoid or use caution with coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, active hemorrhage or renal impairment. Atropine: only with bradycardia.</li><li>* Not considered routine by SMFM.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Right ventricular support (anesthesia, critical care, cardiology)</p>\n<ul><li>Bedside echo / TTE (or TEE) for RV assessment: dilated, hypokinetic RV with septal shift toward the LV (SMFM CG 9).</li><li>Vasopressor: norepinephrine 0.05–3.3 mcg/kg/min (SMFM 2026).</li><li>Inotropes: dobutamine 2.5–5.0 mcg/kg/min or milrinone 0.25–0.75 mcg/kg/min (SMFM 2026).</li><li>Pulmonary vasodilators to unload the RV: inhaled nitric oxide 5–40 ppm, inhaled epoprostenol 10–50 ng/kg/min, or sildenafil 20 mg PO if awake and alert (SMFM 2026).</li><li>Wean FiO2 to SpO2 94–98% (SMFM 2026).</li><li>Prolonged CPR or refractory right heart failure: per Critical Care guidelines.</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Hemorrhage and coagulopathy (house PP-01)</p>\n<ul><li>Activate MTP early; favor cryo/fibrinogen over FFP to limit volume (SMFM 2026; ACOG PB 183 uses 1:1:1). Do not drown in crystalloid.</li><li>Early cryo / fibrinogen. Fibrinogen target &gt;150–200 mg/dL.</li><li>TXA: give early, as soon as coagulopathy is noted or when blood products are started. 1 g IV over 10 min (1 g in 100 mL NS); repeat 1 g once if bleeding continues after 30 min (max 2 g in 24 h).</li><li>Uterotonics: oxytocin plus other uterotonics per PP-01 (SMFM 2026). Calcium with transfusion. Large-bore IV; intraosseous if needed.</li><li>Stage 4 = cardiovascular collapse / AFE → ACLS + definitive surgery + aggressive MTP (PP-01). Refractory bleeding: PP-01 steps; possible hysterectomy. Diffuse bleeding at cesarean not surgically controllable: consider packing and ICU with delayed closure (SMFM CG 9).</li><li>Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.</li></ul>\n<p>Labs</p>\n<ul><li>CBC, PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen, ABG, lactate, type and crossmatch, CMP, troponin; TEG/ROTEM if available. Repeat with each round of products.</li></ul>\n<p>After the event</p>\n<ul><li>ICU. Anesthesia, critical care, MFM, respiratory therapy, blood bank, cardiology (SMFM CG 9).</li><li>Debrief the team; support the family and staff. Report to the AFE Foundation registry as appropriate (SMFM 2026). VTE prophylaxis per the SYS-02 tree once bleeding is controlled.</li></ul>\n<div class=\"pearl\"><p><strong>OBHG pearl — AFE</strong></p>\n<ul><li>Collapse plus coagulopathy out of proportion to blood loss is the signature; DIC is present in up to 83% (SMFM CG 9).</li><li>Atony with secondary coagulopathy is not AFE; SMFM calls it a common diagnostic error.</li><li>The right ventricle is the target: no fluid floods; pressor, inotrope, and pulmonary vasodilator.</li><li>MTP early; TXA as soon as coagulopathy is noted or products start.</li></ul></div>\n<p class=\"bottomline\">BOTTOM LINE. Sudden collapse, or hypoxia with hypotension, around birth: treat as AFE; it is a diagnosis of exclusion. No pulse: SYS-04, hysterotomy by 4 to 5 minutes at ≥20 weeks. Support the right heart; do not flood her. MTP early, cryo and fibrinogen, early TXA, uterotonics.</p>", "flags": [], "text": " Suspect it \n   Sudden cardiorespiratory collapse, or hypotension with hypoxia, in labor, at cesarean, or within 30 min of placental delivery; coagulopathy often follows (SMFM CG 9).    AFE is a clinical diagnosis of exclusion.  No laboratory test confirms or excludes it (SMFM CG 9). Treat on suspicion; do not wait for a test.   Prodrome: agitation, sense of doom, altered mental status (SMFM CG 9).  \n Diagnostic criteria (SMFM / Clark research criteria, 2016): all four \n   (1) Sudden cardiorespiratory arrest, or hypotension (SBP <90) plus respiratory compromise (dyspnea, cyanosis, or SpO2 <90%).   (2) Overt DIC by the ISTH-modified obstetric score, after those first signs and before blood loss alone could explain it.   Score: platelets <100,000 = 1, <50,000 = 2; PT/INR rise 25–50% = 1, >50% = 2; fibrinogen <200 mg/dL = 1. Score ≥3 = overt DIC (Clark 2016).   (3) Onset during labor or within 30 min of delivery of the placenta (Clark 2016).   (4) No fever ≥38.0 °C in labor.   These are research criteria. Do not withhold treatment because one is missing.  \n Clinical phases \n   Initial pulmonary vasoconstriction, then acute RV failure, hypoxia and hypotension; then LV failure; then coagulopathy/DIC, with or after the collapse. Rarely, coagulopathy is the only finding (SMFM CG 9).  \n Differential \n   PE, anaphylaxis, sepsis, high spinal/LAST, eclampsia, hemorrhage/abruption, uterine rupture, MI/peripartum cardiomyopathy, aortic dissection, air embolism, transfusion reaction.  \n Immediate resuscitation \n   Call the code: OB, anesthesia, critical care, neonatal teams. Timekeeper calls each minute. Detach fetal monitors once CPR starts (SMFM 2026).   No pulse: high-quality CPR, left uterine displacement, airway and oxygenation (SYS-04).  Avoid hypoxia ; hypoxia, acidosis, and hypercapnia raise pulmonary vascular resistance (SMFM CG 9).   Perimortem delivery per SYS-04. Resuscitative hysterotomy / perimortem cesarean at ≥20 weeks (uterus at or above the umbilicus) for maternal resuscitation. Neonatal viability (~23 weeks, per neonatology) guides neonatal team preparation, not the decision to deliver. No ROSC at 4 min: incise; deliver by 5 min (AHA 2025; SMFM 2026).   Calcium: give as soon as a reversible cause such as hypermagnesemia is suspected. Dose: 30 mL of 10% calcium gluconate (about 3 g; about 14 mEq), 3x the usual 10 mL dose (about 4.6 mEq).    Avoid fluid overload : 500 mL boluses and reassess. Volume as blood products, not crystalloid (SMFM 2026).   A-OK*: atropine 1 mg IV, ondansetron 8 mg IV, ketorolac 30 mg IV.   Ketorolac: avoid or use caution with coagulopathy, thrombocytopenia, active hemorrhage or renal impairment. Atropine: only with bradycardia.   * Not considered routine by SMFM.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Right ventricular support (anesthesia, critical care, cardiology) \n   Bedside echo / TTE (or TEE) for RV assessment: dilated, hypokinetic RV with septal shift toward the LV (SMFM CG 9).   Vasopressor: norepinephrine 0.05–3.3 mcg/kg/min (SMFM 2026).   Inotropes: dobutamine 2.5–5.0 mcg/kg/min or milrinone 0.25–0.75 mcg/kg/min (SMFM 2026).   Pulmonary vasodilators to unload the RV: inhaled nitric oxide 5–40 ppm, inhaled epoprostenol 10–50 ng/kg/min, or sildenafil 20 mg PO if awake and alert (SMFM 2026).   Wean FiO2 to SpO2 94–98% (SMFM 2026).   Prolonged CPR or refractory right heart failure: per Critical Care guidelines.   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Hemorrhage and coagulopathy (house PP-01) \n   Activate MTP early; favor cryo/fibrinogen over FFP to limit volume (SMFM 2026; ACOG PB 183 uses 1:1:1). Do not drown in crystalloid.   Early cryo / fibrinogen. Fibrinogen target >150–200 mg/dL.   TXA: give early, as soon as coagulopathy is noted or when blood products are started. 1 g IV over 10 min (1 g in 100 mL NS); repeat 1 g once if bleeding continues after 30 min (max 2 g in 24 h).   Uterotonics: oxytocin plus other uterotonics per PP-01 (SMFM 2026). Calcium with transfusion. Large-bore IV; intraosseous if needed.   Stage 4 = cardiovascular collapse / AFE → ACLS + definitive surgery + aggressive MTP (PP-01). Refractory bleeding: PP-01 steps; possible hysterectomy. Diffuse bleeding at cesarean not surgically controllable: consider packing and ICU with delayed closure (SMFM CG 9).   Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build.  \n Labs \n   CBC, PT/INR, aPTT, fibrinogen, ABG, lactate, type and crossmatch, CMP, troponin; TEG/ROTEM if available. Repeat with each round of products.  \n After the event \n   ICU. Anesthesia, critical care, MFM, respiratory therapy, blood bank, cardiology (SMFM CG 9).   Debrief the team; support the family and staff. Report to the AFE Foundation registry as appropriate (SMFM 2026). VTE prophylaxis per the SYS-02 tree once bleeding is controlled.  \n   OBHG pearl — AFE  \n   Collapse plus coagulopathy out of proportion to blood loss is the signature; DIC is present in up to 83% (SMFM CG 9).   Atony with secondary coagulopathy is not AFE; SMFM calls it a common diagnostic error.   The right ventricle is the target: no fluid floods; pressor, inotrope, and pulmonary vasodilator.   MTP early; TXA as soon as coagulopathy is noted or products start.   \n BOTTOM LINE. Sudden collapse, or hypoxia with hypotension, around birth: treat as AFE; it is a diagnosis of exclusion. No pulse: SYS-04, hysterotomy by 4 to 5 minutes at ≥20 weeks. Support the right heart; do not flood her. MTP early, cryo and fibrinogen, early TXA, uterotonics. "}, {"id": "FD-ANT-04", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-04", "title": "Severe-range BP: the 15-minute clock", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-04_SEVERE_BP_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-04_SEVERE_BP_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "17e5500fcc33", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Severe-range BP: the 15-minute clock "}, {"id": "FD-PP-01", "kind": "diagram", "card": "PP-01", "title": "Postpartum hemorrhage flow", "img": "assets/diagrams/PP-01_PPH_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/PP-01_PPH_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "b545063f188d", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Postpartum hemorrhage flow "}, {"id": "FD-LND-01", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-01", "title": "Category II FHR flow", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-01_CAT_II_FHR_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-01_CAT_II_FHR_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "6ba77cd9674b", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Category II FHR flow "}, {"id": "FD-SYS-03", "kind": "diagram", "card": "SYS-03", "title": "Maternal sepsis flow", "img": "assets/diagrams/SYS-03_MATERNAL_SEPSIS_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/SYS-03_MATERNAL_SEPSIS_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "4b875966b2a9", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Maternal sepsis flow "}, {"id": "FD-SYS-02", "kind": "diagram", "card": "SYS-02", "title": "Postpartum VTE prophylaxis decision tree (CMQCC)", "img": "assets/diagrams/SYS-02_VTE_POSTPARTUM_DECISION_TREE_FINAL_2026-10-01.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/card-pulls/SYS-02_VTE_POSTPARTUM_DECISION_TREE_FINAL_2026-10-01.pdf", "sha": "0afab0c92d1a", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "html": "<p><strong>STEP 0 START.</strong> Postpartum patient: assess VTE risk at delivery (and re-assess if clinical change).</p>\n<p><strong>STEP 1: ANY HIGH-RISK TRIGGER? (either mode of delivery).</strong> Any ONE trigger = YES.</p>\n<ul><li>High-risk thrombophilia (including APS), no prior VTE</li><li>Prior provoked, idiopathic, or estrogen-related VTE</li><li>Low-risk thrombophilia + family history of VTE, OR single prior VTE</li><li>Already on outpatient LMWH/UFH</li><li>Multiple prior VTE</li><li>Prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia or APS</li></ul>\n<p>YES → <strong>HIGH RISK · DARK GRAY: SCDs + LMWH/UFH to 6 weeks.</strong> EITHER prophylactic dose to 6 weeks OR therapeutic dose (postpartum dose at least antepartum dose) in hospital and to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if on adjusted-dose antepartum, or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS. Hematology and bleeding-risk judgment decide.</p>\n<p>NO → Step 2.</p>\n<p><strong>STEP 2: MODE OF DELIVERY?</strong></p>\n<h5>Cesarean branch</h5>\n<ul><li>MAJOR (count each): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH needing transfusion, further operation, or IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72 h (current or within last month); chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac disease, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome).</li><li>MINOR (count each): multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion, further operation, or IR; family history VTE (first-degree, before age 50); smoker; preeclampsia.</li><li><strong>STEP 3A: 1 or more MAJOR, OR 2 or more MINOR?</strong></li><li class=\"sub\">YES → <strong>MEDIUM · LIGHT GRAY:</strong> SCDs + prophylactic LMWH/UFH, postpartum until discharge.</li><li class=\"sub\">NO → <strong>LOW · WHITE:</strong> SCDs only (mechanical). Early ambulation, avoid dehydration.</li></ul>\n<h5>Vaginal branch</h5>\n<ul><li><strong>STEP 3B: BMI &gt;40 AND (antepartum hospitalization ≥72 h, current or within last month, OR low-risk thrombophilia)?</strong></li><li class=\"sub\">YES → <strong>MEDIUM · LIGHT GRAY:</strong> SCDs + prophylactic LMWH/UFH during the postpartum hospitalization. Consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia.</li><li class=\"sub\">NO → Step 4.</li><li><strong>STEP 4: ISOLATED DELIVERY BMI &gt;40?</strong></li><li class=\"sub\">YES → <strong>LOW · WHITE:</strong> SCDs only (mechanical only).</li><li class=\"sub\">NO → <strong>LOW · WHITE:</strong> early ambulation. No pharmacologic prophylaxis.</li></ul>\n<h4>Reference strips on the chart</h4>\n<ul><li><strong>Definitions:</strong> High-risk thrombophilia = antithrombin deficiency, APS, or FVL or PT gene homozygous or compound heterozygous. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, or protein C or S deficiency. Family history = first-degree relative with VTE before age 50.</li><li><strong>Dosing (enoxaparin):</strong> prophylactic 40 mg SC daily; intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes or hematology direction only; not a CMQCC default); therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (anti-Xa 0.6 to 1.0 IU/mL, 4 h after a dose).</li><li><strong>Timing and safety:</strong> restart no sooner than 4 to 6 h after vaginal birth or 6 to 12 h after cesarean. Neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH at least 24 h after the block and at least 4 h after catheter removal. Before scheduled birth: hold prophylactic LMWH at least 12 h, therapeutic at least 24 h. SCDs before every cesarean until ambulatory. LMWH, UFH, and warfarin are compatible with breastfeeding.</li></ul>", "text": "Postpartum VTE prophylaxis decision tree (CMQCC)   STEP 0 START.  Postpartum patient: assess VTE risk at delivery (and re-assess if clinical change). \n  STEP 1: ANY HIGH-RISK TRIGGER? (either mode of delivery).  Any ONE trigger = YES. \n  High-risk thrombophilia (including APS), no prior VTE  Prior provoked, idiopathic, or estrogen-related VTE  Low-risk thrombophilia + family history of VTE, OR single prior VTE  Already on outpatient LMWH/UFH  Multiple prior VTE  Prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia or APS  \n YES →  HIGH RISK · DARK GRAY: SCDs + LMWH/UFH to 6 weeks.  EITHER prophylactic dose to 6 weeks OR therapeutic dose (postpartum dose at least antepartum dose) in hospital and to 6 weeks. Favor therapeutic if on adjusted-dose antepartum, or prior VTE + high-risk thrombophilia/APS. Hematology and bleeding-risk judgment decide. \n NO → Step 2. \n  STEP 2: MODE OF DELIVERY?  \n Cesarean branch \n  MAJOR (count each): BMI &gt;35 at delivery; low-risk thrombophilia; PPH needing transfusion, further operation, or IR; infection needing antibiotics; antepartum hospitalization ≥72 h (current or within last month); chronic medical (sickle cell, SLE, significant cardiac disease, active IBD, active cancer, nephrotic syndrome).  MINOR (count each): multiple gestation; age &gt;40; PPH ≥1000 mL without transfusion, further operation, or IR; family history VTE (first-degree, before age 50); smoker; preeclampsia.   STEP 3A: 1 or more MAJOR, OR 2 or more MINOR?   YES →  MEDIUM · LIGHT GRAY:  SCDs + prophylactic LMWH/UFH, postpartum until discharge.  NO →  LOW · WHITE:  SCDs only (mechanical). Early ambulation, avoid dehydration.  \n Vaginal branch \n   STEP 3B: BMI &gt;40 AND (antepartum hospitalization ≥72 h, current or within last month, OR low-risk thrombophilia)?   YES →  MEDIUM · LIGHT GRAY:  SCDs + prophylactic LMWH/UFH during the postpartum hospitalization. Consider 6 weeks if BMI ≥40 + thrombophilia.  NO → Step 4.   STEP 4: ISOLATED DELIVERY BMI &gt;40?   YES →  LOW · WHITE:  SCDs only (mechanical only).  NO →  LOW · WHITE:  early ambulation. No pharmacologic prophylaxis.  \n Reference strips on the chart \n   Definitions:  High-risk thrombophilia = antithrombin deficiency, APS, or FVL or PT gene homozygous or compound heterozygous. Low-risk = FVL or PT heterozygous, or protein C or S deficiency. Family history = first-degree relative with VTE before age 50.   Dosing (enoxaparin):  prophylactic 40 mg SC daily; intermediate 40 mg SC q12h (weight extremes or hematology direction only; not a CMQCC default); therapeutic 1 mg/kg SC q12h (anti-Xa 0.6 to 1.0 IU/mL, 4 h after a dose).   Timing and safety:  restart no sooner than 4 to 6 h after vaginal birth or 6 to 12 h after cesarean. Neuraxial: delay therapeutic LMWH at least 24 h after the block and at least 4 h after catheter removal. Before scheduled birth: hold prophylactic LMWH at least 12 h, therapeutic at least 24 h. SCDs before every cesarean until ambulatory. LMWH, UFH, and warfarin are compatible with breastfeeding.  "}, {"id": "FD-LND-04", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-04", "title": "Intraamniotic infection: diagnose, treat, deliver", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-04_IAI_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-04_IAI_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "61537f364426", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Intraamniotic infection: diagnose, treat, deliver ", "topics": ["LND-04"]}, {"id": "FD-GYN-01", "kind": "diagram", "card": "GYNB-01", "title": "Tubal ectopic and PUL: stable, scan, trend, treat", "img": "assets/diagrams/GYN-01_ECTOPIC_PUL_FLOW_2026-10-26.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/GYN-01_ECTOPIC_PUL_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "990ed1a2a3d8", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Tubal ectopic and PUL: stable, scan, trend, treat ", "topics": ["GYN-01", "GYNB-01"], "display_id": "GYN-01 (GYN + OB book)"}, {"id": "FD-GYNB-02", "kind": "diagram", "card": "GYNB-02", "title": "Acute abnormal uterine bleeding: stabilize, test, stop it", "img": "assets/diagrams/GYN-02_ACUTE_AUB_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/GYN-02_ACUTE_AUB_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "4b0b47ee5a2b", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Acute abnormal uterine bleeding: stabilize, test, stop it ", "topics": ["GYNB-02"], "display_id": "GYN-02 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "FD-GYNB-04", "kind": "diagram", "card": "GYNB-04", "title": "Adnexal torsion and mass: call it, untwist, keep the ovary", "img": "assets/diagrams/GYN-04_ADNEXAL_TORSION_MASS_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/GYN-04_ADNEXAL_TORSION_MASS_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "63a832af56da", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Adnexal torsion and mass: call it, untwist, keep the ovary ", "topics": ["GYNB-04", "GYNB-05"], "display_id": "GYN-04/05 (GYN book)"}, {"id": "FD-LND-06", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-06", "title": "Shoulder dystocia: call it, time it, work the maneuvers", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-06_SHOULDER_DYSTOCIA_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-06_SHOULDER_DYSTOCIA_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "2bb39e977353", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Shoulder dystocia: call it, time it, work the maneuvers ", "topics": ["LND-06"]}, {"id": "FD-ANT-01", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-01", "title": "PROM and PPROM: confirm, date, manage by gestational age", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-01_PROM_PPROM_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-01_PROM_PPROM_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "12441556d8f0", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "PROM and PPROM: confirm, date, manage by gestational age ", "topics": ["ANT-01"]}, {"id": "FD-ANT-07", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-07", "title": "Placenta accreta spectrum: suspect, transfer, leave the placenta", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-07_ACCRETA_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-07_ACCRETA_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "9afa51d5e125", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Placenta accreta spectrum: suspect, transfer, leave the placenta ", "topics": ["ANT-07"]}, {"id": "FD-ED-OB", "kind": "diagram", "card": null, "title": "ED-to-OB pathway decision tree", "img": "assets/diagrams/ED_TO_OB_PATHWAY_DECISION_TREE_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ED_TO_OB_PATHWAY_DECISION_TREE_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "1cd746ab1197", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "ED-to-OB pathway decision tree ", "display_id": "ED→OB"}, {"id": "FD-ANT-02", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-02", "title": "Preterm labor: confirm change, steroids, 48 hours, stop (flow)", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-02_PRETERM_LABOR_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-02_PRETERM_LABOR_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "0501ac28a90d", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Preterm labor: confirm change, steroids, 48 hours, stop (flow) ", "topics": ["ANT-02"]}, {"id": "FD-LND-05", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-05", "title": "GBS prophylaxis: who gets it, which drug (flow)", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-05_GBS_IAP_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-05_GBS_IAP_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "0da4dc4ca61f", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "GBS prophylaxis: who gets it, which drug (flow) ", "topics": ["LND-05"]}, {"id": "FD-LND-02", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-02", "title": "Labor progress: is this really arrest? (flow)", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-02_LABOR_ARREST_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-02_LABOR_ARREST_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "5cd6e672a515", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Labor progress: is this really arrest? (flow) ", "topics": ["LND-02"]}, {"id": "FD-ANT-08", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-08", "title": "NVP and hyperemesis: rule out, climb the ladder (flow)", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-08_NVP_HYPEREMESIS_FLOW_2026-10-25.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-08_NVP_HYPEREMESIS_FLOW_2026-10-25.pdf", "sha": "fdaf2d2c56bc", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "NVP and hyperemesis: rule out, climb the ladder (flow) ", "topics": ["ANT-08"]}, {"id": "FD-SYS-04", "kind": "diagram", "card": "SYS-04", "title": "Maternal cardiac arrest: the 4-minute clock", "img": "assets/diagrams/SYS-04_MATERNAL_ARREST_FLOW_2026-10-31.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/SYS-04_MATERNAL_ARREST_FLOW_2026-10-31.pdf", "sha": "09673ee59d48", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Maternal cardiac arrest: the 4-minute clock ", "topics": ["SYS-04"]}, {"id": "FD-ANT-11", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-11", "title": "Eclamptic seizure: protect, magnesium, stabilize, then deliver", "img": "assets/diagrams/ANT-11_ECLAMPSIA_FLOW_2026-10-31.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/ANT-11_ECLAMPSIA_FLOW_2026-10-31.pdf", "sha": "10d285c347a7", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Eclamptic seizure: protect, magnesium, stabilize, then deliver ", "topics": ["ANT-11"]}, {"id": "FD-SYS-05", "kind": "diagram", "card": "SYS-05", "title": "Amniotic fluid embolism: resuscitate, support the RV, treat the DIC", "img": "assets/diagrams/SYS-05_AFE_FLOW_2026-10-31.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/SYS-05_AFE_FLOW_2026-10-31.pdf", "sha": "ef292f086c94", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Amniotic fluid embolism: resuscitate, support the RV, treat the DIC ", "topics": ["SYS-05"]}, {"id": "FD-LND-09", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-09", "title": "Umbilical cord prolapse: hand up, hold it, deliver fast", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-09_CORD_PROLAPSE_FLOW_2026-10-31.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-09_CORD_PROLAPSE_FLOW_2026-10-31.pdf", "sha": "f34090e45071", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Umbilical cord prolapse: hand up, hold it, deliver fast ", "topics": ["LND-09"]}, {"id": "FD-LND-10", "kind": "diagram", "card": "LND-10", "title": "Uterine rupture and TOLAC emergency: read the tracing, cut now", "img": "assets/diagrams/LND-10_UTERINE_RUPTURE_FLOW_2026-10-31.png", "source": "/workspace/grok-obgyn/cards/flow-diagrams/LND-10_UTERINE_RUPTURE_FLOW_2026-10-31.pdf", "sha": "213e5da77f7a", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "text": "Uterine rupture and TOLAC emergency: read the tracing, cut now ", "topics": ["LND-10"]}, {"id": "FD-ANT-05", "kind": "diagram", "card": "ANT-05", "title": "Diabetes in labor: insulin and IV fluids", "img": "assets/diagrams/DM_INTRAPARTUM_FLOW_2026-11-11.png", "source": "/workspace/director/drafts/ALG-DM-INTRAPARTUM_spec_2026-11-11.md", "sha": "3f2e4fde56de", "flags": [], "book": "OB", "html": "<p><strong>STEP 0.</strong> Diabetes (GDM A1/A2, type 2, type 1) admitted for labor, induction or scheduled cesarean. Unsure of type AND on home insulin, insulin pump, or prior DKA: treat as type 1 for basal (never a basal gap) and get senior/endocrine review now. Not on any insulin and no DKA history: diet/oral rule (no drip).</p>\n<p><strong>STEP 1: ON ADMISSION (CSM).</strong> Stop all insulin, including SC insulin pumps (unless a signed CSM exception), and oral agents. NPO except 8 oz ice chips every 4 hours. POC glucose; BMP and urine ketones if FSBG &gt;160. Type 1: start the IV floor before stopping SC/pump, or keep the glargine/detemir already given; never a gap. Night-before/morning home doses and steroid adjustment: per the CSM provider/pharmacy order set (this app gives no home doses).</p>\n<p><strong>DRIP SET-UP.</strong> Regular insulin (Myxredlin) 100 units/100 mL, so units/h = mL/h; range 0.5–5 units/h. PE-lined tubing: fill with insulin, wait 20 minutes, then flush. Smart pump, dedicated line, independent double-check. High-alert medication. The CSM protocol runs on a provider order.</p>\n<p><strong>MONITORING.</strong></p><ul><li>FSBG before starting IV fluids.</li><li>Insulin drip running: FSBG every 1 hour, regardless of dilation (Michael override of CSM q2h latent; ACOG PB 201).</li><li>Insulin-requiring, drip not yet running: every 2 hours in latent labor or NPO before delivery; every 1 hour at 4–6 cm or with regular contractions (CSM).</li><li>Diet-controlled GDM or oral agents only (no drip): every 4 hours until active labor, then every 2 hours; recheck 1 hour after any sliding-scale dose (Michael decision 11 Nov 2026). Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG).</li><li>After any hypoglycemia rescue: recheck in 15 minutes (go-live resolution 2).</li><li>Hospital point-of-care glucose for insulin decisions; CGM for trends/alarms only.</li></ul>\n<p><strong>STEP 2: FSBG NOW — WHICH ROW?</strong> CSM nomogram; maintenance fluid 125 mL/h. FSBG is read as a whole number in mg/dL. If a value has a decimal, round DOWN (60.5 → 60). 60 is the ≤60 row; 90 holds; 91 starts 0.5 units/h; 120 stays on D5LR; 121 switches to LR. Meter \"LO\": rescue now. Meter \"HI\": call provider now, DKA screen, lab glucose (drip group: &gt;250 row; no-drip group: no drip unless ordered). No bolus. Maximum 5 units/h. Never use this chart to treat DKA.</p>\n<p><strong>BELOW 70.</strong> Any FSBG below 70, or symptoms at any value: hypoglycemia rescue now (rescue box). 61–90 means \"no insulin increase,\" not \"no action.\"</p>\n<table class=\"dm-table\"><thead><tr><th>FSBG (mg/dL)</th><th>GDM A2 / type 2 on insulin</th><th>Type 1: insulin</th><th>Fluid</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>≤60 (CSM \"&lt;60\" row; 60 included)</td><td>No insulin. Contact provider. Hypoglycemia rescue now; recheck 15 min.</td><td>Emergency pause (dextrose continues); resume 0.5 units/h once &gt;70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check).</td><td>D5LR</td></tr><tr><td>61–90 (insulin hold band; 61–69 = LOW, rescue)</td><td>Hold insulin (no added insulin). 61–69: hypoglycemia rescue now, recheck in 15 min, notify provider.</td><td>0.5 units/h floor (provider may set 0.5–1) + notify provider. 61–69: keep the floor AND give the hypoglycemia rescue; recheck in 15 min. Clinician override of the CSM chart (CSM: hold).</td><td>D5LR</td></tr><tr><td>91–120</td><td>0.5 units/h</td><td>0.5 units/h</td><td>D5LR</td></tr><tr><td>121–150</td><td>1 units/h</td><td>1 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal)</td><td>LR</td></tr><tr><td>151–180</td><td>2 units/h</td><td>2 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal)</td><td>LR</td></tr><tr><td>181–220</td><td>3 units/h</td><td>3 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal)</td><td>LR</td></tr><tr><td>221–250</td><td>4 units/h</td><td>4 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal)</td><td>LR</td></tr><tr><td>&gt;250</td><td>5 units/h (maximum; notify provider)</td><td>5 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal)</td><td>LR</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<p><strong>DIET-CONTROLLED GDM OR ORAL AGENTS: NO DRIP.</strong> Diet-controlled GDM (A1) and anyone on oral agents ONLY (no home insulin): NO insulin drip. Any home insulin (with or without metformin/glyburide): drip group. Sliding scale insulin as needed, per provider order (no CSM scale in this app; no doses shown). FSBG every 4 h until active labor, then every 2 h; recheck 1 h after any sliding-scale dose. Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG). Call provider now for any single FSBG &gt;180, any ketones, or vomiting/illness. The provider decides next steps: DKA screen (beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, BMP) and the CSM insulin drip only by provider order. If the drip is ordered, move to the drip group (hourly FSBG, CSM nomogram). Do not start the drip from this chart without an order. Low (&lt;70): rescue box. Glyburide within the last 24 h: FSBG every 2 h until 24 h after the last dose. After any sliding-scale dose: recheck FSBG in 1 h, then every 2 h until 2 values in a row are 70–180 (or per provider), then back to the routine schedule. The drip is for insulin-requiring patients: A2 on insulin, type 2 on insulin, type 1 (Michael decision 11 Nov 2026).</p>\n<p><strong>TYPE 1: NEVER ZERO BASAL.</strong> At 70–90 the floor is 0.5 units/h with D5LR 125 mL/h and provider notification; the provider may set 0.5–1 units/h by trend (prevents ketosis; not glucose correction). Clinician override of the CSM chart (CSM: hold), approved by Dr. Carozza 11 Nov 2026. 61–69: keep the floor AND give the hypoglycemia rescue; recheck in 15 min. The IV floor is not needed only if effective long-acting SC basal (glargine/detemir given within its duration) is on board, or a pump is continued under a signed CSM exception. Otherwise the pump stops only after the IV floor is running. Do not stack. Type 1 insulin stops only for FSBG ≤60 or symptoms, as an emergency pause: dextrose continues, rescue, recheck in 15 minutes, resume 0.5 units/h once &gt;70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check); a pause of about 1 hour or longer needs provider review.</p>\n<p><strong>TYPE 1 ON PLAIN LR (≥121): KETONES.</strong> Type 1 on plain LR: urine ketones (or beta-hydroxybutyrate) at the start of LR and every 4 h on LR (q4h = Doc proposal, not CSM), and now for nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fast breathing or 5 units/h with rising values. Any positive ketones: call provider; go to the DKA box; no ad hoc dextrose.</p>\n<p><strong>HYPOGLYCEMIA &lt;70 OR SYMPTOMS: RESCUE NOW (CSM Adult Hypoglycemic Protocol).</strong></p><ul><li><strong>D50 50 mL IV:</strong> FSBG &lt;50 with IV access: alert and NPO or unable to swallow (NPO = unable to swallow), OR questionable consciousness (CSM; pharmacy).</li><li><strong>D50 25 mL IV push:</strong> FSBG 50–69 with IV access: alert and NPO or unable to swallow, OR questionable consciousness (CSM; pharmacy).</li><li><strong>Glutose 40% gel 30 g / 15 g buccal:</strong> ALERT, no IV, can swallow safely: 30 g if &lt;50; 15 g if 50–59 (CSM). 15 g also at 60–69 (Michael-approved addition; pending CSM pharmacy build).</li><li><strong>Glucagon (GlucaGen) 1 mg IM, ONCE:</strong> Only if ALERT AND no IV AND cannot swallow, FSBG &lt;70; OR questionable consciousness, no IV, FSBG &lt;60 (CSM). Also questionable consciousness, no IV, 60–69 (Michael-approved addition; pending CSM pharmacy build).</li><li><strong>No IV in any rescue:</strong> Get IV access now (urgent provider / rapid-response call if consciousness is impaired).</li><li>Recheck FSBG in 15 minutes. Still &lt;70: repeat D50 or gel per the matching row. Glucagon is ONCE only: if still &lt;70 15 minutes after glucagon, get IV access for D50 and call the provider / rapid response. Notify the provider after every rescue.</li><li>Below 54 or any symptoms: rescue and call the provider immediately.</li><li>Symptoms but FSBG ≥70: repeat POC, send lab glucose, call provider. If you cannot check, treat as low.</li><li>D10 is not in the CSM protocol: not used here. D50 is a vesicant: patent vein, then flush. D5LR continues during rescue.</li><li>GDM/type 2: hold IV insulin during rescue and restart per the nomogram row. Type 1: resume 0.5 units/h once &gt;70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check).</li></ul>\n<p><strong>SICK, KETONES, VOMITING? → DKA.</strong> Pregnancy DKA can occur below 200 mg/dL; there is no glucose gate for ketone testing when ill. Check beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, electrolytes/K+, creatinine and anion gap; maternal assessment and fetal monitoring. Use the CSM DKA order set, not this chart. Reference only, per CSM DKA order set: insulin 0.1 units/kg/h once K+ is safe. Type 1 on plain LR with positive ketones comes here; no ad hoc dextrose. Delivery is not DKA treatment.</p>\n<p><strong>A · AFTER DELIVERY (CSM).</strong> Notify the provider when delivery is complete to start the CSM Postpartum Diabetes Management Order Set (doses there). GDM: stop insulin at placenta; 75-g 2-hour OGTT at 4–12 weeks postpartum. Type 1: basal always covered. Continue IV insulin 1–2 hours after the first SC basal dose; then stop D5LR and insulin together.</p>\n<p><strong>B · NEWBORN.</strong> Tell the nursery the diabetes type, treatment, last maternal glucose and steroids. Stable baby: feed within 1 hour of birth; the nursery team owns the neonatal glucose protocol.</p>\n<p><strong>C · STEROIDS / TIMING.</strong> Steroids raise glucose up to 5 days: more FSBG checks; follow the nomogram. SMFM #58: no late-preterm (34+0–36+6) steroids in pregestational diabetes. Timing and EFW ≥4,500 g counseling: Diabetes in pregnancy card.</p>\n<p><strong>D · NOTIFY.</strong> RN to OBHG hospitalist: any FSBG &lt;70 or any rescue; 61–90 on a type 1 floor; 5 units/h with rising values; ketones; any gap in type 1 basal; diet/oral group: &gt;180, ketones or illness.</p>\n<p class=\"verify\">Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build. Decision support only, not orders or unit policy.</p>", "text": "Diabetes in labor: insulin and IV fluids  STEP 0. Diabetes (GDM A1/A2, type 2, type 1) admitted for labor, induction or scheduled cesarean. Unsure of type AND on home insulin, insulin pump, or prior DKA: treat as type 1 for basal (never a basal gap) and get senior/endocrine review now. Not on any insulin and no DKA history: diet/oral rule (no drip). STEP 1: ON ADMISSION (CSM). Stop all insulin, including SC insulin pumps (unless a signed CSM exception), and oral agents. NPO except 8 oz ice chips every 4 hours. POC glucose; BMP and urine ketones if FSBG >160. Type 1: start the IV floor before stopping SC/pump, or keep the glargine/detemir already given; never a gap. Night-before/morning home doses and steroid adjustment: per the CSM provider/pharmacy order set (this app gives no home doses). DRIP SET-UP. Regular insulin (Myxredlin) 100 units/100 mL, so units/h = mL/h; range 0.5–5 units/h. PE-lined tubing: fill with insulin, wait 20 minutes, then flush. Smart pump, dedicated line, independent double-check. High-alert medication. The CSM protocol runs on a provider order. MONITORING. FSBG before starting IV fluids. Insulin drip running: FSBG every 1 hour, regardless of dilation (Michael override of CSM q2h latent; ACOG PB 201). Insulin-requiring, drip not yet running: every 2 hours in latent labor or NPO before delivery; every 1 hour at 4–6 cm or with regular contractions (CSM). Diet-controlled GDM or oral agents only (no drip): every 4 hours until active labor, then every 2 hours; recheck 1 hour after any sliding-scale dose (Michael decision 11 Nov 2026). Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG). After any hypoglycemia rescue: recheck in 15 minutes (go-live resolution 2). Hospital point-of-care glucose for insulin decisions; CGM for trends/alarms only. STEP 2: FSBG NOW — WHICH ROW? CSM nomogram; maintenance fluid 125 mL/h. FSBG is read as a whole number in mg/dL. If a value has a decimal, round DOWN (60.5 → 60). 60 is the ≤60 row; 90 holds; 91 starts 0.5 units/h; 120 stays on D5LR; 121 switches to LR. Meter \"LO\": rescue now. Meter \"HI\": call provider now, DKA screen, lab glucose (drip group: >250 row; no-drip group: no drip unless ordered). No bolus. Maximum 5 units/h. Never use this chart to treat DKA. BELOW 70. Any FSBG below 70, or symptoms at any value: hypoglycemia rescue now (rescue box). 61–90 means \"no insulin increase,\" not \"no action.\" FSBG (mg/dL) GDM A2 / type 2 on insulin Type 1: insulin Fluid ≤60 (CSM \"<60\" row; 60 included) No insulin. Contact provider. Hypoglycemia rescue now; recheck 15 min. Emergency pause (dextrose continues); resume 0.5 units/h once >70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check). D5LR 61–90 (insulin hold band; 61–69 = LOW, rescue) Hold insulin (no added insulin). 61–69: hypoglycemia rescue now, recheck in 15 min, notify provider. 0.5 units/h floor (provider may set 0.5–1) + notify provider. 61–69: keep the floor AND give the hypoglycemia rescue; recheck in 15 min. Clinician override of the CSM chart (CSM: hold). D5LR 91–120 0.5 units/h 0.5 units/h D5LR 121–150 1 units/h 1 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal) LR 151–180 2 units/h 2 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal) LR 181–220 3 units/h 3 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal) LR 221–250 4 units/h 4 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal) LR >250 5 units/h (maximum; notify provider) 5 units/h; ketones at LR start, then q4h (Doc proposal) LR DIET-CONTROLLED GDM OR ORAL AGENTS: NO DRIP. Diet-controlled GDM (A1) and anyone on oral agents ONLY (no home insulin): NO insulin drip. Any home insulin (with or without metformin/glyburide): drip group. Sliding scale insulin as needed, per provider order (no CSM scale in this app; no doses shown). FSBG every 4 h until active labor, then every 2 h; recheck 1 h after any sliding-scale dose. Active labor = 6 cm or more (ACOG). Call provider now for any single FSBG >180, any ketones, or vomiting/illness. The provider decides next steps: DKA screen (beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, BMP) and the CSM insulin drip only by provider order. If the drip is ordered, move to the drip group (hourly FSBG, CSM nomogram). Do not start the drip from this chart without an order. Low (<70): rescue box. Glyburide within the last 24 h: FSBG every 2 h until 24 h after the last dose. After any sliding-scale dose: recheck FSBG in 1 h, then every 2 h until 2 values in a row are 70–180 (or per provider), then back to the routine schedule. The drip is for insulin-requiring patients: A2 on insulin, type 2 on insulin, type 1 (Michael decision 11 Nov 2026). TYPE 1: NEVER ZERO BASAL. At 70–90 the floor is 0.5 units/h with D5LR 125 mL/h and provider notification; the provider may set 0.5–1 units/h by trend (prevents ketosis; not glucose correction). Clinician override of the CSM chart (CSM: hold), approved by Dr. Carozza 11 Nov 2026. 61–69: keep the floor AND give the hypoglycemia rescue; recheck in 15 min. The IV floor is not needed only if effective long-acting SC basal (glargine/detemir given within its duration) is on board, or a pump is continued under a signed CSM exception. Otherwise the pump stops only after the IV floor is running. Do not stack. Type 1 insulin stops only for FSBG ≤60 or symptoms, as an emergency pause: dextrose continues, rescue, recheck in 15 minutes, resume 0.5 units/h once >70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check); a pause of about 1 hour or longer needs provider review. TYPE 1 ON PLAIN LR (≥121): KETONES. Type 1 on plain LR: urine ketones (or beta-hydroxybutyrate) at the start of LR and every 4 h on LR (q4h = Doc proposal, not CSM), and now for nausea, vomiting, abdominal pain, fast breathing or 5 units/h with rising values. Any positive ketones: call provider; go to the DKA box; no ad hoc dextrose. HYPOGLYCEMIA <70 OR SYMPTOMS: RESCUE NOW (CSM Adult Hypoglycemic Protocol). D50 50 mL IV: FSBG <50 with IV access: alert and NPO or unable to swallow (NPO = unable to swallow), OR questionable consciousness (CSM; pharmacy). D50 25 mL IV push: FSBG 50–69 with IV access: alert and NPO or unable to swallow, OR questionable consciousness (CSM; pharmacy). Glutose 40% gel 30 g / 15 g buccal: ALERT, no IV, can swallow safely: 30 g if <50; 15 g if 50–59 (CSM). 15 g also at 60–69 (Michael-approved addition; pending CSM pharmacy build). Glucagon (GlucaGen) 1 mg IM, ONCE: Only if ALERT AND no IV AND cannot swallow, FSBG <70; OR questionable consciousness, no IV, FSBG <60 (CSM). Also questionable consciousness, no IV, 60–69 (Michael-approved addition; pending CSM pharmacy build). No IV in any rescue: Get IV access now (urgent provider / rapid-response call if consciousness is impaired). Recheck FSBG in 15 minutes. Still <70: repeat D50 or gel per the matching row. Glucagon is ONCE only: if still <70 15 minutes after glucagon, get IV access for D50 and call the provider / rapid response. Notify the provider after every rescue. Below 54 or any symptoms: rescue and call the provider immediately. Symptoms but FSBG ≥70: repeat POC, send lab glucose, call provider. If you cannot check, treat as low. D10 is not in the CSM protocol: not used here. D50 is a vesicant: patent vein, then flush. D5LR continues during rescue. GDM/type 2: hold IV insulin during rescue and restart per the nomogram row. Type 1: resume 0.5 units/h once >70 and rising (rising = higher than the previous check). SICK, KETONES, VOMITING? → DKA. Pregnancy DKA can occur below 200 mg/dL; there is no glucose gate for ketone testing when ill. Check beta-hydroxybutyrate, VBG, electrolytes/K+, creatinine and anion gap; maternal assessment and fetal monitoring. Use the CSM DKA order set, not this chart. Reference only, per CSM DKA order set: insulin 0.1 units/kg/h once K+ is safe. Type 1 on plain LR with positive ketones comes here; no ad hoc dextrose. Delivery is not DKA treatment. A · AFTER DELIVERY (CSM). Notify the provider when delivery is complete to start the CSM Postpartum Diabetes Management Order Set (doses there). GDM: stop insulin at placenta; 75-g 2-hour OGTT at 4–12 weeks postpartum. Type 1: basal always covered. Continue IV insulin 1–2 hours after the first SC basal dose; then stop D5LR and insulin together. B · NEWBORN. Tell the nursery the diabetes type, treatment, last maternal glucose and steroids. Stable baby: feed within 1 hour of birth; the nursery team owns the neonatal glucose protocol. C · STEROIDS / TIMING. Steroids raise glucose up to 5 days: more FSBG checks; follow the nomogram. SMFM #58: no late-preterm (34+0–36+6) steroids in pregestational diabetes. Timing and EFW ≥4,500 g counseling: Diabetes in pregnancy card. D · NOTIFY. RN to OBHG hospitalist: any FSBG <70 or any rescue; 61–90 on a type 1 floor; 5 units/h with rising values; ketones; any gap in type 1 basal; diet/oral group: >180, ketones or illness. Doses to be checked against CSM pharmacy build. Decision support only, not orders or unit policy. ", "topics": ["ANT-05"], "algorithm_id": "ALG-DM-INTRAPARTUM"}]};
